2 Using this manual
*******************

You can read the early chapters in sequence or use this manual to answer
a question while working.  *note Find a task: Finding Tasks. links
everyday goals to their detailed instructions.  *note Terms used in this
manual: Emacsvox Terminology. explains names such as palette, profile,
scope, and draft without requiring you to study the whole settings
system first.

   Start with *note Read the key instructions: Emacs Key Notation. if
Emacs notation is unfamiliar.  It explains which keys to hold together,
when to release them, and how to enter a command by name.  The reading
routes below assume that notation, not prior experience with Emacs.

* Menu:

* Answer a prompt and return to your work: Answering Emacs Prompts.  Know where to type, how completion helps, and when a command has finished.
* Choose a reading route: Manual Reading Routes.  Choose sequential lessons, everyday tasks or a specific reference answer.
* Find an answer in Info and return: Finding Help In Info.  Use the index and task links, retrace a lookup and return to work.
* Recognise the document you are reading: Manual Editions And Formats.  Distinguish current guidance, generated reference, heritage and published formats.


File: emacsvox.info,  Node: Answering Emacs Prompts,  Next: Manual Reading Routes,  Up: Using This Manual

2.1 Answer a prompt and return to your work
===========================================

A command may ask for a file, a buffer, a search term or another value.
Emacs reads that answer in the _minibuffer_, a temporary input area
separate from your document.  While the prompt is active, ordinary
typing answers the prompt; it does not add words to the document.
Emacsvox speaks prompts and completion feedback according to your
settings.

   For example, ‘M-x’ asks for a command name.  Type ‘describe-mode’,
including its hyphen, then press ‘<RET>’ to run it.  Do not type the
prompt label or the letters ‘M-x’.  A command may then ask another
question: after choosing ‘eww’, for example, its next prompt asks for a
web address or search words.  Answer one prompt at a time rather than
pasting the whole printed instruction.

   Completion helps you enter an existing name.  Type a distinctive
beginning, then press ‘<TAB>’.  Emacs may extend the text or offer
several candidates.  With several matches, type more of the name; you
can always type the complete name and press ‘<RET>’.  Completion
interfaces differ between configurations, so the guides do not assume
that Down Arrow accepts the first suggestion.  Opening a new file
permits a name that does not yet exist; selecting an installed command
normally requires a recognised name.

   ‘<RET>’ submits the current answer, including any text already in the
prompt.  An empty answer may accept a displayed default.  Read that
default before accepting it.  For a yes/no question, use the response
requested: some prompts accept ‘y’ or ‘n’, while others require ‘yes’ or
‘no’ followed by ‘<RET>’.

   Use ‘C-g’ to abandon an unfinished ordinary prompt.  If you have
already submitted it and the command has acted, cancellation does not
undo the result.  Likewise, quitting a settings menu does not undo an
adjustment made there.  Follow the recovery instructions for the
operation you actually performed.

   After the prompt finishes, notice which buffer has been selected.  An
open-file command selects a document; Help may appear in another window;
a save normally leaves you in the document.  Press ‘C-e m’ to identify
the current buffer and mode before editing.  Finish or cancel an active
prompt before switching buffers with ‘C-x b’; otherwise its answer is
still unfinished.  See *note Using Online Help: Using Online Help. for
entering and leaving a Help window.

* Menu:

* Interpret filenames and examples: Filenames And Example Commands.  Resolve paths in the right environment and distinguish input from instructions.


File: emacsvox.info,  Node: Filenames And Example Commands,  Up: Answering Emacs Prompts

2.1.1 Interpret filenames and examples
--------------------------------------

A _path_ identifies a file's location as well as its name.  A file
prompt often starts with a directory already filled in.  Append a
filename to use that directory, or replace the editable answer with a
complete path.  In an ordinary file minibuffer, ‘C-a C-k’ removes the
current editable answer: Control-a goes to its beginning and Control-k
removes the text that follows.  This edits the answer, not a file.  Then
type the intended path and press ‘<RET>’.

   Like other kill commands, ‘C-k’ also puts the removed text in the
kill ring.  If you intended to paste a previously copied path, clearing
the prompt this way can change the next paste.  Type the intended path
here, or use *note Choose text copied earlier: (emacsvox)Choosing
Earlier Copied Text. to retrieve the right entry.

   The examples use ‘~/’ for the home directory understood by the
running Emacs.  For example, ‘~/evox-notes.org’ names ‘evox-notes.org’
in that home directory.  In WSL this normally means the Linux home, not
Windows Documents.  A Windows C-drive path is normally reached through
‘/mnt/c/’ in WSL; native Windows Emacs uses its own Windows paths.  Use
a directory that exists and is writable in the environment running
Emacs.  A speech server running on another host does not change where
ordinary Emacs document files are stored.

   A relative name is resolved against the directory in the prompt,
which may change with the selected buffer.  Do not assume it means the
repository root or the folder used in an earlier task.  When an
instruction says "from the checkout", it explicitly means that source
directory.  Replace placeholders such as ‘/path/to/document.txt’ with
real names; do not create those directories merely to copy the example
literally.  In Emacs file prompts, type spaces in names normally rather
than adding shell quotes around the whole path.

   Keyboard sequences invoke Emacs commands.  A shell example belongs at
a shell or terminal prompt in the environment named by the instructions,
not after ‘M-x’.  Lisp configuration belongs in the specified Emacs
configuration file; it is not ordinary document text or a shell command.
A displayed command's output is something to check, not another command
to enter.  Read the sentence introducing each example to establish which
of these contexts it uses.

   When practising, choose unused names.  If a proposed name already
contains work, cancel and substitute another name consistently.  An
overwrite warning is not a routine confirmation to accept.  Opening,
copying, saving and deleting have different effects; the following task
guides explain the effect before the step.


File: emacsvox.info,  Node: Manual Reading Routes,  Next: Finding Help In Info,  Prev: Answering Emacs Prompts,  Up: Using This Manual

2.2 Choose a reading route
==========================

After *note Installation: (emacsvox)Installation. gives you speech, work
through *note Learn the audio desktop: (emacsvox)Guided Tutorial. for
six progressive lessons using a disposable practice document.  It
teaches reading, editing, Help, structure, temporary settings, and voice
previews, with exercises and separate answers.  No additional engine is
needed.

   If Emacs itself is new to you, ‘C-h t’ opens its separate interactive
tutorial.  Use it for more practice with general editing.  *note Basic
Usage: (emacsvox)Basic Usage., *note Using Online Help: (emacsvox)Using
Online Help., and *note Audio Desktop: (emacsvox)Audio Desktop. are
references to return to as you work.

   If you already use Emacs, start with *note Basic Usage:
(emacsvox)Basic Usage. once speech works.  Keep your usual editing and
mode commands.  The tutorial's *note temporary-settings lesson:
(emacsvox)Tutorial Temporary Settings. and *note voice experiments:
(emacsvox)Tutorial Voice Experiments. provide guided practice without
making its earlier editing lessons prerequisites for experienced users;
prepare its disposable buffer before experimenting.  Then read *note
Aural Presentation: (emacsvox)Aural Presentation. for application
meaning, voices, sounds, and settings, and choose an application guide
for your work.

   For independent everyday tasks, use *note Work with files and
directories: Working With Files., *note Write a note with headings:
Writing An Org Document., or *note Web browsing with EWW: Web Browsing.
These guides explain real files and pages; they do not require
completing the tutorial or changing your voice configuration.

   For one question, use *note Find a task: Finding Tasks., Info's index
or a text search.  For a familiar action whose key you cannot remember,
use *note Everyday commands and key prefixes: (emacsvox)Emacsvox
Keyboard Commands.  When something is not working, *note Something is
wrong: Finding Problem Tasks. starts with symptoms rather than the names
of settings.  *note Speech Backends: (emacsvox)Speech Backends. contains
alternative installation and backend configuration procedures; it is not
required reading between your first speaking session and everyday
editing.  The final Developer Guide is for changing and maintaining
Emacsvox, not for ordinary use.


File: emacsvox.info,  Node: Finding Help In Info,  Next: Manual Editions And Formats,  Prev: Manual Reading Routes,  Up: Using This Manual

2.3 Find an answer in Info and return
=====================================

Info is Emacs's manual reader.  A _node_ is one navigable topic, which
can have its own menu of subtopics.  These instructions apply to the
Info buffer, not to Emacsvox's settings menus.

   Try this lookup without changing your configuration:

  1. From a working buffer, press ‘C-h i’ to open Info, ‘d’ for its
     directory, then ‘m’.  Enter ‘Emacsvox’ and press ‘<RET>’ to open
     this manual.
  2. Press ‘i’, enter ‘ordinary reading voice’, and press ‘<RET>’.  The
     index takes you to the voice-task links.  Read with your ordinary
     speech commands.
  3. Use ‘<TAB>’ to reach _Change my ordinary reading voice_ and press
     ‘<RET>’.  You are now at the named-voice instructions.  Reading
     them changes nothing; leave the editing commands for when you
     deliberately want to make a change.
  4. Press lowercase ‘l’ to return to the task links.  Another ‘l’
     returns to the topic you were reading before the index lookup.
     This follows your visiting history rather than the chapter order.
  5. Press ‘q’ to leave Info.  From an Aural interface's contextual
     manual link, ‘q’ returns to that interface.  If you opened other
     windows or switched buffers during the exercise, ‘C-x b’ also lets
     you select your original working buffer by name.

   If the directory has no Emacsvox entry, the manual is not yet
discoverable by that Emacs session; follow *note Installation:
(emacsvox)Installation. rather than guessing a file path.  In an Aural
editor, ‘C-c C-i’ opens its relevant manual topic, and ‘i’ in that
editor's Help opens the same topic.

   The following Info commands are useful during later lookups:

‘i’
     Look up a subject in the manual's indexes.  Use ordinary words, a
     command name, or a key sequence.  ‘,’ visits further matching
     entries.
‘s’
     Search the manual text, including other nodes.  Enter the words and
     press ‘<RET>’; ‘s <RET>’ continues the same search.
‘TAB, S-TAB’
     Move among links.  ‘<RET>’ follows the selected link.
‘m’
     Choose a subtopic from the current node's menu by name, with
     completion.
‘u’
     Go to the containing topic.  This is different from ‘l’, which goes
     back to the topic you last visited.
‘g’
     Go to a node by its identifier.  For example, enter ‘Finding Tasks’
     to return directly to the task finder.

   For the full reader tutorial and search behaviour, see the *note Info
manual: (info)Top.  If a key behaves differently in your session, ‘C-h
k’ followed by that key explains its effective binding without invoking
it.


File: emacsvox.info,  Node: Manual Editions And Formats,  Prev: Finding Help In Info,  Up: Using This Manual

2.4 Recognise the document you are reading
==========================================

This user manual explains tasks, feedback, and recovery.  The generated
_Emacsvox Reference Manual_ lists commands, options, and keymaps from
source.  The heritage manual preserves historical Emacspeak material; it
is not a current setup guide.  A linked application's own manual remains
authoritative for that application's general operations.

   Some stable Info identifiers predate their displayed headings.  For
example, ‘Aural Home’ now identifies _Getting started with settings
menus_.  This keeps old links and bookmarks valid; it does not make the
retired Home menu entry part of the learning route.

   In HTML, use the contents, headings, and task links, then your
browser's Back command to return from a link.  The Info keys above are
not browser commands.  The published website may describe a different
revision from your installed manual.  Use the manual matching your
Emacsvox version, and check live Help when a key differs from the
documented default.


File: emacsvox.info,  Node: Installation,  Next: Guided Tutorial,  Prev: Using This Manual,  Up: Top

3 Getting Started: Installation and First Speech
************************************************

The aim of installation is a clean speaking session that you can test
before changing your personal Emacs setup.  Choose the route for the
machine running Emacs.  A Debian package is suitable when your
distribution provides the required Emacs and Transient.  The source
checkout supports GNU/Linux, macOS, and WSL2.  Native Windows uses its
own *note Windows installation procedure: Trying Native Windows
Emacsvox.  If speech will play on another workstation, use *note
Installing Remote Emacsvox: Installing Remote Emacsvox. instead of
installing a local speech server.

   The settings-menu instructions in this development manual describe
‘ui-experiments’.  Use a checkout of that branch to follow them; an
older release package may not contain the same interface.  Keep an
existing working installation available while testing a development
checkout.

   Commands shown before Emacsvox starts are entered in a terminal.
Keys written with ‘C-’ or ‘M-’ are entered inside Emacs.  The published
installation chapter is also available before installation at
<https://bartbunting.github.io/emacsvox/Installation.html>; its
published version may differ from this development branch.

* Menu:

* Before You Begin: Obtaining Emacsvox.  Prepare the required tools and Emacsvox checkout.
* Installing On A Remote Linux Host: Installing Remote Emacsvox.  Prepare Emacsvox without installing local speech engines.
* Updating An Existing WSL2 Installation: Updating Emacsvox Under WSL2.  Update the source checkout and rerun the guided installer.
* Install And Check Omnivox: Building And Testing The Speech Server.  Install the recommended server and hear its check.
* Quick Installation::           Build Emacsvox and reach an isolated speaking session.
* Confirming First Speech::      Verify the live adapter and basic feedback.
* Recovering From No Speech::    Diagnose the boundary at which speech failed.
* Persistent Startup::           Add Emacsvox to personal configuration safely.

3.1 Debian and Ubuntu package
=============================

Emacsvox 2026.9.2 predates Debian packaging.  Use a package from a
release that includes one, or a locally built development package.
Omnivox 1.17.0 includes an amd64 deb for supported Ubuntu systems.

   With a downloaded Emacsvox deb, install it using apt:

     sudo apt install ./emacsvox_VERSION_all.deb
     emacsvox --diagnose
     emacsvox --check
     emacsvox

   Replace ‘VERSION’ with the version in the downloaded filename.  Apt
requires a packaged Emacs 30.2 or newer; an installed terminal-only
‘emacs-nox’ also satisfies this dependency.  Settings menus also require
Transient 0.13.3 or newer.  Apt installs ‘elpa-transient’ when a
packaged Emacs 31.1 or newer does not already supply it.  Ubuntu 26.10
supplies both dependencies.  Ubuntu 26.04's stock Transient is too old;
use the source installation route below and upgrade Transient through
Emacs's package manager.  The package never builds GNU Emacs or adds an
apt repository to resolve missing dependencies.  If your distribution
also lacks a supported Emacs, the source route offers an explicit Emacs
31 build.

   Use Omnivox 1.17.0 or newer for the Omnivox route documented here.
If it is unavailable from your apt repositories, include its downloaded
deb in the same apt command, or select an existing installation.
Confirm both tone and speech from ‘emacsvox --check’ before starting the
session.

   For remote Emacs or WSL with an existing Windows speech server, use
‘sudo apt install --no-install-recommends ./emacsvox_VERSION_all.deb’.
Remote sessions start with ‘emacsvox --remote’ after the workstation
service, token and tunnel are prepared as described in *note Installing
Remote Emacsvox: Installing Remote Emacsvox.  The package does not
acquire a Windows speech runtime; the guided WSL source installer below
handles that.

   Emacsvox installs under ‘/usr/share/emacsvox’, with the command
‘/usr/bin/emacsvox’.  It includes Lisp sources, sounds and offline
manuals.  No personal initialisation is changed and ordinary Emacs
sessions do not automatically start speaking.  To enable Emacsvox in
your own Emacs setup, add ‘(load-file
"/usr/share/emacsvox/lisp/emacsvox-setup.el")’ explicitly.  Upgrade the
system tree using apt; the source build instructions below apply to
checkouts.  Removing the package preserves personal settings and speech
tokens.  Package details are in ‘/usr/share/doc/emacsvox/README.Debian’.


File: emacsvox.info,  Node: Obtaining Emacsvox,  Next: Installing Remote Emacsvox,  Up: Installation

3.2 Before You Begin
====================

Install ‘git’ to obtain the checkout.  Emacsvox requires Emacs 30.2 or
newer (https://www.gnu.org/software/emacs/download.html).  WSL2 users
can let the guided installer inspect an existing Emacs, suggest a
suitable package, or offer an explicit source build.  It does not begin
an Emacs source build by default.

   On GNU/Linux and macOS, install Emacs 30.2 or newer and ‘make’.
Omnivox 1.17.0 or newer is the supported speech-server baseline.  On
macOS, use Homebrew (https://brew.sh) for Omnivox as described in *note
Installing An Omnivox Release Binary: Installing An Omnivox Release
Binary.; Emacs and Emacsvox are installed separately.  Manual
release-archive installation requires ‘curl’, ‘tar’, and ‘sha256sum’ on
GNU/Linux or ‘shasum’ on macOS.  GNU Texinfo is needed for building or
editing the documentation, not for an ordinary installation.

   Ubuntu 26.04 provides Emacs 30.2 as a distribution package.  The same
Emacs minimum applies to native Windows.  On Emacs 30, install
‘lua-mode’ separately if you use Lua; it is built into Emacs 31.  Corfu
on an Emacs 30 text terminal also needs ‘corfu-terminal’; Emacs 31
supports its terminal popup directly.

   Check the Emacs found by your shell:

     emacs --version

   The reported version must be 30.2 or newer.  If the command is
missing or reports an older version, install a supported Emacs or locate
its executable.  You can check a specific executable in the same way:

     /path/to/emacs-31/bin/emacs --version

3.2.1 Prepare the settings-menu dependency
------------------------------------------

Emacsvox requires Transient 0.13.3 or newer.  Emacs 31 supplies a
supported version; Emacs 30.2's bundled 0.7.2.2 needs an upgrade before
building Emacsvox.  Use your selected Emacs and its package manager,
with GNU ELPA enabled:

  1. Run ‘M-x set-variable <RET> package-install-upgrade-built-in <RET>
     t <RET>’.  Answer the variable-name and value prompts separately.
     This permits package dependencies to replace older built-in copies
     in this Emacs session; it does not save a new startup preference.
     See *note Examining and setting variables: (emacs)Examining. in the
     GNU Emacs Manual for this command.
  2. Run ‘M-x package-refresh-contents <RET>’, then ‘M-x package-upgrade
     <RET> transient <RET>’.  Accept the package's dependencies.
  3. Start a fresh Emacs before building or trying the menus.  A running
     session can retain the older loaded version after installation.

   An isolated Emacsvox session activates an installed Transient and its
required packages when its bundled version is too old.  It does not load
your init file, activate unrelated packages, or download dependencies.
Install into the package directory used by the same Emacs profile; a
custom package directory must be on that session's load path.  Debian
installations also discover the system ELPA directory without site
startup.

   If a menu reports an unsupported version, inspect the version and
loaded path in its error.  Upgrade that installation and restart Emacs;
restarting only speech does not replace loaded Lisp.  The Emacs minimum
remains 30.2.

3.2.2 Obtain the development checkout
-------------------------------------

Run the clone from a terminal on GNU/Linux, macOS, or WSL.  The
following location works for an ordinary per-user installation and
selects the branch described by this manual:

     mkdir -p ~/src
     cd ~/src
     git clone --branch ui-experiments https://github.com/bartbunting/emacsvox
     cd emacsvox

   For an existing checkout, inspect ‘git status --short’ and ‘git
branch --show-current’ before changing branches or updating.  Do not
replace or discard local work to make the checkout match an example.

   The checkout is the installation directory.  It builds in place and
does not create a second tree to copy elsewhere.  Under WSL, keep it on
the Linux filesystem, not below a Windows-mounted path such as ‘/mnt/c’;
Windows filesystem semantics can interfere with Unix executable
permissions and build tools.  Clone Omnivox beside Emacsvox only for an
Omnivox source build or the advanced reproducible WSL2 build.

3.2.3 Use the guided WSL2 installer
-----------------------------------

For a local WSL2 desktop, first inspect the machine without changing it:

     cd ~/src/emacsvox
     ./bin/emacsvox-wsl-install --check

   If Emacs is missing, the report checks the available apt version and
suggests ‘sudo apt install emacs’ only when that candidate satisfies
30.2.  Older or unavailable candidates are identified without being
recommended.  Refresh apt metadata and check again, or choose the pinned
source build with ‘./bin/emacsvox-wsl-install --build-emacs’.  Add
‘--check’ to inspect that build's prerequisites first.

   With Emacs selected, the report names it, the matching Omnivox
Windows archive, and the per-user installation paths.  If source-build
packages are missing, it prints one ‘apt’ command for you to review and
run.  The installer never invokes ‘sudo’ itself.  After resolving
reported prerequisites, run:

     ./bin/emacsvox-wsl-install

   If you chose ‘--build-emacs’, include that option in the installation
command.  It prepares the pinned stable Emacs for your user.  The
installer then downloads and verifies the matching Omnivox 1.17.0
Windows archive, records its path without editing a shell startup file,
compiles Emacsvox, runs a diagnostic, and plays the final speech-server
check.  Later runs reuse the Emacs build and verified downloads.

   Routine compiler output is kept below
‘$XDG_CACHE_HOME/emacsvox/logs’, normally ‘~/.cache/emacsvox/logs’.  If
a build fails, the installer shows its final log lines and the complete
log path.  Continue only after hearing both the final tone and speech.
You can then go to the final step in *note Quick Installation: Quick
Installation. and start Emacsvox.  Optional engines can wait until first
speech is working.


File: emacsvox.info,  Node: Installing Remote Emacsvox,  Next: Updating Emacsvox Under WSL2,  Prev: Obtaining Emacsvox,  Up: Installation

3.3 Installing On A Remote Linux Host
=====================================

Use this route when Emacs runs on a Linux host and speech plays on your
workstation through an SSH reverse forward.  It also works for
remote-only Emacs under WSL, without Windows interoperability.  Remote
access is a preview feature.  The workstation needs Omnivox 1.17.0 or
newer, including ‘--serve’.

   After cloning Emacsvox on the Emacs host, run:

     cd ~/src/emacsvox
     ./bin/emacsvox-install --role remote-emacs --check
     ./bin/emacsvox-install --role remote-emacs

   The first command reports prerequisites and paths without changing
files.  The second reuses Emacs 30.2 or newer and compiles Emacsvox.  If
Emacs is missing, it checks the local apt candidate and suggests ‘sudo
apt install emacs-nox’ when that version is suitable.  To choose the
source build instead, run ‘./bin/emacsvox-install --role remote-emacs
--build-emacs’.  Add ‘--check’ to inspect build prerequisites first.
The checksum-verified GNU Emacs 31.1 source is built as a terminal-only
installation under ‘~/.local/opt/emacs-31.1-terminal’.  Other Linux
distributions currently need an existing Emacs 30.2 or newer.  A
supported GUI Emacs can also be reused.  Explicit ‘EMACS’ and ‘local.mk’
selections are preserved; if they conflict or select an unsupported
version, resolve the reported setting first.

   No local Omnivox, sound device, GUI build packages, or Windows speech
tools are required.  Missing Debian/Ubuntu build packages are reported
as one ‘apt’ command for you to review and run.  The installer never
invokes ‘sudo’ or changes a shell startup file.  Build logs are kept
below ‘~/.cache/emacsvox/logs’, or the corresponding ‘XDG_CACHE_HOME’
location.

   Installation can finish before the workstation is connected.  Follow
*note Remote Workstation Speech: Remote workstation speech setup. to
prepare its service, private token, and SSH reverse forward.  Then run
on the Emacs host:

     ./bin/emacsvox --remote --diagnose
     ./bin/emacsvox --remote --check
     ./bin/emacsvox --remote

   Diagnosis reports local configuration without connecting or reading
the token's contents.  The check connects both speech lanes, waits for
workstation inventory and voice registration, and speaks a foreground
announcement followed by a notification announcement.  Confirm that both
are audible.  The last command starts an isolated terminal Emacsvox
session.

   The default forwarded endpoint is ‘127.0.0.1:6417’.  The token file
defaults to ‘~/.config/omnivox/remote-token’, respecting
‘XDG_CONFIG_HOME’ when set.  To select another forwarded port or private
token path, supply environment values to the same command:

     EMACSVOX_REMOTE_PORT=6418 \
     EMACSVOX_REMOTE_TOKEN_FILE="$HOME/.config/omnivox/other-token" \
       ./bin/emacsvox --remote --check

   ‘EMACSVOX_REMOTE_HOST’ accepts only ‘127.0.0.1’ or ‘::1’.  The token
path must be absolute; its contents stay in the private file.  Use the
same settings when starting the session.  Remote mode selects the
Omnivox transport and skips local speech executable and playback probes;
leave ‘TTS_PROGRAM’ unset or set it to ‘omnivox’.

   For updates, keep your speaking session open, pull the checkout with
‘git pull --ff-only’, and rerun the remote installer.  It rebuilds
byte-code with the selected Emacs.  Perform the connected check while
the workstation service is free, and test the updated session before
closing the known-good one.  A single service admits only one Emacs
session at a time: use a separate test service or explicitly disconnect
the old remote session before connecting its replacement.


File: emacsvox.info,  Node: Updating Emacsvox Under WSL2,  Next: Building And Testing The Speech Server,  Prev: Installing Remote Emacsvox,  Up: Installation

3.4 Updating An Existing WSL2 Installation
==========================================

The guided installer recompiles the source in the checkout; it does not
fetch newer source.  Keep the current speaking Emacsvox session open
while updating.  Run these commands from ‘M-x shell <RET>’ in that
session or another accessible WSL terminal:

     cd ~/src/emacsvox
     git status --short
     git branch --show-current
     git pull --ff-only
     ./bin/emacsvox-wsl-install --check
     ./bin/emacsvox-wsl-install

   Replace ‘~/src/emacsvox’ if you chose another Linux-filesystem
location.  Check that this is the intended branch before pulling.  The
fast-forward-only pull does not create a merge commit.  If Git reports
local changes, divergence, or another error, stop and review the
checkout; do not reset or discard files to complete an update.

   Rerunning the installer reuses supported Emacs and verified cached
downloads, installs a newly pinned Omnivox release when necessary,
rebuilds Emacsvox byte-code from scratch so files from the previous
source revision cannot survive, and repeats the launch checks.  These
operations change files on disk but do not stop the Emacsvox process
already providing speech.  If the update fails, leave that session
running while inspecting the error and named log.

   After the installer succeeds and you hear its final check, start a
second, isolated process from the updated checkout without closing the
old one:

     cd ~/src/emacsvox
     ./bin/emacsvox

   The old session remains the known-good source of speech while you
test its replacement.  The new process cannot retain Lisp loaded from
the previous version.  Confirm its ready message and ordinary buffer
reading.  In the new session, ‘M-x emacsvox-speak-version <RET>’ reports
the Emacsvox version and ‘M-x tts-speak-version <RET>’ reports the live
speech-server version.  Only after these checks pass should you save
work in the old session and exit it.

   Continue in the isolated session or start Emacsvox through your
normal customised entry point.  If the new session does not speak, close
it but leave the old session running while inspecting the diagnostic and
installer logs.

   To update Emacsvox without rerunning the guided installer, replace
the two installer commands after ‘git pull --ff-only’ with:

     make check-emacs
     make bytecode-rebuild
     ./bin/emacsvox --diagnose
     ./bin/emacsvox --check

   All four commands must succeed, and the final check must produce both
tone and speech, before the second-session test above.  This manual
procedure rebuilds Emacsvox but does not download or select a newly
pinned Omnivox release.  When Omnivox also needs an independent update,
follow _Manual Release Installation_ in *note Installing An Omnivox
Windows Release Under WSL2: Installing An Omnivox Windows Release Under
WSL2., then repeat the diagnostic and audible check.


File: emacsvox.info,  Node: Building And Testing The Speech Server,  Next: Quick Installation,  Prev: Updating Emacsvox Under WSL2,  Up: Installation

3.5 Install And Check Omnivox
=============================

If the guided WSL2 installer succeeded, Omnivox is already installed and
checked.  Continue to *note Quick Installation: Quick Installation.

   On macOS, follow the Homebrew instructions in *note Installing An
Omnivox Release Binary: Installing An Omnivox Release Binary.  They
install the matching Mac binary and record its stable executable path
for Emacsvox, including sessions that do not inherit the shell's ‘PATH’.
Homebrew installs Omnivox only; continue below to build and start
Emacsvox with your selected Emacs 30.2 or newer.

   On a matching GNU/Linux x86-64 host, or for a manual Mac
installation, use the archive instructions in that section.  They verify
the published 1.17.0 archive, keep its runtime data beside the
executable, and add its versioned installation directory to ‘PATH’.
Then run:

     omnivox --check

   Use *note Installing Omnivox From Source: Installing Omnivox From
Source. on Linux ARM64, an incompatible distribution, or a host whose
security policy does not permit the unsigned macOS archive.  No
proprietary speech runtime is needed for the GNU/Linux or macOS routes.
For a manual WSL2 installation, or details of what its guided installer
checks, see *note Installing An Omnivox Windows Release Under WSL2:
Installing An Omnivox Windows Release Under WSL2.

   The check plays a tone followed by speech.  Hearing both is the
acceptance condition; a successful exit code alone does not prove
audible output.  If either is missing, follow *note Backend Diagnostics
And Responsibilities: Backend Diagnostics And Ownership. before
continuing.

3.5.1 When the binary does not match the host
---------------------------------------------

The Linux x86-64 archive requires an Ubuntu 24.04-compatible glibc,
libstdc++, libgcc, and ALSA runtime.  The Omnivox source route requires
Rust, Python 3, a C and C++ toolchain, CMake, and Clang with libclang.
It also requires ‘pkg-config’ and ALSA development headers on GNU/Linux,
or Xcode command-line tools on macOS.  These are not requirements for
the recommended WSL2 binary route.  Docker and Windows .NET Framework
are needed only for the advanced reproducible source-build route in
*note Building Omnivox For WSL2: Building Omnivox For WSL2.

   *note Speech Backends: Speech Backends. owns the complete platform
matrix, alternative builds, and compatibility servers.  Native Windows
x64 is supported with Emacs 30.2 or newer; its PowerShell installer
reuses a prebuilt Emacs, can download a verified Emacs with
‘-DownloadEmacs’, or offers an explicit source build.  It installs
Omnivox without WSL; follow *note Installing Native Windows Emacsvox:
Trying Native Windows Emacsvox.


File: emacsvox.info,  Node: Quick Installation,  Next: Confirming First Speech,  Prev: Building And Testing The Speech Server,  Up: Installation

3.6 Build And Start Emacsvox
============================

The guided WSL2 installer has already selected Emacs, compiled Emacsvox,
and run the two checks below.  Go directly to the final step to start
the isolated session.  The full sequence is for GNU/Linux, macOS, manual
WSL2 installation, or later diagnosis.

  1. Enter the Emacsvox checkout:

          cd /path/where/you/keep/source/emacsvox

  2. Select Emacs 30.2 or newer.  If it is not the system ‘emacs’ and no
     ‘local.mk’ exists, create the ignored local settings file:

          cp local.mk.example local.mk

     Edit an existing ‘local.mk’ rather than replacing it.  Set ‘EMACS’
     there:

          EMACS=/path/to/emacs-31/bin/emacs

     The earlier ‘emacs --version’ checks your shell's default.  Verify
     the executable selected by the build separately:

          make check-emacs

     This checks the Emacs selected by ‘local.mk’ when present.  It can
     finish without printing anything when the version is supported.
     Resolve any executable or version error before compiling.

  3. Compile Emacsvox:

          make bytecode

     A successful build returns to the shell without an error.  Compile
     with the same supported Emacs selected in the previous step.

  4. Run the non-audible launcher preflight:

          ./bin/emacsvox --diagnose

     This checks the checkout, Emacs, and speech server without playing
     audio or opening interactive Emacs.  A successful report names
     Emacs 30.2 or newer, the ‘omnivox’ speech server, and an isolated
     ‘-Q’ startup.  Correct any reported path or version problem, then
     repeat the command.

  5. Check the complete launch path with sound:

          ./bin/emacsvox --check

     The launcher prints its selections, then plays a tone and speech.
     Confirm that both are audible before continuing.

  6. Start a clean Emacsvox session:

          ./bin/emacsvox

     The launcher uses Omnivox and the selected Emacs without loading
     site files or your personal init file.  It does not change an
     existing Emacs setup.

   For explicit paths, ‘./bin/emacsvox --help’ describes the ‘EMACS’,
‘TTS_PROGRAM’, and ‘EMACSVOX_DIR’ overrides.  Arguments after ‘--’ are
passed to Emacs.


File: emacsvox.info,  Node: Confirming First Speech,  Next: Recovering From No Speech,  Prev: Quick Installation,  Up: Installation

3.7 Confirming First Speech
===========================

The stable first-speech checkpoint is:

     Emacsvox is ready.

   It normally follows Omnivox's acknowledgement of initial engine
inventory, routing policy, and logical voices, and therefore uses the
restored engine policy.  If routing does not finish within five seconds,
Emacsvox speaks it through the capability-ready fallback.  Hearing the
checkpoint does not by itself prove that routing completed or every
package integration loaded.  An earlier introduction, overview key,
version number, and startup auditory icon may also be heard; those can
change.

   Run ‘M-x emacsvox-startup-status <RET>’ to check background
preparation of Emacsvox and its package integrations.  The command
speaks a summary and opens a report.  Integrations waiting for their
packages are normal: speech support loads when you open those packages.
Run the command again to refresh the report; ‘q’ closes it.

   Before connecting Emacsvox to your personal configuration, check the
live adapter and everyday feedback:

  1. Begin in an ordinary working buffer.  Press ‘C-e m’ and note its
     name, then run ‘M-x tts-speak-version <RET>’.  The live adapter
     should speak its version response.
  2. Use ‘C-h v tts-program <RET>’.  In this isolated launcher session,
     the value should name ‘omnivox’.  This opens Help; it is no longer
     a test in the original working buffer.
  3. Leave Help with ‘q’, then use ‘C-x b’ to select the working buffer
     by name if necessary.  Confirm it with ‘C-e m’ before moving over
     its lines with ‘C-n’ and ‘C-p’.  The reached lines should be
     spoken.

   ‘M-x emacsvox-describe-emacsvox <RET>’ opens the overview.  Return to
your working buffer before trying the buffer-local rate adjustments
below.  See *note Using Online Help: (emacsvox)Using Online Help. for
Help's selection and window behaviour.

   For a rate change through the settings interface, open ‘C-e H s’ and
select _Rate_; ‘<RET>’ or ‘r’ enters an exact value.  The direct
commands remain useful when first learning the menus: ‘C-e d -’,
followed by repeated ‘-’, slows the current buffer, and ‘C-e d r’
accepts an exact rate such as ‘40’ for Omnivox.  For individually echoed
characters, ‘C-e d f’ accepts a multiplier such as ‘0.7’.  A prefix
‘C-u’ on the exact-rate or character-factor command changes the global
default rather than only a buffer-local setting.

   When these checks pass, continue to *note Learn the audio desktop:
(emacsvox)Guided Tutorial. for connected practice, or use *note Basic
Usage: Basic Usage. as an everyday reference.  Connecting Emacsvox to
your normal configuration through *note Persistent Startup: Persistent
Startup. is a separate choice; it is not required to continue learning
in the isolated session.

3.7.1 Add optional engines after first speech
---------------------------------------------

‘C-e H v b’ opens the engine browser.  The same list is available with
‘M-x emacsvox-omnivox-manage-components <RET>’.  Select an engine and
press ‘<RET>’ for its details.  These distinguish current engine
availability from files installed for a separately managed release.

   For supported WSL release management, choose _Manage installed
release_ and check its version and location before choosing _Install
engine_.  The manager confirms, checksum-verifies, installs, and
validates the selected engine without ‘sudo’.  The installed-release
view also offers _Uninstall engine_ for a manager-installed engine.
Removal retains its verified download cache; _Install engine_ can
reinstall it.  Core engines and separately supplied runtimes cannot be
removed through that operation.

   The browser distinguishes included engines, downloadable components,
and bridges requiring a user-supplied runtime.  See *note Optional
Omnivox Engines: Optional Omnivox Engine Modules. for activation,
recovery, and licensing boundaries.  Optional-engine management is not a
prerequisite for ordinary WinRT speech.


File: emacsvox.info,  Node: Recovering From No Speech,  Next: Persistent Startup,  Prev: Confirming First Speech,  Up: Installation

3.8 Recovering From No Speech
=============================

Keep the isolated launcher as your known starting point.  Find the first
stage that failed rather than adding configuration at several layers at
once.

‘./bin/emacsvox --diagnose’ fails
     No audio or interactive Emacs has started.  Under WSL2, begin with
     ‘./bin/emacsvox-wsl-install --check’ and resolve its first reported
     item.  Otherwise, correct the selected ‘EMACS’, the ‘EMACS’ value
     in ‘local.mk’, or the installed Emacs version.  When the speech
     server is not executable, finish installing Omnivox, put it on
     ‘PATH’, or set ‘OMNIVOX_PROGRAM’ to its absolute executable.  The
     guided WSL2 installer records that selection in a per-user file;
     rerunning it repairs a missing installation or selection without
     requiring a shell startup edit.
The diagnostic passes, but ‘--check’ errors or is inaudible
     Diagnose the speech server before Emacs configuration.  Follow
     *note Backend Diagnostics And Responsibilities: Backend Diagnostics
     And Ownership. until both tone and speech are audible.
The audible check passes, but "Emacsvox is ready" is absent
     Run ‘./bin/emacsvox --diagnose’ again from the same checkout and
     shell.  Remove an unintended ‘TTS_PROGRAM’ override if it does not
     select ‘omnivox’.  Request a startup backtrace with:

          ./bin/emacsvox -- --debug-init

     ‘Could not find speech server executable’ means the server visible
     during that start cannot be executed.  Correct its reported path
     rather than adding more init-file configuration.  For an Omnivox
     process error, inspect the private log directory described below.
The ready message is heard, but one mode lacks spoken feedback
     Run ‘M-x emacsvox-startup-status <RET>’.  It names failed startup
     work or package integrations and retains the original error.
     Select the report and inspect its lines or paragraphs.  ‘M-x
     emacsvox-speak-buffer <RET>’ can read a short report; large
     requests have the limits described in *note Short reading and
     buffer limits: (emacsvox)Reading Without Moving Point.  Correct the
     named package, configuration, or Emacsvox problem, then verify in a
     fresh session.  For source edits, follow the byte-code build
     procedure before testing.  Repeating startup or restarting the
     speech server does not retry failed integrations: an interrupted
     load can leave some hooks or advice installed.  Keep the original
     error when requesting help.
The ready message is heard, but speech later stops
     If Emacs reports that a speech server exited, inspect the Omnivox
     session log and run ‘M-x tts-restart <RET>’ after correcting the
     server problem.  Logs are below ‘$XDG_STATE_HOME/emacsvox/omnivox’,
     defaulting to ‘~/.local/state/emacsvox/omnivox’.  Spoken text is
     omitted unless explicit text logging is enabled.
The isolated launcher works, but a customised Emacs start does not
     Remove or comment out the Emacsvox environment and ‘load-file’
     forms from your init file.  This rollback leaves the checkout and
     known-good ‘./bin/emacsvox’ path intact.  Re-add the forms in the
     order shown in *note Persistent Startup: Persistent Startup.;
     server selection must precede loading setup.


File: emacsvox.info,  Node: Persistent Startup,  Prev: Recovering From No Speech,  Up: Installation

3.9 Persistent Startup
======================

After *note Confirming First Speech: Confirming First Speech. succeeds,
add these forms to your init file, replacing the example path with the
checkout's absolute path:

     (setenv "TTS_PROGRAM" "omnivox")
     (load-file "/path/to/emacsvox/lisp/emacsvox-setup.el")

   Keep that order: Emacsvox selects and starts the server while loading
setup.  A shell alternative is to export the server name before
launching Emacs:

     export TTS_PROGRAM=omnivox

   Keep the explicit ‘load-file’ form for a changing source checkout.
Its setup entry point can warn and prefer source when newer Lisp would
otherwise be hidden by stale local byte-code.  A customised startup
should speak the same ready message as the isolated launcher.  If it
does not, remove or comment out the two init forms and return to the
isolated launcher before another change.


File: emacsvox.info,  Node: Guided Tutorial,  Next: Basic Usage,  Prev: Installation,  Up: Top

4 Learn the audio desktop
*************************

This tutorial takes you from reading a short document to exploring its
structure and making a deliberate speech adjustment.  Work through the
lessons in order the first time.  Each builds on the same practice
document; the exercises ask you to use what you have learned without
repeating every step.  Answers are collected at the end so you can try
before consulting them.

   Start with a speaking Emacsvox session prepared through *note
Installation: (emacsvox)Installation., including its settings-menu
dependency.  No mail account, agent service, downloaded voice, or
terminal package is needed.  The first five lessons work with text and
the Org mode supplied with Emacs.  The final lesson uses an already
available voice.  If your backend cannot preview an adjustment, keep the
working voice and follow the reported limitation rather than installing
another engine to complete the exercise.

   The required exercises do not save speech settings.  One clearly
marked optional exercise creates a personal palette; you can finish the
tutorial without doing it.  Do not practise in a document, draft, or
configuration that you need to preserve.

   If key notation is new to you, read *note Read the key instructions:
Emacs Key Notation. before starting.  It explains modifiers, separate
steps, capital letters and command names.  The short reminders below use
that notation.

   Keys are the defaults.  ‘C-n’ means hold Control and press n.  In
‘C-e l’, press Control-e, release it, then press lowercase l.  ‘M-’
normally means Alt or Option, ‘<RET>’ means Enter, and ‘<SPC>’ means
Space.  Capital letters matter.  Use ‘M-x’, type the command name, and
press ‘<RET>’ when a command is given by name.  *note Using this manual:
(emacsvox)Using This Manual. explains finding a topic and returning from
it.

* Menu:

* Prepare a practice buffer: Tutorial Practice Buffer.  Make a disposable copy of the practice text without editing repository files.
* Lesson 1 read and locate point: Tutorial Reading.  Distinguish movement, explicit reading, and stopping speech.
* Lesson 2 find, edit, and recover text: Tutorial Editing.  Search for a known word, make a small edit, and verify undo.
* Lesson 3 get Help and return to your work: Tutorial Help And Buffers.  Keep two tasks open, inspect a key, and return without losing your place.
* Lesson 4 navigate the document's structure: Tutorial Structured Navigation.  Explore Org headings, links, and a table without evaluating code.
* Lesson 5 change speech in one buffer: Tutorial Temporary Settings.  Compare punctuation levels, inspect scope, and restore the previous policy.
* Lesson 6 preview and keep a voice experiment: Tutorial Voice Experiments.  Compare an unsaved voice draft, resume it, and distinguish saving from selection.
* Exercise answers: Tutorial Exercise Answers.  Check the exercises and the reasons behind their recovery steps.
* Finish and choose your next task: Tutorial Next Steps.  Leave practice safely and choose a task or a deeper explanation.


File: emacsvox.info,  Node: Tutorial Practice Buffer,  Next: Tutorial Reading,  Up: Guided Tutorial

4.1 Prepare a practice buffer
=============================

A buffer is Emacs's working copy of text.  It need not have a file
behind it.  We will use a new buffer so that editing, undo, and
temporary settings cannot change the supplied example or an existing
document.

  1. Press ‘C-x b’, enter ‘evox-tutorial’, and press ‘<RET>’.  If that
     name already belongs to work you want to keep, use an unused name
     such as ‘evox-tutorial-2’ and substitute it throughout these
     lessons.
  2. Run ‘M-x text-mode <RET>’.  This starts with ordinary text rather
     than Org's structural commands.  Press ‘C-e m’ to hear the buffer
     and mode.
  3. Insert the practice text below.  In a source checkout, you can use
     ‘C-x i’ and select ‘docs/manual/examples/tutorial-practice.org’
     relative to that checkout.  This inserts a copy; it does not visit
     or modify that file.  Otherwise copy or type the displayed text
     into the empty buffer.
  4. Press ‘M-<’ to move to the beginning.  ‘C-e l’ reads the current
     line.  It should contain the heading ‘* Practice note’.

   The example is included here so you can use an installed Info or HTML
manual without a source checkout.  Preserve its asterisks, brackets,
pipes, and line breaks.  Those characters become useful in the structure
lesson.  The code at the end is for reading, not evaluation; do not run
it.

     * Practice note
     The green notebook is on the desk.
     This sentence is ready.
     Check (A+B), then C.

     ** Packing list
     - Notebook
     - Headphones
     - Cable

     ** Journey plan
     | Day     | Task | Status  |
     |---------+------+---------|
     | Monday  | Read | Ready   |
     | Tuesday | Edit | Planned |

     ** Reference
     [[*Packing list][Return to the packing list]]

     ** Code to read
     #+begin_src emacs-lisp
     (+ 2 3)
     #+end_src

   Keep the manual and the practice buffer available.  ‘C-x b’ followed
by your practice buffer's name returns to your work without closing the
manual.  Finish any active minibuffer prompt first; ‘C-g’ cancels an
ordinary unfinished Emacs command.  In a settings menu or an editor, use
the return procedure specified in the lesson instead of assuming that
every quit key is identical.

   If you need a fresh copy, leave menus and editors first, then use
‘C-x k’ while in the disposable practice buffer.  Check the buffer name
and discard only that practice text if asked.  Create a new buffer and
insert the example again.  Do not use this reset procedure on other
work.


File: emacsvox.info,  Node: Tutorial Reading,  Next: Tutorial Editing,  Prev: Tutorial Practice Buffer,  Up: Guided Tutorial

4.2 Lesson 1: read and locate point
===================================

Emacs calls the editing position _point_.  Movement changes point and
Emacsvox speaks what you reach.  Short inspections, such as reading a
line or character, leave point in place.  Learning that distinction
makes it easier to recover when speech is interrupted or you are unsure
where the next edit will occur.

  1. In the practice buffer, press ‘M-<’, then ‘C-n’.  You reach the
     line about the green notebook and hear its text.
  2. Press ‘C-e l’ twice.  Each invocation reads that line again without
     moving point.  Now press ‘C-n’: you reach the sentence about being
     ready, not a line farther down because of the repeated reading.
  3. Press ‘C-p’ to return to the notebook line, then ‘C-a’ to reach its
     start.  ‘C-e c’ reads the character at point.  Use ‘C-f’ to move
     one character forward and ‘C-b’ to move back.  Point moves only
     with the movement commands.
  4. Press ‘C-e b’ to start reading the buffer and ‘C-e s’ to stop that
     speech.  Then press ‘C-e l’.  It still works: stopping an utterance
     has not switched speech off or closed the document.

   This small-buffer exercise uses ‘C-e b’, which leaves point in place.
Tracked reading with ‘C-e i’ is different: on a supporting speech server
it advances point by chunks.  Stopping leaves a chunk boundary, not
necessarily the exact word last heard.  See *note Read from point, stop,
and start again: Reading From Point. when you are ready for longer
reading; it is not required for this exercise.

   The exact voice and pronunciation depend on your current settings.
Check the text you reached, not whether your system sounds like someone
else's.  ‘C-e m’ identifies the buffer and mode whenever your context is
unclear.

   If ‘M-<’ is intercepted by your desktop or screen reader, run ‘M-x
beginning-of-buffer <RET>’ instead.  This is an Emacs command, not a
requirement to change screen-reader configuration.

4.2.1 Try it
------------

*1A.*  From the start of the buffer, reach the line beginning ‘Check’
using line movement.  Read it twice without changing point.

   *1B.*  Start a longer reading, stop it, and establish both which
buffer you are in and what its current line contains.  Do not restart
Emacs or speech.

   See *note Answers for lesson 1: tutorial-answer-1.


File: emacsvox.info,  Node: Tutorial Editing,  Next: Tutorial Help And Buffers,  Prev: Tutorial Reading,  Up: Guided Tutorial

4.3 Lesson 2: find, edit, and recover text
==========================================

Searching locates text; it does not change the text.  Editing happens at
point, which is after the match when a forward search succeeds.  We will
make one small insertion, inspect it, and undo it before attempting a
replacement.

  1. Press ‘M-<’, then ‘C-s’ and type ‘ready’.  When the search finds
     the word, press ‘<RET>’ to finish.  Point is after ‘ready’ and
     before its full stop.
  2. Type a space followed by ‘now’.  ‘C-e l’ should read the sentence
     with ‘ready now’.  The other sentences are unchanged.
  3. In the practice document, press ‘C-g’ to deactivate any selection,
     then run ‘M-x undo-only <RET>’.  Read the line with ‘C-e l’.  If
     another undo is needed, run ‘undo-only’ again and inspect again.
     Undo works in editing groups, not necessarily one character at a
     time.  Stop when the sentence is back to ‘This sentence is ready.’;
     do not undo the original example.  Use ‘undo-only’ for this
     listen-between-steps pattern: after an intervening reading command,
     ordinary ‘C-/’ can redo an earlier undo instead.
  4. Search for ‘notebook’ from the beginning and finish with ‘<RET>’.
     Notice that searching and reading did not modify the document.  You
     can return to any known phrase without counting lines.

   If a search does not find its text, check your spelling.  ‘C-g’
during a failing search first backs out of the failure; another ‘C-g’
may be needed to leave the search.  Wait until the search prompt is gone
before typing ordinary text.  A successful search is accepted with
‘<RET>’, not by typing the edit into its search prompt.

4.3.1 Try it
------------

*2A.*  Replace ‘green’ with ‘blue’.  Use a successful search for the
whole word, then ‘M-<DEL>’ to remove the word immediately before point,
and type its replacement.  Here ‘<DEL>’ means Backspace; ‘M-x
backward-kill-word <RET>’ is an alternative to ‘M-<DEL>’.  Inspect the
complete line and restore ‘green’ before continuing.

   *2B.*  What would happen if you typed ‘now’ before accepting the
search?  Explain the difference between changing the query and changing
the document.

   See *note Answers for lesson 2: tutorial-answer-2.  Text undo is not
a general undo command for settings menus; we will practise their
recovery separately.  For more on inspecting between undo steps, see
*note Recover a text edit before saving: (emacsvox)Recovering Text
Edits. and *note Undo: (emacs)Undo. in the GNU Emacs Manual.


File: emacsvox.info,  Node: Tutorial Help And Buffers,  Next: Tutorial Structured Navigation,  Prev: Tutorial Editing,  Up: Guided Tutorial

4.4 Lesson 3: get Help and return to your work
==============================================

A Help buffer is another piece of work you can visit.  It need not
replace the document you were editing.  We will keep a second text
buffer available, then inspect a command without running it.

  1. Press ‘C-x b’, enter an unused name such as ‘evox-tutorial-notes’,
     and press ‘<RET>’.  Run ‘M-x text-mode <RET>’ and type ‘I can
     return to my document by name.’.
  2. Use ‘C-x b’ and your original practice buffer's name to return.
     Its text and position are still available.  ‘C-e m’ and ‘C-e l’
     confirm where you are.
  3. From the practice document, press ‘C-h k C-e l’.  Emacs describes
     the current-line reading key instead of executing it as the
     requested action.  Spoken key Help selects the ‘*Help*’ buffer and
     can remove other windows.  Your practice document is still open.
     ‘C-e h’ reads Help again; it selects Help rather than speaking it
     from the document's window.
  4. Press ‘C-e m’ to check which buffer is selected.  If it is
     ‘*Help*’, read there directly; otherwise use ‘C-x b’ to select
     ‘*Help*’ by name.  Do not switch windows merely because Help
     appeared.  Press ‘q’ while in Help when finished, then select the
     practice buffer with ‘C-x b’ if necessary.  Check its name and line
     before editing.
  5. Press ‘C-h m’ from the practice buffer, not from Help.  It
     describes that buffer's modes and commands.  Mode Help need not
     select its text for you; use the same check-and-select procedure to
     read it and return.

   The information comes from your running Emacs, so it is particularly
useful when a package or personal binding differs from this manual.  A
buffer and a window are different: switching buffers chooses content,
while ‘C-x o’ selects another window.  A changed layout does not mean a
buffer was closed.  You do not have to recreate a split to finish this
exercise: returning by buffer name is enough.  *note Find help while you
work: Using Online Help. explains how to read and leave ordinary Help
and the distinct return route used by settings-menu Help.

4.4.1 Try it
------------

*3A.*  Without closing either text buffer, visit the notes, add a second
line, and return to the practice document.  Confirm that each contains
its own text.

   *3B.*  Discover what ‘C-e s’ does without stopping speech as the
action you are investigating.  Then find the keys for the command name
reported by Help, using ‘C-h w’.

   See *note Answers for lesson 3: tutorial-answer-3.  For manual lookup
and navigation history, practise *note Find an answer in Info and
return: (emacsvox)Using This Manual. alongside this buffer-based Help
exercise.


File: emacsvox.info,  Node: Tutorial Structured Navigation,  Next: Tutorial Temporary Settings,  Prev: Tutorial Help And Buffers,  Up: Guided Tutorial

4.5 Lesson 4: navigate the document's structure
===============================================

The practice text is also an Org document.  Changing its major mode
gives Emacs commands that understand headings, lists, links, and tables.
The text remains yours; a folded section is hidden, not deleted.

  1. Return to the practice buffer and run ‘M-x org-mode <RET>’.  Press
     ‘M-<’, then ‘C-u C-u C-u <TAB>’ to show the whole document,
     including any folded sections.  Each ‘C-u’ is a separate prefix
     keystroke.  ‘C-e m’ now identifies Org rather than Text mode.
  2. From the first heading, press ‘C-c C-n’ to reach ‘Packing list’.
     This moves to a heading, skipping intervening body text.  ‘C-c C-p’
     returns to the previous visible heading.  Go forward to ‘Packing
     list’ again.
  3. Press ‘<TAB>’ on that heading to fold its body.  ‘C-c C-n’ reaches
     ‘Journey plan’.  Return with ‘C-c C-p’ and press ‘<TAB>’ to expose
     the list again.  Read down into it to confirm that the items are
     still present.
  4. Show all content again with ‘C-u C-u C-u <TAB>’, then search from
     the beginning for ‘Return to the packing list’.  Finish with
     ‘<RET>’ and press ‘C-b’ once to leave point inside the link text.
     ‘C-c C-o’ follows this internal link to the heading; no website or
     external file is opened.

   Heading speech and collapse cues depend on your presentation
settings.  What matters is the location and visibility you can inspect.
Do not require one exact spoken phrase or one physical voice to
recognise a heading.

4.5.1 Read the table without editing it
---------------------------------------

Show the whole document again, then search from the beginning for
‘Monday’ and finish with ‘<RET>’.  You are in the first data row's Day
cell.

  1. Press ‘C-e C-t C-r’ to enter table reading without moving.
     Emacsvox announces the table dimensions and the current cell.
  2. Press ‘<TAB>’ to reach the Task cell, ‘Read’.  ‘<RET>’ reads that
     cell again.  In this reader these keys do not align the table or
     insert a row.
  3. Press Down.  You remain in the Task column and hear the complete
     Tuesday row.  Press ‘c’ to read the column, or ‘r’ to repeat the
     current row.  ‘.’ reports coordinates; headers and data rows are
     distinguished.
  4. Press ‘q’ to leave table reading, then ‘C-e l’ to inspect the text
     line.  The table reader closes, not the practice buffer.  Ordinary
     Org editing keys are restored.

   Outside table reading, Org's ‘<TAB>’ is an editing/navigation command
that can align a table and create rows.  A key's meaning belongs to the
active mode or reader, not to its letter alone.  Do not evaluate the
source block at the end of the document: it is present so you can also
inspect literal code.

4.5.2 Try it
------------

*4A.*  Enter table reading at ‘Monday’ again.  Reach Tuesday's Status
cell and read it without typing into the table.  Then leave reading
mode.

   *4B.*  Fold ‘Packing list’, read another section, and bring the list
back.  Explain why text undo is not needed to recover the hidden items.

   See *note Answers for lesson 4: tutorial-answer-4.  *note Browsing
Structure: (emacsvox)Browsing Structure. documents the complete shared
table reader; *note Org heading motion: (org)Motion. and *note Org
visibility cycling: (org)Visibility Cycling. explain the underlying
document commands.


File: emacsvox.info,  Node: Tutorial Temporary Settings,  Next: Tutorial Voice Experiments,  Prev: Tutorial Structured Navigation,  Up: Guided Tutorial

4.6 Lesson 5: change speech in one buffer
=========================================

Now change how one line is spoken without changing its text.  This is a
live buffer adjustment, not a draft preview.  Closing the menu keeps it
in effect; restoring it is a separate action.  We will not save a
default.

  1. In the practice buffer, leave table reading if active.  Show all
     content, then search from the beginning for ‘Check (A+B), then C.’
     and accept the match.  Press ‘C-a’, then ‘C-e l’ to hear the
     starting presentation.
  2. Press ‘C-e H s p’ without a prefix argument.  Read the scope and
     current punctuation policy.  It must name the current buffer, not
     global defaults.  Record whether the original policy is automatic,
     an explicit level, or a named profile.  If scope is global, use ‘g’
     to choose buffer scope before changing anything.  Practise
     selecting an item with up/down and changing sections with
     left/right.  Press ‘C-c l’ to repeat the selected item, then ‘M-f’
     to hear its first word.  ‘C-a C-f’ reviews its first character.
     These review keys leave the same item selected and do not change
     punctuation.  In a menu, ‘C-e’ goes to the end of item review; the
     ordinary speech prefix returns after you close the menu.  See *note
     menu navigation: settings-menu-navigation. for the full controls.
  3. Press ‘? a’ for Help on All, then ‘q’ to return to the punctuation
     menu.  Press ‘a’ to apply All, followed by ‘C-q’ to close the
     menus.  Back in the document, ‘C-e l’ lets you compare the same
     line with punctuation spoken.
  4. Visit the notes buffer and read its line.  This operation did not
     set its punctuation policy.  Return to the practice buffer, reopen
     ‘C-e H s p’, choose ‘n’ for None, close with ‘C-q’, and compare the
     same line again.
  5. Restore the original policy.  For an automatic policy, use ‘r’ in
     that buffer's punctuation menu.  For an explicit level or named
     profile, use ‘p’ and select the recorded choice instead.  Close the
     menu and verify the line and the reported policy.  Do not press
     uppercase ‘S’.

   Punctuation pronunciation varies by engine and configuration, so
judge the change by the treatment of the line's punctuation rather than
an exact sample transcript.  The characters in the buffer never changed.
Restoring Automatic returns control to the mode policy; it is not a
synonym for choosing Some.

   An explicit global change can affect both the default and the source
buffer.  Saving a default adds a persistence step and does not cancel
stronger mode policies or buffer overrides.  Those are different tasks
from this temporary experiment; see *note Choose What Persists:
(emacsvox)Saving And Startup.

4.6.1 Try it
------------

*5A.*  From the practice buffer, press ‘C-e H /’, search for
‘punctuation’, and choose the punctuation control from completion.  The
first ‘<RET>’ selects its menu item without activating it.  Read its
Help, then leave without making a change.  Check the policy afterward.

   *5B.*  After choosing All and then closing the menu, should the
original policy return?  Describe how to restore it and why ‘C-/’ in the
document is not the right operation.

   See *note Answers for lesson 5: tutorial-answer-5.


File: emacsvox.info,  Node: Tutorial Voice Experiments,  Next: Tutorial Exercise Answers,  Prev: Tutorial Temporary Settings,  Up: Guided Tutorial

4.7 Lesson 6: preview and keep a voice experiment
=================================================

An installed _physical voice_ is a voice an engine can produce.  A
palette's _named voice_, such as ‘default’ for ordinary reading, chooses
physical voices and adjustments for a purpose.  Browsing a physical
voice does not replace that named entry.  We will first try a physical
voice without selecting it for ordinary reading or changing startup.

  1. From the practice buffer, press ‘C-e H v b’.  Select an available
     engine and press ‘v’ to browse its voices.  Expand a language group
     with ‘<RET>’ if needed, then choose an already usable voice.
  2. Press ‘P’ to hear its sample and ‘S’ to stop.  An unavailable or
     inactive downloaded voice is not suitable for this first exercise.
     Read its details rather than enabling, installing, or applying
     packages as a workaround.
  3. Press ‘t’ on the usable voice to open an experiment.  If this
     resumes someone else's or your earlier unfinished draft, leave it
     with Keep and choose another voice; do not overwrite or discard
     unrelated work.
  4. In your new editor draft, find _Average Pitch_ and note its value.
     Use ‘<RET>’ to enter ‘6’, or ‘5’ if it is already six.  Press ‘P’
     to preview and ‘B’ to compare the opening and edited versions with
     the same sample.  ‘S’ stops.  An automatic sample may also follow
     an adjustment in the full editor; navigation stops it.
  5. Press ‘u’ to undo that draft adjustment, compare again, then make
     the small adjustment once more.  This is editor undo, not text
     undo.  If the backend reports the adjustment unsupported, keep that
     limitation explicit and continue with browsing; do not infer that
     saving will fix support.
  6. Press ‘q’ in the editor and choose to keep the unfinished draft.
     Return through the browser's parent views with ‘q’ until you reach
     its menu, then close the menu sequence with ‘C-q’.  Ordinary
     reading is unchanged.
  7. From the practice buffer, press ‘C-e H p d’ and select your
     retained experiment.  Verify the value is still there.  To finish
     without saving, leave its editor with ‘q’ and choose to discard
     this practice draft only.  Return through the remaining views and
     menus.

   A retained draft lasts within the Emacs session.  It is not a saved
palette and does not survive restart.  Discarding it leaves ordinary
reading untouched, but discarding a draft would not undo a save that had
already completed.  The editor's status distinguishes unsaved work,
successful saving, and failed or pending application.  Never treat a
preview as proof of persistence.

4.7.1 Optional: save an inactive personal palette
-------------------------------------------------

This exercise writes personal palette data.  Skip it to keep the
tutorial entirely free of saved speech-configuration changes.  It
deliberately avoids startup selection and does not require you to
restart a working session.

  1. Open ‘C-e H v p’ and note the active palette and whether selection
     follows the standard baseline or is explicit.  Press ‘N’ to create
     a palette with an unused name such as ‘tutorial-reading’, a short
     description, and ‘acss-default’ as parent.  Creation saves the new
     palette but does not activate it.  Use another name if that one
     already exists.
  2. Enter your new palette with ‘<RET>’, select ‘default’, and press
     ‘e’ to edit it.  Change _Average Pitch_ as above and compare with
     ‘B’.  This entry controls ordinary reading, not every named voice
     in the palette.
  3. Choose _Save to collection without selecting_.  Wait for its result
     and check the destination and saved status.  Leave with ‘q’.  The
     palette now holds your entry, but it is still not selected for
     ordinary reading.
  4. To try live selection deliberately, return to the palette manager
     with ‘o’, select your new palette, and press ‘a’.  Return to the
     practice document and read the notebook line.  Other named voices
     retain their own definitions.  Rules or unsupported adjustments can
     make a contrast subtle; *note Inspect Or Adjust A Voice In Context:
     (emacsvox)Voices In Context. explains how to investigate.
  5. Reopen the palette manager.  If you originally followed the
     standard baseline, use ‘f’ to follow it again.  Otherwise select
     the original palette you recorded and press ‘a’.  Do not use ‘U’,
     the separate startup action, during this tutorial.  The inactive
     tutorial palette remains saved for later use.

   If saving or application fails, keep the reported status and follow
*note Recovering Unfinished Changes: (emacsvox)Recovering Aural Changes.
Do not repeat Create, delete files, or restart Emacs to hide the
failure.  After an accepted save, text undo and draft discard are not
rollback commands.  *note Make a Personal Reading Palette:
(emacsvox)Personal Palette Walkthrough. gives the fuller lifecycle,
including inheritance and startup selection.

4.7.2 Try it
------------

*6A.*  Create another small preview-only experiment, keep its draft, and
resume it without saving.  Then discard that practice draft and
establish that your working document is still there.

   *6B.*  Explain the difference between Preview, Keep draft, Save to
collection, Activate, and Use at Startup.  Which of them did the
required exercise use?  Which additional operations belong to the
optional exercise?

   See *note Answers for lesson 6: tutorial-answer-6.


File: emacsvox.info,  Node: Tutorial Exercise Answers,  Next: Tutorial Next Steps,  Prev: Tutorial Voice Experiments,  Up: Guided Tutorial

4.8 Exercise answers
====================

Use these answers to check your approach, not as a script you must
memorise.  Equivalent commands are useful when their scope and result
are the same.  In Info, ‘l’ retraces the link from an exercise to its
answer.

4.8.1 Answers: lesson 1
-----------------------

*1A.*  With the unmodified example, ‘M-< C-n C-n C-n’ reaches the Check
line.  ‘C-e l’ reads it without moving, so two repetitions leave the
editing position unchanged.  A different number of inserted lines
changes the count; search is more dependable when you no longer know the
exact layout.

   *1B.*  Use ‘C-e b’, then ‘C-e s’.  ‘C-e m’ identifies the buffer and
mode; ‘C-e l’ reads the line at point.  Stopping speech does not delete
text, move to another buffer, or disable later speech commands.

4.8.2 Answers: lesson 2
-----------------------

*2A.*  ‘M-< C-s green <RET>’ places point after green.  ‘M-<DEL>’
removes that word; typing ‘blue’ supplies the replacement.  Read the
whole line to catch spacing or punctuation mistakes.  Restore it with a
search for blue followed by the same replacement procedure.
Alternatively, use ‘undo-only’ and inspect after each step, as in the
lesson.  Deletion and insertion may need separate undos.  Stop at the
original line rather than assuming a fixed undo count.

   *2B.*  Before ‘<RET>’, the letters extend the search query.  No
document edit has occurred.  Accept a successful search, then insert the
text at point.

4.8.3 Answers: lesson 3
-----------------------

*3A.*  Switch to the notes by name, use ‘M->’ to reach their end, add a
newline and a short sentence, then switch back by name.  Neither switch
closes the other buffer.  ‘C-e m’ and line reading distinguish them.

   *3B.*  ‘C-h k C-e s’ describes the effective binding.  With the
default maps, the command is ‘tts-stop’.  ‘C-h w tts-stop <RET>’ reports
its keys in the current context.  Help describes the binding; it does
not invoke it as the requested action.  If you need the keys available
in the practice document, return there before using ‘C-h w’.  A Help
buffer has its own local bindings.

4.8.4 Answers: lesson 4
-----------------------

*4A.*  From Monday's cell, enter with ‘C-e C-t C-r’, press Down for
Tuesday, then ‘<TAB>’ twice to reach Status.  ‘<RET>’ reads ‘Planned’.
‘q’ restores normal Org editing.  While table reading is active, these
operations do not realign the source, add rows, or evaluate formulas.

   *4B.*  ‘<TAB>’ on the heading changes visibility.  Return to it and
cycle open, or use ‘C-u C-u C-u <TAB>’ to expose the whole document.
The hidden items never left the buffer.  Text undo would affect edits,
not provide the intended folding operation.

4.8.5 Answers: lesson 5
-----------------------

*5A.*  Search accepts a result by selecting its menu control.  The
separate activation step is what can open an editor or change a value.
Reading Help and leaving without activation does not change the
punctuation policy.

   *5B.*  Closing keeps the chosen live adjustment.  Restore the
recorded policy in the buffer's punctuation menu: ‘r’ for Automatic, or
‘p’ and the recorded explicit choice.  Text undo does not restore speech
settings.  Uppercase ‘S’ would save a default, so it is deliberately
absent from this exercise.

4.8.6 Answers: lesson 6
-----------------------

*6A.*  Keep the practice draft on leaving its editor, return to the
document, and use ‘C-e H p d’ to resume that specific draft.  Inspect
it, then leave with Discard.  Use the editor and menu return commands,
or select the practice buffer by name once the menus are closed.  Do not
discard another draft to make the chooser empty.

   *6B.*  Preview auditions working values.  Keep draft retains unsaved
work in this Emacs session.  Save to collection writes the destination
without selecting an inactive palette.  Activate chooses the palette for
this session.  Use at Startup changes its selection in a saved
Presentation Profile; it is not the same as saving the palette.  The
required exercise uses Preview and Keep draft, then discards.  The
optional exercise additionally creates and saves a palette and
optionally activates it before restoring the original selection.
Neither exercise needs Use at Startup.


File: emacsvox.info,  Node: Tutorial Next Steps,  Prev: Tutorial Exercise Answers,  Up: Guided Tutorial

4.9 Finish and choose your next task
====================================

Finish any practice draft in its editor, close menus, restore the
punctuation policy, and restore the original palette if you deliberately
activated another.  Keep saved work separate from unsaved practice when
choosing what to remove.

   To discard the text exercises, use ‘C-x k’ on each tutorial buffer
after checking its name.  To keep your own text instead, use ‘C-x C-w’
and choose an unused file in your personal documents directory.  Review
any overwrite prompt and cancel rather than replacing an existing file.
Saving text does not save voice settings, and saving a palette does not
save the practice document.

   A saved tutorial palette can remain inactive.  To remove it later,
use the palette manager's deletion action only on the unused personal
palette you created, after finishing its drafts and checking known
references.  Do not remove a built-in palette or other saved work as
part of tutorial cleanup.

   You can now read a location twice without moving, recover a small
edit, visit Help without abandoning work, distinguish a document's
structure from its text, and tell a temporary setting from a preview or
a saved resource.  *note Find a task: (emacsvox)Finding Tasks. is the
quickest route to a specific goal.  *note Basic Usage: (emacsvox)Basic
Usage. and *note Audio Desktop: (emacsvox)Audio Desktop. provide the
everyday reference, while *note Aural Presentation: (emacsvox)Aural
Presentation. explains rules, palettes, voices, and saved setups in
depth.  The application guides then apply these habits to mail,
terminals, and agents.


File: emacsvox.info,  Node: Basic Usage,  Next: Using Online Help,  Prev: Guided Tutorial,  Up: Top

5 Basic Usage
*************

Once Emacsvox is speaking, ordinary Emacs commands let you read, edit
and navigate.  Emacsvox announces their results.  Short speech commands
inspect text at your editing position, while tracked reading follows a
longer passage.  This chapter moves from those reading choices to
selecting and editing text, then to everyday speech controls.  *note
Audio Desktop: Audio Desktop. explains how to keep several tasks
available through buffers and windows.

* Menu:

* How Emacsvox Helps: Overview.  Understand the main kinds of Emacsvox feedback.
* Using Emacs Buffers: Working.  Understand feedback while editing and completing text.
* Reading And Navigating: Reading.  Reading chunks of information.
* Select, copy, and move text: Editing Text With Speech.  Check a selection, copy or move it, and recover a mistaken edit.
* Find and replace text carefully: Searching And Replacing Text.  Search deliberately and choose the scope before replacing occurrences.
* Adjust Speech While You Work: Speech System.  Adjust speech for the way you are working.
* Hear Structure With Voice Lock: Voice-lock.  Hear document and program structure through voice changes.
* Find Out Where You Are: Status Information.  Ask about the current buffer, position, and Emacs state.

5.1 Key Notation And Emacs Words
================================

Start with *note Read the key instructions: Emacs Key Notation. if the
notation is unfamiliar.  It explains modifiers, uppercase letters and
successive keypresses before asking you to use them.  *note Answer a
prompt and return to your work: Answering Emacs Prompts. covers command
names, completion and filename questions.

   A _buffer_ holds text or an application view; _point_ is the position
at which movement and editing act.  The selection guide below introduces
the mark and region when they are needed.  For a separate introduction
to Emacs editing, ‘C-h t’ opens its interactive tutorial.

5.2 Your First Few Minutes
==========================

Try these in the speaking session started during installation:

  1. Press ‘C-n’ to move down one line and ‘C-p’ to move back up.
     Emacsvox speaks the destination line.
  2. Press ‘C-e l’ to hear that line without moving.
  3. Press ‘C-e s’ to stop speech.  Later commands still speak.
  4. Press ‘C-h k’, then a key, to learn what it does.
  5. Press ‘C-h m’ to discover the current modes and commands.
  6. Press ‘C-h C-e’ for the Emacsvox overview.

   When you want to adjust speech, ‘C-e H s’ opens Speech settings.
Browse with arrows and ask for an item's Help with ‘?’ followed by its
key.  ‘<RET>’ activates it; ‘q’ goes back and ‘C-q’ closes the menu
sequence.  Browsing does not change a value, but leaving does not
reverse adjustments already applied.  Detailed scope and saving
instructions follow below.

   The keys in this manual are defaults.  Use ‘C-h k’ if one behaves
differently.  The generated *note Emacsvox Keymaps:
(emacsvox-reference)Emacsvox Keymaps. records default bindings; live
Help reflects installed packages and personal customisations.  These
menu quit keys apply while an Emacsvox menu is active, not to every
application buffer.


File: emacsvox.info,  Node: Overview,  Next: Working,  Up: Basic Usage

5.3 Emacsvox Overview
=====================

Emacsvox speaks movement and command results.  Integrations present
information in meaningful units such as messages, search results,
tables, source code, and Web pages.  Voices can distinguish headings,
links, comments, and other text; auditory icons and tones can identify
events without adding words.  Pronunciation dictionaries correct names
and terms globally or for a mode or file.

   Notice these distinctions before changing them.  Compare structural
voices in *note Hear Structure With Voice Lock: Voice-lock.  *note Aural
Presentation: Aural Presentation. explains speech, sounds, rules, and
reusable voice settings in detail.


File: emacsvox.info,  Node: Working,  Next: Reading,  Prev: Overview,  Up: Basic Usage

5.4 Using Emacs Buffers
=======================

Much editing feedback is automatic.  Echo settings can speak each
character or each completed word.  Completion announces candidates,
inserted completion, and the selected item.  Prompts and error messages
are also spoken.

   Other information is requested explicitly:

   • Reading commands speak a character, word, line, region or larger
     unit.  Most inspections preserve point; tracked rest-of-buffer
     reading advances it.  Names usually begin with ‘emacsvox-speak-’
     and default keys use ‘C-e’.(1)
   • Speech-system commands adjust rate, punctuation, echo, and related
     controls.  Many use ‘C-e d’ and names beginning ‘tts-’.(2)
   • Help commands explain the active modes, bindings, and integrations.

   The settings menus provide discoverable routes to these controls; the
direct keys remain useful for frequent actions.  ‘C-e S’ opens focused
settings for the current integration, or general buffer settings when no
focused menu exists.  ‘C-e H .’ always opens general buffer settings.

   Buffers remain available as you change tasks.  ‘C-e n’ and ‘C-e p’
cycle among buffers with the same major mode; repeat with ‘n’ or ‘p’.
See *note Audio Desktop: Desktop Objects. for buffer selection and its
other navigation controls.

   ---------- Footnotes ----------

   (1) The prefix comes from Emacspeak, where C-e was mnemonic for the
project name.

   (2) The d originally referred to DECtalk; this prefix now contains
engine-independent controls.


File: emacsvox.info,  Node: Reading,  Next: Editing Text With Speech,  Prev: Working,  Up: Basic Usage

5.5 Reading And Navigating
==========================

Choose the amount of text you need to hear.  Use a line or paragraph for
a local check, a selected region for exact editing boundaries, or ‘C-e
i’ for longer reading with a tracked position.  Ordinary movement speaks
what you reach; explicit inspection does not require that movement
first.

   The default keys are collected here.  The following two sections
explain which preserve point, how to stop and start again, and the
limits of large requests.  These distinctions matter before you begin
selecting text.

‘C-e c’

     ‘emacsvox-speak-char’ speaks the character at point phonetically.
‘C-e w’

     ‘emacsvox-speak-word’ speaks the current word.  Repeating it at the
     same position spells the word.
‘C-e l’

     ‘emacsvox-speak-line’ speaks the current line.
‘C-e [’

     ‘emacsvox-speak-paragraph’ speaks the current paragraph.
‘C-e ]’

     ‘emacsvox-speak-page’ speaks the current page.
‘C-e r’

     ‘emacsvox-speak-region’ speaks the region between point and mark.
     Establish and check the selection before editing; large requests
     have the windowful limit explained below.
‘C-e i’

     ‘emacsvox-speak-rest-of-buffer’ reads from point towards the buffer
     end with chunk-based position tracking.  It requires a supporting
     server; see *note Read from point, stop, and start again: Reading
     From Point.
‘C-e b’

     ‘emacsvox-speak-buffer’ reads the accessible buffer without
     tracking point through the speech.  Large requests fall back to
     windowful reading, as explained below.
‘C-e s’

     ‘tts-stop’ stops current speech.
‘C-e C-d’

     ‘emacsvox-toggle-show-point’ toggles the aural indication of point
     within spoken text.

   Many reading commands accept a prefix to limit speech before or after
point.  Use ‘C-h k’ before relying on a variant; exact prefix behaviour
belongs to that command's documentation.

* Menu:

* Read a short passage without moving point: Reading Without Moving Point.  Inspect words, sentences and paragraphs while keeping your editing position.
* Read from point, stop, and start again: Reading From Point.  Use tracked reading and understand where interruption leaves point.
* Hear phonetic names after a pause: Delayed Phonetic Reading.  Inspect characters with delayed phonetic names and cancel stale feedback.


File: emacsvox.info,  Node: Reading Without Moving Point,  Next: Reading From Point,  Up: Reading

5.5.1 Read a short passage without moving point
-----------------------------------------------

Use ‘C-e l’ to check the line containing the next edit, ‘C-e w’ to hear
its word, and ‘C-e c’ to inspect its character.  Repeating ‘C-e w’ at
the same position spells the word.  These are reading commands, not
movement commands.  For a larger unit, ‘C-e [’ reads the paragraph and
‘M-x emacsvox-speak-sentence <RET>’ reads the sentence around point.

   A positive prefix changes line, sentence or paragraph reading to
start at point.  For example, ‘C-u C-e l’ reads the rest of the line;
‘C-u C-e [’ reads the rest of the paragraph.  ‘C-u -1 C-e l’ reads from
the line's beginning to point.  Enter the minus sign and digit as part
of the prefix argument, not as document text.  The default form without
a prefix reads the whole unit.

   Emacs determines sentences, paragraphs and pages from the mode and
text.  A page for ‘C-e ]’ is an Emacs text page, not necessarily one
screenful or a printed page.  That command also sets a page mark: do not
use it while preserving a selection for an edit.  For more detail, see
*note Sentences: (emacs)Sentences., *note Paragraphs: (emacs)Paragraphs.
and *note Pages: (emacs)Pages. in the GNU Emacs Manual.

   For a bounded passage, set and inspect a region as described in *note
Select and check the text first: Selecting Text With Speech., then use
‘C-e r’.  A short region read preserves its endpoints and active
selection.  Speech follows pronunciation and presentation settings, so
silence is not proof that the selected source contains no punctuation or
whitespace.  Inspect the boundaries before editing.

   Whole-buffer reading with ‘C-e b’, or the untracked rest-of-buffer
form ‘C-u C-e b’, is useful for short text.  Neither supplies the
interruption position tracking described next.  With the current default
size limit, buffer or region requests of 40,000 characters or more fall
back to windowful reading rather than reading the entire request.  That
helper removes other windows and reads a screenful from point.  Use
smaller passages to retain your window arrangement, or tracked reading
when the backend supports it.  The limit is recorded in
‘ems--large-text-size’.

   These commands read the buffer's accessible text.  In Info, that is
the current node, not the entire manual; narrowing can also restrict a
document.  Folding, pronunciation and mode-specific presentation affect
what you hear.  Use the application's structural reader for tables,
messages or other special views, and *note Narrowing: (emacs)Narrowing.
in the GNU Emacs Manual for restricted editing views.


File: emacsvox.info,  Node: Reading From Point,  Next: Delayed Phonetic Reading,  Prev: Reading Without Moving Point,  Up: Reading

5.5.2 Read from point, stop, and start again
--------------------------------------------

‘C-e i’ starts reading at point and continues towards the accessible end
of the buffer.  Unlike a line or paragraph inspection, it advances point
as sentence-oriented chunks complete.  This requires a speech server
supporting tracked playback completion.  An unsupported server reports
that limitation; it does not silently provide equivalent position
tracking.

   Try it in an ordinary prose buffer, with no selection you need to
preserve:

  1. Find the passage you want and finish any search or prompt.  Use
     ‘C-a’ for the beginning of its line and ‘C-e l’ to check it.  For
     the beginning of the accessible buffer, use ‘M-<’ instead.
  2. Press ‘C-e i’ and listen without entering another command.  The
     reading position advances by chunks, not by each spoken word.
  3. Press ‘C-e s’ to stop without moving to a different passage.  Then
     use ‘C-e l’ to inspect the retained line before editing or moving.
  4. Press ‘C-e i’ again to start from the retained position.  Part of
     the last chunk can be repeated; this is not an exact-word
     pause/resume control.

   Stopping retains the beginning of the current chunk, not necessarily
the last word you heard.  Another user command interrupts tracked
reading before performing its own action.  A movement command therefore
also changes the retained position, and ordinary typing can insert text
there.  Use the explicit Stop command when you want to listen, stop, and
decide what to do next.  An existing active region would change as point
advances, so use ‘C-e r’, not ‘C-e i’, to check a selection.

   Chunks prefer sentence boundaries and are bounded by
‘emacsvox-tracked-reading-max-chars’, currently 280 source characters.
This is not a word count or an exact acoustic position.  Completion
reaches the saved end of the accessible text; failure or interruption
ends the reading session.  You can inspect the text and issue a new
reading command without restarting Emacs.  On a backend without tracked
support, read paragraphs or short regions, or use the untracked buffer
form with the size/layout limitations above.

   For movement to another starting point, see *note Moving point:
(emacs)Moving Point. in the GNU Emacs Manual.  The speech commands
themselves belong to Emacsvox; their exact interfaces are in *note the
core speech reference: (emacsvox-reference)emacsvox-speak.


File: emacsvox.info,  Node: Delayed Phonetic Reading,  Prev: Reading From Point,  Up: Reading

5.5.3 Hear Phonetic Names After A Pause
---------------------------------------

‘M-x emacsvox-delayed-phonetic-mode’ toggles delayed phonetics.  With it
enabled, character movement speaks the letter normally, then a pause
gives its phonetic name, such as "alpha" for "a".  These are the English
names also used by ‘C-e c’.

   The mode starts enabled with a one-second delay.  Customize
‘emacsvox-delayed-phonetic-delay’ to another positive number of seconds
and save the mode preference through Customize.  The menu at ‘C-e H s t’
also exposes delayed phonetics and its global delay, using a minimum 0.1
seconds for numeric entry.

   Further input, including the first key of a prefix, cancels the
pending name.  New speech, stopping speech, edited source text, or
leaving its buffer/window also prevents stale feedback.  Each pause
produces at most one name.  Typing, deletion, punctuation, and explicit
phonetic reading do not trigger the delay.


File: emacsvox.info,  Node: Editing Text With Speech,  Next: Searching And Replacing Text,  Prev: Reading,  Up: Basic Usage

5.6 Select, copy, and move text
===============================

Reading a passage is not the same as selecting it for an operation.
These procedures make the affected text and destination explicit before
changing anything.  They apply to ordinary editable text, not terminal
input, a settings editor or a table reader.  Keep using the
application's own instructions in those contexts.

   For practice, use ‘C-x b’ to create an unused buffer named
‘evox-edit-source’, run ‘M-x text-mode <RET>’, and insert the following
text, including the blank lines and a final newline after the last
sentence.  Use another unused name if that buffer already contains work.
No file or speech-setting save is needed.

     Keep the opening paragraph.

     Move this paragraph.

     Keep the closing paragraph.

   Keep the manual open separately and return to this buffer before
trying a step.  *note Answer a prompt and return to your work: Answering
Emacs Prompts. explains command entry; the *note Killing and moving
text: (emacs)Killing. chapter in the GNU Emacs Manual supplies the wider
editing reference.

* Menu:

* Select and check the text first: Selecting Text With Speech.  Set point and mark, hear the region, and check its newline boundary.
* Copy a passage into another buffer: Copying Text Between Buffers.  Preserve the source and check what is inserted at the destination.
* Move a paragraph without losing it: Moving Text Safely.  Cut a checked passage, place it elsewhere, and verify both locations.
* Choose text copied earlier: Choosing Earlier Copied Text.  Retrieve a previous kill-ring entry without confusing insertion and replacement.
* Recover a text edit before saving: Recovering Text Edits.  Undo deliberately in the right buffer and distinguish recovery from saving.
* Exchange text with another application: Copying Through The Clipboard.  Distinguish the Emacs kill ring from operating-system clipboard integration.


File: emacsvox.info,  Node: Selecting Text With Speech,  Next: Copying Text Between Buffers,  Up: Editing Text With Speech

5.6.1 Select and check the text first
-------------------------------------

Point is the moving end of a selection; the mark holds its other end.
The _region_ is the text between them, whichever comes first.  This
procedure uses Emacs's normal Transient Mark mode, not the Transient
settings-menu package.  Do not change either mode just because their
names are similar.

  1. In the practice buffer, press ‘M-<’, then ‘C-n’ twice and ‘C-a’.
     Check with ‘C-e l’ that you reached ‘Move this paragraph.’.
  2. Press ‘C-<SPC>’: hold Control and press the space bar.  This sets
     the mark at the line's start.  Release the keys, then press ‘C-n’
     once.  Point is now at the start of the following blank line.
  3. Press ‘C-e r’ to hear the region.  It contains the sentence and its
     terminating newline.  Reading this short selection does not move
     its ends or deactivate it, so it remains ready for copying or
     cutting.
  4. Press ‘C-b’ once to move point back before that newline.  The
     sentence is still selected, but its newline is not.  ‘C-e c’ can
     identify the character at this boundary; the character at the
     region's end is excluded.  Press ‘C-f’ to include the newline
     again.

   If the operating system intercepts ‘C-<SPC>’, ‘M-x set-mark-command
<RET>’ is an alternative.  Use a small selection and ordinary unshifted
movement for this exercise.  Shift-selection has different deactivation
rules.

   ‘C-x C-x’ exchanges point and mark without changing the selected
text.  Emacsvox speaks the line at the new point with point indication.
You can inspect that boundary, move it if necessary, and exchange again
to return to the other end.  Starting a new selection with ‘C-<SPC>’
replaces the boundary; it is not the command for inspecting the old one.

   In the ordinary text buffer, ‘C-g’ deactivates the selection without
deleting text.  It does not erase the saved mark.  Some region commands
can still use an inactive mark, so always establish a fresh, checked
selection before cutting.  Likewise, typing over selected text depends
on Delete Selection mode; use the explicit operations below rather than
assuming typing will replace it.

   More detail: *note The mark and the region: (emacs)Mark., *note
Setting the mark: (emacs)Setting Mark., *note Operating on the region:
(emacs)Using Region. and *note Shift selection: (emacs)Shift Selection.
in the GNU Emacs Manual.


File: emacsvox.info,  Node: Copying Text Between Buffers,  Next: Moving Text Safely,  Prev: Selecting Text With Speech,  Up: Editing Text With Speech

5.6.2 Copy a passage into another buffer
----------------------------------------

Emacs calls its remembered copied or cut text the _kill ring_.  Copy
with ‘M-w’ and insert with ‘C-y’, called _yank_ in Emacs.  The names do
not imply that copying deletes anything.

  1. Select the middle sentence and its newline using the previous
     procedure.  Hear it with ‘C-e r’, then press ‘M-w’.  Emacsvox
     reports the copy; the source text stays in place and the mark is
     normally deactivated.
  2. Press ‘C-x b’, choose an unused name such as ‘evox-edit-notes’, and
     run ‘M-x text-mode <RET>’.  Check the buffer with ‘C-e m’.  Use an
     empty buffer for this first copy, rather than inserting into an
     existing document.
  3. Press ‘C-y’.  Emacsvox speaks the inserted text.  Point is after it
     and the mark is at its beginning, normally inactive.  Immediately
     press ‘C-e r’ to hear that range again; do not set a new mark
     first.
  4. Return to ‘evox-edit-source’ with ‘C-x b’ and read the middle
     paragraph.  It is still there.  The destination contains an
     independent text copy.

   For a real document, check the insertion point and deactivate any
unrelated selection before pasting.  A copied newline moves subsequent
text to another line; a copied blank line can preserve paragraph
separation.  Inspect the surrounding text as well as the pasted passage
before saving.

   During the transfer, ‘C-e m’ and ‘C-e l’ are useful checks.  Avoid
‘C-e f’ for filename inspection and ‘C-e a’ for message repetition at
this point: those commands also put their result in the kill ring and
can change the next paste.  ‘M-x emacsvox-speak-current-kill <RET>’
inspects the current entry without inserting it.  Another application's
clipboard copy can also change what ‘C-y’ inserts.

   If the text goes to the wrong place, stop and use *note Recover a
text edit before saving: Recovering Text Edits. rather than pasting more
copies.  For the general commands, see *note Yanking: (emacs)Yanking.
and *note The kill ring: (emacs)Kill Ring. in the GNU Emacs Manual.
*note Keep a reference and a draft in separate windows: Reference And
Draft Windows. offers another way to manage the two working contexts.


File: emacsvox.info,  Node: Moving Text Safely,  Next: Choosing Earlier Copied Text,  Prev: Copying Text Between Buffers,  Up: Editing Text With Speech

5.6.3 Move a paragraph without losing it
----------------------------------------

Cutting with ‘C-w’ removes the region and stores it for yanking.
Practise in the unchanged three-paragraph source above; copying in the
previous task did not alter it.  Do not overwrite an existing buffer to
recreate the example.

  1. Select the middle sentence from its beginning.  This time move down
     twice after setting the mark, stopping at the beginning of ‘Keep
     the closing paragraph.’.  The region contains the middle paragraph
     and both following newlines, but none of the closing paragraph.
     Check with ‘C-e r’.
  2. Press ‘C-w’.  Emacsvox announces the cut.  The source now contains
     the opening and closing paragraphs, and point is where the removed
     passage began.  Inspect the remaining text without making another
     cut or copy.
  3. Press ‘M->’ for the buffer end.  With the example's final newline
     present, press ‘<RET>’ once to add a blank line after the closing
     paragraph.  Press ‘C-y’ to insert the cut paragraph there.
  4. Read from the beginning using short passages.  The order should be
     opening, closing, then the moved paragraph, separated by blank
     lines.  Check that the middle paragraph appears only once.

   In other text, decide deliberately whether to move a following
newline or blank line.  Cutting only the visible words can leave an
empty paragraph at the source and join words at the destination.  For a
transfer between files, verify both buffers before saving either: this
is not one atomic file move.

   Consecutive kills can combine into one kill-ring entry.  Avoid using
another kill command to tidy the destination before yanking the passage;
it can alter what you insert.  ‘C-k’, for example, kills to the line end
and can remove the newline when used there.  See *note Killing by lines:
(emacs)Killing by Lines. and *note Appending kills: (emacs)Appending
Kills. in the GNU Emacs Manual before using that faster editing pattern.


File: emacsvox.info,  Node: Choosing Earlier Copied Text,  Next: Recovering Text Edits,  Prev: Moving Text Safely,  Up: Editing Text With Speech

5.6.4 Choose text copied earlier
--------------------------------

The kill ring keeps more than the latest passage.  At the intended
destination, ‘M-y’ after a non-yank command opens a chooser for earlier
entries.  Use completion or prompt history to inspect candidates, then
‘<RET>’ to insert the chosen text.  ‘C-g’ cancels before insertion.
These entries may contain private text; use a harmless example when
practising or sharing your screen or audio.

   Immediately after ‘C-y’, the same ‘M-y’ instead replaces that
insertion with an earlier entry.  Further consecutive ‘M-y’ commands
cycle through earlier entries, and Emacsvox speaks each inserted
candidate.  Once you issue a separate reading or movement command, do
not assume ‘M-y’ will still replace the last paste: outside the yank
sequence it opens the chooser for another insertion.

   To verify a candidate while cycling, listen to the automatic yank
feedback.  When you decide to keep it, inspect its surroundings
normally.  If you have already broken the sequence and want a different
passage, undo the unwanted insertion, check the destination, and choose
again.  Repeated ‘C-y’ inserts more copies; it is not an accept or
preview command.

   More detail: *note Yanking earlier kills: (emacs)Earlier Kills. in
the GNU Emacs Manual.  Kill-ring history is useful recovery information,
not a permanent backup of your work.


File: emacsvox.info,  Node: Recovering Text Edits,  Next: Copying Through The Clipboard,  Prev: Choosing Earlier Copied Text,  Up: Editing Text With Speech

5.6.5 Recover a text edit before saving
---------------------------------------

Undo belongs to the selected buffer.  Finish prompts and leave special
menus, check the buffer with ‘C-e m’, and press ‘C-g’ in ordinary text
to deactivate any selection before undoing across the buffer.  An active
region can limit undo to that region.

   Use ‘M-x undo-only <RET>’ to undo one recorded change, then inspect
with ‘C-e l’ or a freshly selected short region.  Deactivate that
inspection region before the next undo.  Repeat only as far as
necessary.  Insertion and deletion can occupy separate undo records; a
multi-step move can therefore need more than one correction.

   ‘C-/’ is the familiar undo key and works for an initial undo.
However, an intervening reading or movement command breaks its undo
sequence, and a later ordinary undo can redo what you just undid.
‘undo-only’ avoids that reversal when you pause to inspect through
speech.  ‘M-x undo-redo <RET>’ deliberately reapplies undone changes
when the buffer's undo history permits it.

   For an accidental transfer between buffers, undo the insertion at the
destination and the cut at the source separately, inspecting each.
Copying needs no source undo because it left the source unchanged.
Neither operation writes a file until you save.  Correcting a saved edit
changes the buffer again; save the verified correction explicitly if the
file on disk must change too.  Do not use Revert or close an unsaved
buffer as a substitute for inspecting it.

   The GNU Emacs Manual's *note Undo: (emacs)Undo. section explains
history, selective undo and limits.  For file destinations and save
failures, see *note Save a separate working copy: Saving A Working Copy.
Text undo does not reverse a settings-menu save, a filesystem operation
or an action already sent to a terminal.


File: emacsvox.info,  Node: Copying Through The Clipboard,  Prev: Recovering Text Edits,  Up: Editing Text With Speech

5.6.6 Exchange text with another application
--------------------------------------------

The kill ring is part of Emacs.  The operating-system clipboard can
connect it to other applications when the display session and
configuration support that connection.  Under normal graphical clipboard
integration, ‘M-w’ copies to both, and ‘C-y’ can insert text copied more
recently outside Emacs.  That is why checking the pasted result matters
even after checking the selection.

   Test the boundary with a short, non-confidential sentence in each
direction: copy from Emacs and paste into a disposable external
document, then copy a different sentence externally and paste into an
empty Emacs buffer.  Verify the actual text rather than assuming that
success in one direction proves both.  Native Windows Emacs, WSLg,
terminal Emacs and remote sessions need not share identical clipboard
behaviour.  This guide does not install a bridge or change your
clipboard configuration.

   If copying between Emacs buffers works but the external test fails,
investigate that display/clipboard boundary rather than changing speech
settings.  Do not paste test commands into a live terminal.  For the
general integration and its options, see *note Using the clipboard:
(emacs)Clipboard. and *note Cut and paste with other window
applications: (emacs)Primary Selection. in the GNU Emacs Manual; a
primary selection is not the same as the clipboard.


File: emacsvox.info,  Node: Searching And Replacing Text,  Next: Speech System,  Prev: Editing Text With Speech,  Up: Basic Usage

5.7 Find and replace text carefully
===================================

‘C-s’ searches forward and ‘C-r’ searches backwards in the current
buffer.  Type the query, listen to the match feedback, and repeat the
search key to reach another occurrence.  Reverse direction with the
other search key when you overshoot.  Searching the current text does
not send a web or mail query.

   When you find the occurrence you want, press ‘<RET>’ to end the
search before typing document text.  Forward search leaves point after
the match; backward search leaves it before the match.  Check with ‘C-e
l’ before editing.  With a failing search, ‘C-g’ first backs out of the
failed part; another ‘C-g’ may be needed to abandon the search.  Do not
begin an edit while its prompt is active.

   For details of repetition, search history, case and failure recovery,
use *note Incremental search: (emacs)Incremental Search. in the GNU
Emacs Manual.  Its rules describe the editor; Emacsvox adds spoken match
context and search cues.  The next procedure changes text, so it begins
by establishing a narrower scope.

* Menu:

* Review replacements one at a time: Reviewing Text Replacements.  Limit query replacement to a checked region and verify the changed text.


File: emacsvox.info,  Node: Reviewing Text Replacements,  Up: Searching And Replacing Text

5.7.1 Review replacements one at a time
---------------------------------------

Use a disposable Text-mode buffer for this example.  Insert these three
lines:

     The draft is ready.
     Keep this draft for comparison.
     The draft outside the selection must stay unchanged.

  1. From the beginning, set the mark with ‘C-<SPC>’ and move down two
     lines.  Point is at the third line's beginning.  ‘C-e r’ should
     read only the first two lines.  Keep this region active for the
     next command.
  2. Press ‘M-%’ to start query replacement.  At the first prompt enter
     ‘draft’ and press ‘<RET>’; at the second enter ‘version’ and press
     ‘<RET>’.  These are answers to successive prompts, not text
     inserted into the document.
  3. Emacsvox reads the line containing the proposed match.  Press ‘y’
     to replace the first occurrence.  At the second, press ‘n’ to leave
     it unchanged.
  4. When the command finishes, read all three lines.  Only the first
     should say ‘version’.  The second was skipped and the third was
     outside the selection.

   ‘M-x query-replace <RET>’ is an alternative when Meta-percent is
difficult to enter.  The active region limits the operation under normal
Transient Mark mode; without it, ordinary forward query replacement
works from point towards the accessible buffer end.  Do not deactivate
or replace the checked mark between selection and starting the command.
For a full-document operation, first abandon unrelated selection and
deliberately choose the starting point.

   At a replacement question, ‘q’ or ‘<RET>’ stops without changing
further matches; earlier replacements remain.  Comma, ‘,’, replaces the
current match and lets you review it before advancing.  ‘!’ replaces the
remaining matches without more questions and is not needed for this
guided task.  Other editing keys can leave the replacement loop and then
act on the document.  Rely on the automatic match reading, or stop
before issuing ordinary speech/editing keys.

   Check the result before saving.  Use *note Recover a text edit before
saving: Recovering Text Edits. if the scope or replacement was wrong.
*note Replacement commands: (emacs)Replace. and *note Query replace:
(emacs)Query Replace. in the GNU Emacs Manual cover exact matching, case
behaviour and the full set of answers.  Regular-expression replacement
is a different, more specialised task.


File: emacsvox.info,  Node: Speech System,  Next: Voice-lock,  Prev: Searching And Replacing Text,  Up: Basic Usage

5.8 Adjust Speech While You Work
================================

Use ‘C-e H s’ for rate, punctuation, indentation, capitalisation, and
typing feedback.  It starts with the captured buffer's settings; ‘g’
switches scope, and ‘C-u C-e H’ starts the menu with global defaults.
Individual controls retain their documented exceptions, such as global
volume or phonetic delay.

   Changing a global default is not necessarily saving it for restart.
A menu's Save action records only its documented values, and local or
mode settings can still override defaults.  *note Choose What Persists:
(emacsvox)Saving And Startup. explains those distinctions.  The direct
keys below are alternatives to menu navigation; use ‘C-h k’ to check a
command's global prefix form before using it.

* Menu:

* Choose Typing Echo: Controlling Echo.  Choose character, word, and line feedback while typing.
* Adjust Rate And Spoken Detail: Speech Output Control.  Adjust rate and how textual detail is presented.
* Stop Or Restart Speech: Miscellaneous.  Stop, silence, restart, or identify the speech server.


File: emacsvox.info,  Node: Controlling Echo,  Next: Speech Output Control,  Up: Speech System

5.8.1 Choose Typing Echo
------------------------

Character echo reads each typed character, word echo each completed
word, and optional line echo a completed line.  They can be toggled
independently.  Use ‘C-e H s t’ for explained controls, or the keys
below.  These direct commands affect the current buffer; a preceding
‘C-u’ changes the corresponding default and applies it to this buffer.

‘C-e d k’

     ‘emacsvox-toggle-character-echo’

     Toggle character echo.
‘C-e d w’

     ‘emacsvox-toggle-word-echo’

     Toggle word echo.
‘C-e d l’

     ‘emacsvox-toggle-line-echo’

     Toggle line echo.


File: emacsvox.info,  Node: Speech Output Control,  Next: Miscellaneous,  Prev: Controlling Echo,  Up: Speech System

5.8.2 Adjust Rate And Spoken Detail
-----------------------------------

Start with rate and punctuation, then adjust other feedback when the
distinction helps the task.  Familiar prose may suit faster speech;
exact code inspection may need full punctuation.  Direct commands affect
the current buffer unless their Help specifies a global prefix form.

‘C-e d r’
     Enter a rate with ‘tts-set-rate’.  The menu alternative is _Rate_
     in ‘C-e H s’.
‘C-e d DIGIT’
     Choose one of ten predefined rates with ‘0’ through ‘9’.
‘C-e d p’
     Choose ‘some’, ‘all’, or ‘none’ with ‘tts-set-punctuations’.  A
     supporting host also offers named profiles, described below.
‘C-e d s’
     Toggle splitting of mixed-capital identifiers into words.
‘C-e d c / C-e d C’
     Toggle capitalisation indication with the lowercase key.  The
     uppercase key chooses and previews spoken, tone, combined,
     personal-rule, or no indication.  ‘C-e H s c’ opens the
     Capitalization menu.
‘C-e d i / C-e d I’
     Toggle indentation feedback with the lowercase key, or open its
     menu with the uppercase key.  ‘C-e H s i’ reaches the same menu
     through settings.
‘C-e d T’
     Choose and preview _Alongside speech_ (default) or _Before speech_
     for indentation tones.  The current buffer changes; a prefix also
     updates the default.  Save ‘emacsvox-indentation-tone-timing’
     through Customize for restart.  Timing does not choose a
     spoken/tonal presentation or enable indentation.  Backends without
     overlay support play tones before speech for either setting.

  1. Use The Indentation Menu

     Open ‘C-e H s i’ or ‘C-e d I’.  Select spoken columns with ‘s’,
     pitch tones with ‘p’, or duration tones with ‘d’; uppercase ‘P~/~D’
     combines speech and tones.  ‘b’ places tones before speech and ‘a’
     alongside.  Timing choices are unavailable for presentations
     without built-in tones.

     ‘g’ switches _Current buffer_ and _Global defaults_.  Global
     defaults leave existing local choices intact.  A prefix starts the
     menu in global scope; ‘e’ toggles indentation feedback in the
     displayed scope.

     Up/down selects a control and left/right changes sections.
     Shift-left/right on _Volume_ changes by five, bounded to 0–100.
     Other items give guidance rather than changing volume.  ‘<RET>’
     activates an item.  These bindings apply inside the menu, not in
     prompts or the source buffer.

     ‘v’ or _Volume_ accepts an exact value; ‘-~/~+’ remain shortcuts.
     Volume affects all main-stream Omnivox tones even in buffer scope.
     Adjustments speak the percentage and interrupt obsolete main
     speech, so repeated keys announce the latest value rather than
     queueing old values.

     Changes apply immediately.  ‘<SPC>’ previews four-column
     indentation even when feedback is off.  ‘q’ leaves changes in
     effect.  In global scope, ‘S’ saves indentation defaults and tone
     volume; closing without saving keeps defaults changed only for this
     session.  ‘M-x emacsvox-set-indentation-presentation’ remains a
     direct alternative.

     The following punctuation sections are reference material for
     changing individual pronunciations.  They are not prerequisites for
     ordinary reading.  Reusable palettes/profiles, engine-specific
     tuning, and the wider output model belong to *note Aural
     Presentation: Aural Presentation.  Specialist character-rate and
     chunking controls are also available through live Help and the
     generated reference.

  2. Edit Punctuation Pronunciations

     A punctuation level decides how much punctuation is spoken; the
     pronunciation editor changes how particular characters are read.
     Open it with ‘C-e H s p e’ or ‘M-x omnivox-punctuation’.

     This interface needs the bundled local launcher and the host's
     punctuation-editor capability.  Older hosts report that an update
     is required; remote and native-direct configuration editing are not
     provided here.  It displays saved host settings and an unsaved
     draft separately.  Running speech can still use an earlier
     configuration.

     Rows identify Unicode characters, pronunciations, and
     default/override origin.  The initial level is _some_.

     ‘l’
          Choose none, some, or all, each with independent overrides;
          supporting hosts also offer named profiles.
     ‘RET’
          Speak a name, preserve the character, or restore its shipped
          default.
     ‘a’
          Add a character not already listed.
     ‘d’
          Restore the selected character's default in this level.
     ‘s’
          Save drafts on the host without restarting speech or altering
          unrelated configuration.
     ‘r’
          Restart this session's main and notification workers with
          saved settings after confirmation; save or discard drafts
          first.
     ‘g’
          Reread the saved file, asking before discarding edits.
     ‘q’
          Return while retaining the draft; reopening the editor resumes
          it.  ‘C-e H p d’ also resumes retained drafts.
     ‘?’
          Read Help, including the selected host configuration path.

     _Preserve character_ leaves pronunciation and pauses to the engine;
     it does not promise silence.  Org normally uses _some_.  To name
     apostrophes, set both _APOSTROPHE_ U+0027 and _RIGHT SINGLE
     QUOTATION MARK_ U+2019 to speak “apostrophe” in _some_.  That
     includes contractions.  Omnivox 1.15.0 names straight, curly, and
     modifier apostrophes at _all_ without a configuration file.

       1. Save And Activate Changes

          Saving rejects a file or target changed since review and
          retains the draft for reconciliation.  Other configuration is
          preserved.  A named-profile-capable host writes Omnivox
          configuration version 4, unreadable by 1.16.0 and earlier.
          The older built-in-only editor writes version 3, requiring
          1.15.0 or newer.  Do not save a shared file with a format
          another selected executable cannot read.  Capability and
          configuration-format checks matter even when the main
          installation uses a newer supported release.

          The editor's ‘s’ saves pronunciation data; its ‘r’ activates
          through worker restart.  By contrast, ‘S’ in the parent
          Punctuation menu saves the selected level/profile through
          Customize, not the host editor's draft.

       2. Work With Named Profiles

          With host support, ‘N’ creates a profile inheriting ‘none’,
          ‘some’, or ‘all’.  Use a lowercase ID such as ‘prose’ or
          ‘proofreading’.  ‘l’ switches levels and profiles; ‘X’ removes
          the selected profile from the draft.  Profiles inherit
          base-level edits and override individual characters.  ‘s’
          saves all drafts without changing speech; ‘r’ loads the same
          definitions into both workers.

          ‘tts-set-punctuations’ offers profiles whose definitions agree
          in current workers.  Explicit selection applies to the buffer.
          A preference in ‘tts-punctuation-mode-policy-alist’ can use
          ‘(text-mode profile "prose" some)’, retaining ‘some’ as
          fallback for older hosts, missing profiles, or inconsistent
          worker definitions.  Status identifies fallback use.
          ‘tts-reset-punctuation-mode’ restores mode policy.  Explicitly
          choosing a built-in level overrides a profile.  Character
          inspection is independent, and there is no built-in ‘most’
          level.


File: emacsvox.info,  Node: Miscellaneous,  Prev: Speech Output Control,  Up: Speech System

5.8.3 Stop Or Restart Speech
----------------------------

Stopping ends current output without disabling future feedback.  Quiet
mode lets you work temporarily without speech.  Restart after correcting
a backend problem or changing the selected synthesiser, not as an
assumed cure for every missing integration.

‘C-e s’
     Stop current main and notification speech with ‘tts-stop’.
‘C-e d q’
     Toggle current-buffer quiet mode; ‘C-u’ toggles it globally.
‘C-e C-s’
     Stop and restart the selected speech server.
‘C-e d V’
     Speak adapter and version information.

   Server selection, repeated-pattern cleanup, text splitting, language
selection, and direct engine controls have specialist Help and reference
entries.  For missing speech, follow *note Recovering From No Speech:
(emacsvox)Recovering From No Speech.


File: emacsvox.info,  Node: Voice-lock,  Next: Status Information,  Prev: Speech System,  Up: Basic Usage

5.9 Hear Structure With Voice Lock
==================================

Voice Lock starts automatically in supported buffers.  It maps faces and
structural text properties to personalities, making comments, strings,
headings, links, and other text sound distinct without extra words.

   Compare two styled elements in code, Org, Info, or EWW.  Toggle ‘C-e
d v’ locally, read the same elements, then toggle again to restore.
‘C-e d C-v’ changes Voice Lock globally.  Disabling it removes that
layer of voices, not ordinary speech, semantic feedback, tones, or
icons.

   The result depends on engine and presentation settings.  Voice Lock,
explicit face rules, and semantic rules are separate; see *note Voice
Lock: Voice Lock. in Aural Presentation.  Reusable voice, sound, and
profile choices belong to that chapter rather than to a Voice Lock
toggle.


File: emacsvox.info,  Node: Status Information,  Prev: Voice-lock,  Up: Basic Usage

5.10 Find Out Where You Are
===========================

These commands report context without moving point: the last message,
current buffer/modes, or position.

‘C-e a’

     ‘emacsvox-speak-message-again’

     Repeat the last Emacs message and copy it to the kill ring.  This
     can change what the next yank inserts.
‘C-e m’

     ‘emacsvox-speak-mode-line’

     Speak the mode line, including buffer name and major mode.
‘C-e f’

     ‘emacsvox-speak-buffer-filename’

     Speak the file name, or default directory when no file is visited,
     and copy that location to the kill ring.  Inspect it before copying
     text for a transfer.
‘C-e C-l’

     ‘what-line’

     Report the line number and position in the buffer.
‘C-e =’

     ‘emacsvox-speak-current-column’

     Speak the current column.
‘C-e %’

     ‘emacsvox-speak-current-percentage’

     Announce the percentage into the buffer.


File: emacsvox.info,  Node: Using Online Help,  Next: Audio Desktop,  Prev: Basic Usage,  Up: Top

6 Find Help While You Work
**************************

Emacs can explain the key you are about to use, the modes active in a
buffer, and the commands and settings available in your session.  This
live help reflects installed packages and personal customisations.  Use
it to understand an unfamiliar command or resolve a difference between a
manual and your running Emacs.

   Most Emacs help commands begin with ‘C-h’.  Press ‘C-h C-h’ for
‘help-for-help’ when you need to discover what can follow that prefix.
Settings menus also have their own contextual Help, described below.

6.1 Ask About A Key, Command, Or Setting
========================================

Begin in the buffer whose commands or settings you want to inspect.
Help can select a different buffer, so return to your working buffer
before asking about another local binding or value.  Choose according to
what you know:

   • ‘C-h k’ followed by a key runs ‘describe-key’ and explains the
     command currently bound to that key.
   • ‘C-h f’ runs ‘describe-function’.  Enter a command or function name
     to read its documentation.
   • ‘C-h v’ runs ‘describe-variable’.  Enter an option name to inspect
     its current value and documentation.
   • ‘C-h w’ runs ‘where-is’ and reports the keys that invoke a command.

   For example, ‘C-h k C-e l’ explains the current-line speaking key
without running that command.  If you know its command name but not its
key, use ‘C-h w’ and enter the name instead.

6.2 Discover What The Current Buffer Can Do
===========================================

Start with ‘C-h m’ after opening Dired, EWW, mail, a terminal, or
another application.  This runs ‘describe-mode’ and explains the major
mode, active minor modes, and their local commands.  Use ‘C-h b’
(‘describe-bindings’) when you need the effective key bindings after
package maps and personal customisations have been applied.

   For Emacsvox settings that apply to the current task, press ‘C-e S’.
It opens the integration's focused menu when one is available, otherwise
general buffer settings.  ‘C-e H .’ always opens general buffer
settings.  The menu captures the buffer on entry, so its controls
continue to refer to that task while you navigate its menus and editors.

6.2.1 Find a setting and read its menu Help
-------------------------------------------

Press ‘C-e H /’ to find a setting by part of its name.  The search is
case-insensitive and includes supported aliases.  For example, ‘cap’
finds capitalisation controls.  Space inserts a literal space, so you
can type a multiword query such as ‘all caps beep’; ‘<TAB>’ completes
it.  Completion lets you choose a result; accepting it opens the
appropriate menu and selects the control without changing its value.
Press ‘<RET>’ again to activate or edit it.  Cancelling completion
leaves the originating menu open.

   The main menu groups its entries in this order: _Everyday settings_,
_This buffer and item_, _Rules and saved work_, _Find and get help_, and
_Finish_.  Use left/right to reach a group, then up/down to select an
item.  Changing sections selects the first available item.  Plain arrows
navigate; Shift-left/right adjusts a selected numeric control where
offered.  Press ‘C-e H n’ to choose navigation and announcement
preferences; the same controls remain available through ‘C-e H s n’.
‘<RET>’, ‘M-<RET>’, or ‘C-j’ activates the selected item; its displayed
shortcut also works directly.

   To hear the selected item again, press ‘C-c l’.  This is especially
useful after a section-only announcement: the first item is selected
even though only the heading was spoken.  ‘M-f~/~M-b’ reviews its words
and ‘C-f~/~C-b’ its characters, leaving the selection and Enter's target
unchanged.  These menu keys differ from ordinary buffer reading; see
*note Review an item without changing the selection:
settings-menu-review. for endpoints, repeats and menu-specific
overrides.

   Press ‘?’ followed by an item's key to hear its effect and scope, or
‘? ?’ for the menu overview and available review keys.  The overview is
a read-only snapshot with ordinary text navigation.  Its displayed
actions run after you return to the menu.  ‘q’ in Help returns with the
selection retained.

   Within the menu itself, ‘q’ or ‘C-g’ goes back one level, while ‘C-q’
closes the sequence.  Returning from an Emacsvox submenu selects the
entry that opened it, including when you used its shortcut.  The return
announcement identifies that item; ‘<RET>’ opens it again.  These
navigation commands do not undo changes already applied.  Choice lists
and minibuffer prompts have different roles: arrows browse a choice list
without applying it, while a prompt uses your normal text editing and
completion.  See *note Navigate menus and read Help:
settings-menu-navigation. for the complete interaction.  A linked editor
keeps its own preview, saving, and discard rules.  See *note Choose What
Persists: (emacsvox)Saving And Startup. before treating a saved default,
a live adjustment, or a retained draft as the same thing.

   Use ‘C-e H a’ to find an action such as browsing voices, inspecting
diagnostics, or reading the manual.  It uses the same partial-name
matching and two-Enter selection as settings search, including literal
spaces and ‘<TAB>’ completion.  Alternative names lead to one displayed
result for each action: for example, ‘Feedback for this item’ finds
_Explain this item_, and ‘Recent aural feedback’ finds _Recent
feedback_.  Editors opened from these menus retain their own Help,
preview, save, and return commands.

6.3 Read And Leave A Help Window
================================

Ordinary Emacs Help writes its explanation in ‘*Help*’.  Some commands,
such as ‘C-h m’, can display that buffer without selecting it.  Spoken
descriptions such as key, function and variable Help use
‘emacsvox-speak-help’, also bound to ‘C-e h’.  This helper selects Help,
removes other windows in the frame, and reads a windowful starting at
Help's current point.  It does not kill the buffers those windows
displayed, and it does not necessarily read all of Help.

   After requesting Help, press ‘C-e m’ to check the selected buffer.
If it is ‘*Help*’, read there directly.  Otherwise, use ‘C-x b’ and
choose ‘*Help*’ by name, or select its existing window deliberately.  Do
not automatically press ‘C-x o’: Help may already be selected, or there
may now be only one window.  ‘C-e C-w’ describes the arrangement when
needed.

   Once in Help, use line or paragraph reading for a local check.  ‘C-e
h’ repeats a windowful from the current Help position; ‘M-<’ first
returns to its beginning.  For longer reading and its effect on point,
see *note Reading and navigating: (emacsvox)Reading.  Press ‘q’ while in
Help to leave its view.  Then check the selected buffer and use ‘C-x b’
to return to your document before editing or requesting buffer-specific
Help again.  To restore a reference window, follow *note Keep a
reference and a draft in separate windows: (emacsvox)Reference And Draft
Windows.

   Help reached through an Emacsvox settings menu has the menu-return
behaviour explained above.  Use its ‘q’ to return to the originating
menu item, not the ordinary document-return procedure.  Dedicated
editors likewise keep their own Help and return commands.

6.4 Learn Interactively And Read Manuals
========================================

‘C-h t’ opens the interactive Emacs tutorial.  Emacsvox speaks its text
and your navigation as you work through it.  For a separate key-learning
exercise, ‘C-h C-l’ runs ‘emacsvox-learn-emacs’: it speaks the function
of each key you press.  Leave that loop with ‘C-g’.

   Press ‘C-e H i’ to read the offline Emacsvox manual from the settings
menu.  It opens at the top in Info, plays the opening cue, and reads the
first screen or the whole node according to your Info speech setting.
Info's navigation and index lookup remain available.  Press ‘q’ to
return to the menu with _Read the manual_ selected and the same source
buffer and settings scope.  ‘C-e C-i’ opens the manual directly.

   ‘C-h i’ opens Info's general manual reader.  Press ‘d’ for the Info
directory, then ‘m’ and enter ‘Emacsvox’ to choose this manual.  *note
Using this manual: Using This Manual. provides an exercise in index
lookup, following a link, and returning to your work.  *note Find a
task: Finding Tasks. starts from an everyday goal, and *note Terms used
in this manual: Emacsvox Terminology. explains the names used in the
procedures.  The *note Info manual: (info)Top. covers the reader's
complete navigation and search commands.

   In an Aural interface, ‘C-c C-i’ opens the relevant manual topic and
‘q’ in Info returns to that interface.  In the interface's own Help, ‘i’
opens the same topic.

6.5 Emacsvox-Specific Help
==========================

Use ‘C-h C-e’ (‘emacsvox-describe-emacsvox’) for the Emacsvox overview.
‘C-e N’ (‘emacsvox-view-emacsvox-news’) opens project news, and ‘C-e T’
(‘emacsvox-view-emacsvox-tips’) opens productivity tips.

   For unexpected feedback, leave point on the item and press ‘C-e E’ to
explain its voices, sounds, and other presentation actions.  ‘C-e H x’
opens Feedback Details through the settings menu.  Its report
distinguishes retained output from a simulation; see *note Understand
Why Something Sounded That Way: (emacsvox)Explaining Presentation.

   ‘C-e H D’ opens diagnostics.  Use ‘d’ there for Aural Doctor, ‘s’ for
spatial capabilities, or ‘m’ and ‘n’ for retained main and notification
diagnostics.  These inspection routes do not imply that speech has been
repaired.  Run a repair or restart only when you deliberately choose
that action.  If there is no speech at all, begin with *note Recovering
From No Speech: (emacsvox)Recovering From No Speech. rather than
presentation settings.

   The generated *note Emacsvox Keymaps: (emacsvox-reference)Emacsvox
Keymaps. in the _Emacsvox Reference Manual_ records default bindings.
Return to ‘C-h k’, ‘C-h m’, and ‘C-h b’ when a package or personal
configuration changes those defaults.


File: emacsvox.info,  Node: Audio Desktop,  Next: Aural Presentation,  Prev: Using Online Help,  Up: Top

7 Work With The Audio Desktop
*****************************

Emacs uses buffers for documents, application views, search results, and
other work.  Each buffer has modes that understand its content.
Emacsvox uses that information to present text and state changes through
speech, voices, sounds, and structured navigation.  Together, these form
the _audio desktop_: the same Emacs environment, experienced through its
commands and auditory feedback rather than through a separate graphical
desktop.

   A useful habit is to press ‘C-e m’ to hear the current buffer and
mode, then ‘C-h m’ to discover the commands available there.  This
approach works in files, Web pages, mail, shells, and other Emacs
applications.

* Menu:

* Buffers hold your work: Desktop Objects.  Understand and move between Emacs buffers.
* Work with windows without losing your place: Working With Windows.  Keep several views available while knowing where commands and edits will act.
* Let modes give each buffer its tools: Object Oriented Desktop.  Let major and minor modes interpret each kind of content.
* Hear and navigate the current context: Context-Sensitive Interaction.  Use aural structure, mode commands, buffers, and search.


File: emacsvox.info,  Node: Desktop Objects,  Next: Working With Windows,  Up: Audio Desktop

7.1 Buffers Hold Your Work
==========================

A buffer holds the content or application view you are working with.  It
might contain a file, an Info manual, a Dired listing, a Web page, mail
search results, a shell session, or Help text.  Some buffers visit
files; others exist only within the Emacs session.

   The mode line identifies the selected buffer and its major mode.
Press ‘C-e m’ to hear it.  These are the main ways to change buffers:

‘C-x b’
     Switch to another buffer by name.  Completion lets you narrow the
     choices instead of remembering the complete name.

‘C-x C-b’
     Open a buffer list.  In that list, use ‘C-h m’ to learn its
     navigation, selection, saving, and cleanup commands.

‘C-x k’
     Close a buffer that you no longer need.  Emacs asks before
     discarding unsaved file changes.

   Switching buffers does not close the one you left.  You can work on a
document, consult Help or a reference, visit a shell, and return to the
document without reopening it.

7.1.1 Cycle Between Buffers of the Same Kind
--------------------------------------------

When several buffers use the same major mode, you can cycle among them
without recalling their names.  This is useful for comparing Org notes,
visiting several Dired directories, or moving between source files.

   Press ‘C-e n’ to select the next buffer with the same major mode, or
‘C-e p’ to select the previous one.  For example, from an Org buffer
these commands visit other Org buffers, skipping all buffers that do not
use the same major mode.  Buffers are grouped by major mode, not by
project or directory.

   After the first command, you only need the final letter: ‘n’
continues forward and ‘p’ moves backward.  You can change direction at
any time, and cycling wraps around when you reach the end.  For example,
‘C-e n n p’ moves forward twice, then returns to the buffer you just
left.

   To navigate without the major-mode filter, use ‘C-e .’ for next
buffer or ‘C-e ,’ for previous buffer.  You can then repeat with just
‘.’ or ‘,’, in the same way as ‘n’ and ‘p’.  You can also switch between
these controls while cycling.

   The temporary buffer-selection controls also include ‘b’ to select a
buffer by name, ‘f’ to open a file, ‘k’ to kill a buffer, and ‘o’ to
change windows.

   Press ‘C-g’ when finished to return to normal editing.  A key outside
the temporary buffer-selection controls also ends cycling and performs
its normal action.  To choose a particular buffer by name, use ‘C-x b’.


File: emacsvox.info,  Node: Working With Windows,  Next: Object Oriented Desktop,  Prev: Desktop Objects,  Up: Audio Desktop

7.2 Work with windows without losing your place
===============================================

A buffer contains your work; a window is a view of a buffer.  Emacs
calls its outer application window a _frame_.  A frame can contain
several Emacs windows, but only one is selected for ordinary commands.
You can work with those views by name and spoken context without needing
to picture their arrangement.

   Switching buffers with ‘C-x b’ changes the selected window's
contents.  Selecting another window with ‘C-x o’ moves to that view
instead.  The final character is lowercase o, for "other", not the digit
zero.  Emacsvox announces the selected buffer and mode; ‘C-e m’ repeats
that context and ‘C-e l’ checks the current line.  ‘C-e C-w’ describes
the window arrangement.

   Two windows can show the same buffer at different positions.  They
are not independent document copies: edits in either change the same
text, and the buffer has one mark.  For two independent documents,
select different buffers.  The GNU Emacs Manual's *note Multiple
windows: (emacs)Windows. section explains this model in detail.

* Menu:

* Keep a reference and a draft in separate windows: Reference And Draft Windows.  Consult a reference, copy a passage and return to the intended draft.
* Close a window without closing the document: Closing Windows Safely.  Simplify the layout without killing buffers or discarding unsaved text.


File: emacsvox.info,  Node: Reference And Draft Windows,  Next: Closing Windows Safely,  Up: Working With Windows

7.2.1 Keep a reference and a draft in separate windows
------------------------------------------------------

Begin with an editable draft selected and a reference buffer already
open.  Finish prompts and leave settings editors first.  For a safe
first attempt, use the source and notes buffers from *note Copy a
passage into another buffer: Copying Text Between Buffers.  Keep their
names available so that you can recover by buffer name at any time.

  1. In the draft, press ‘C-x 4 b’, enter the reference buffer's name,
     and press ‘<RET>’.  The digit 4 is a separate keypress.  Emacs
     displays and selects the reference in another window, creating or
     reusing a view according to its display configuration.  ‘C-x 4 f’
     does the equivalent for a file pathname.
  2. Check the selected buffer with ‘C-e m’ and read a line or
     paragraph.  Use short inspections when you intend to retain point.
     Tracked reading with ‘C-e i’ moves the reference's reading position
     as explained in *note Read from point, stop, and start again:
     Reading From Point.
  3. Press ‘C-x o’ to select the next window and identify it before
     typing.  With two ordinary windows this usually alternates between
     reference and draft.  With more windows, repeat until the intended
     draft is selected; do not assume the next view must be the draft.
  4. To transfer text, return to the reference, select and check a short
     passage, then copy with ‘M-w’.  Use ‘C-x o’ to return to the draft,
     confirm its buffer and insertion point, and press ‘C-y’.  The
     reference text remains unchanged.  Follow *note Copy a passage into
     another buffer: Copying Text Between Buffers. for kill-ring and
     newline checks.
  5. Return to the reference with ‘C-x o’.  Its position is where you
     last left it, including movement made while selecting or reading.
     Inspect it rather than assuming copying restored an earlier reading
     position.

   If window selection becomes confusing, ‘C-x b’ and a known buffer
name is always a useful recovery route from ordinary work.  It can
replace the current view rather than restore the old layout; the
buffer's text remains available.

   Some speech commands intentionally change the layout.  ‘C-e h’
selects and reads Help using a windowful-reading helper, which removes
other windows.  Whole-buffer and region reading can use that helper for
a large request too.  A lost split therefore does not necessarily mean
the reference was closed.  Reopen the desired view with ‘C-x 4 b’.  For
a short inspection without that helper, use line or paragraph reading as
described under *note Read a short passage without moving point: Reading
Without Moving Point.

   For the general display and selection commands, see *note Displaying
in another window: (emacs)Pop Up Window. and *note Using other windows:
(emacs)Other Window. in the GNU Emacs Manual.  Emacsvox adds speech to
the selection; it does not make the ordinary display rules identical in
every setup.


File: emacsvox.info,  Node: Closing Windows Safely,  Prev: Reference And Draft Windows,  Up: Working With Windows

7.2.2 Close a window without closing the document
-------------------------------------------------

Finish active prompts, then select the view you want to remove and check
its buffer with ‘C-e m’.  Press ‘C-x 0’: Control-x, then the digit zero.
This removes the selected window, not its buffer or file.  Select the
buffer again with ‘C-x b’ when you need it.

   To keep only the selected view in the frame, use ‘C-x 1’ instead.
The other buffers remain available and unsaved text remains unsaved.
These layout commands are neither file saving nor undo.  If Emacs cannot
remove the last ordinary window or a specially managed view, follow its
explanation rather than closing Emacs to force the layout change.

   ‘C-x k’ has a different purpose: it kills a buffer, with any
applicable unsaved-work prompt.  ‘C-x 4 0’ kills the buffer as well as
its window; it is not a longer spelling of ‘C-x 0’.  Likewise, ‘q’
belongs to the current mode: in a plain document it usually inserts a
letter, while Help, Info and Dired have their own return commands.

   See *note Deleting and rearranging windows: (emacs)Change Window. in
the GNU Emacs Manual for the full set of layout operations.  Keep *note
Buffers hold your work: Desktop Objects. as the reference for buffer
selection and closing work itself.


File: emacsvox.info,  Node: Object Oriented Desktop,  Next: Context-Sensitive Interaction,  Prev: Working With Windows,  Up: Audio Desktop

7.3 Let Modes Give Each Buffer Its Tools
========================================

Each buffer has one major mode responsible for its principal editing or
application commands.  Programming-language modes work with source code,
Dired with directories, EWW with Web pages, and Org with structured
notes.  Minor modes add optional behaviour without replacing the major
mode.

   The active mode determines which structures Emacs can navigate.  In
prose you might move by sentence; in Org by heading; in a list by item.
Emacsvox speaks the result and can announce relevant state changes.  To
discover the mode's commands, press ‘C-h m’, then ‘C-h k’ followed by a
key for more detail.  *note Using Online Help: Using Online Help.
explains these help commands.


File: emacsvox.info,  Node: Context-Sensitive Interaction,  Prev: Object Oriented Desktop,  Up: Audio Desktop

7.4 Hear And Navigate The Current Context
=========================================

Because Emacsvox has access to Emacs modes and application state, it can
announce the kind of object you have reached rather than treating
everything as undifferentiated text.  Use the active mode's navigation
for the structure you are working with, and request more spoken detail
as needed.

7.4.1 Hear Structure Without Extra Words
----------------------------------------

Voice changes can distinguish headings, comments, strings, links,
warnings, and other meaningful text.  Short sounds and tones can mark
events or state changes without interrupting the spoken content.
Ordinary text remains available through the usual reading commands.

   The exact result depends on the mode, the Emacsvox integration, and
your Aural Presentation settings.  *note Aural Presentation: Aural
Presentation. explains how the parts fit together, including the
established Voice Lock compatibility layer; ‘C-e E’ explains the
presentation chosen for the object at point.

7.4.2 Move By The Structure Of The Content
------------------------------------------

Prefer the navigation supplied by the active mode when it matches your
task.  For example, Info moves among nodes and menu entries, Org and
Outline move among headings, Dired moves among files, programming modes
understand definitions and diagnostics, and EWW exposes links and
document structure.

   The mode's Help, available through ‘C-h m’, describes its navigation
commands, including those supplied by installed packages and your
customisations.  *note Browsing Structure: Browsing Structure. points to
guides for common kinds of structured content.

7.4.3 Move Between Tasks Without Losing Your Place
--------------------------------------------------

Use ‘C-x b’ to return to a known buffer or ‘C-x C-b’ to browse the
current set.  For a reference and draft displayed together, follow *note
Keep a reference and a draft in separate windows: Reference And Draft
Windows.  Select the intended view and inspect its buffer before
editing; ‘C-e C-w’ describes the arrangement.

   Applications often create several related buffers, such as a search
result and an individual message.  Their mode help explains the commands
for moving among those views.  Treat each buffer as a place you can
revisit, rather than as a dialog that must be completed before doing
anything else.

7.4.4 Search Wherever You Are
-----------------------------

In most text buffers, ‘C-s’ searches forward and ‘C-r’ backwards.  Type
the query, repeat the key for another match, and finish with ‘<RET>’
before typing an edit.  In a failing search, ‘C-g’ first backs out of
the failed part; a second cancellation may be needed to leave it.  This
search works in documents, Help, source code and many application
results.  *note Find and replace text carefully: Searching And Replacing
Text. explains match positions and scoped replacement; *note Incremental
search: (emacs)Incremental Search. in the GNU Emacs Manual provides the
full search reference.

   Also look for the mode's semantic search and filtering commands.  A
mail application can search message fields, Dired can narrow files, and
a browser can move directly among links or headings.  Use the general
search when text is what matters and the mode-specific command when the
kind of object matters.


File: emacsvox.info,  Node: Aural Presentation,  Next: Emacs Packages,  Prev: Audio Desktop,  Up: Top

8 Aural Presentation: Speech, Voices, And Sound
***********************************************

A heading, an unread message, and a warning need not sound alike.  Aural
Presentation lets Emacsvox express those differences through speech,
voices, short sounds, tones, pauses, and spatial cues.  The application
identifies what happened; presentation rules choose the feedback.  The
speech server then produces it with the voices and output capabilities
on your machine.

   Return to the item whose feedback you want to understand and leave
point on it.  Press ‘C-e E’ to explain that item, ‘C-e H’ for the
settings menu, or ‘C-e S’ for the current task's focused settings where
available.  The workflows below use these inspection and editing tools
rather than hand-edited configuration files.  *note Read the key
instructions: Emacs Key Notation. explains how to enter the sequences.

   *note Choose the change you need: Choosing A Presentation Change.
helps you pick the right tool.  To listen without making changes, start
with *note the voice-preview exercise: Previewing An Installed Voice.
The rest of the chapter explains feedback rules, voices, engines,
sounds, output and saved setups.  Each procedure includes its own menu
route.  Action search at ‘C-e H a’ can also select the relevant control
before you activate it.

   When something is wrong, go directly to *note Troubleshooting and
recovery: Presentation Troubleshooting.  In an Aural interface, ‘C-c
C-i’ opens its manual topic; ‘q’ in Info returns to that interface.  In
the interface's Help, ‘i’ opens the same topic.

* Menu:

* Choose the change you need: Choosing A Presentation Change.  Choose between a voice edit, a matching rule, an engine preference, and output settings.
* Getting started with settings menus: Aural Home.  Try a voice, navigate settings menus, and return from editors.
* How aural presentation works: Presentation Model.  Follow meaning from an Emacs application to audible output.
* Understand why something sounded that way: Explaining Presentation.  Explain current or recently queued feedback.
* Change feedback and manage rules: Adjusting Presentation.  Preview, scope, save, and undo presentation changes.
* Choose And Manage Voice Palettes: Voices And Routing.  Understand named voices, standard inheritance, and personal palettes.
* Edit A Named Voice: Editing Named Voices.  Adjust a voice, compare it, and save the complete result.
* Name Occasional Emoji In Speech: Emoji Speech Names.  Keep occasional decoration from forcing a different speech engine.
* Browse Installed Voices And Choose Engines: Installed Voices And Engines.  Find physical voices and configure shared engine preferences.
* Sounds And Space::             Audition sound packs and inspect spatial fallback.
* Speech Output And Notifications: Aural Speech Output.  Set speech rate and understand foreground and background output.
* Save, switch and share your setup: Presentation Profiles.  Save selected settings and distinguish profiles from resource backups.
* Troubleshooting and recovery: Presentation Troubleshooting.  Trace unexpected output and use safe diagnostics.
* Voice Lock and advanced configuration: Voice Lock.  Understand the established face and personality voice adapter.


File: emacsvox.info,  Node: Choosing A Presentation Change,  Next: Aural Home,  Up: Aural Presentation

8.1 Choose the change you need
==============================

Start with the result you want, not with the name of an editor.  A voice
edit changes how a shared voice sounds.  A rule changes which items
receive that voice or other feedback.  Output settings control delivery.
The distinctions below help avoid making a wider change than intended.

8.1.1 Change ordinary reading
-----------------------------

Edit the active palette's ‘default’ named voice to change normal text
and labels that do not request an explicit voice.  Other entries, such
as the voice used for headings, keep their own settings.  Choose this
route for a more comfortable everyday reading voice, not for one
exceptional heading.  See *note Edit a named voice: Editing Named
Voices.

8.1.2 Change one kind of item or one context
--------------------------------------------

Use a presentation rule when the change belongs to matching headings,
prompts, message fields or events.  Inspect a representative item,
choose the criteria, and decide how long the rule should last.  A rule
for the current buffer can match several items there; it is not
automatically limited to the one under point.  The *note heading
exercise: Heading Voice Walkthrough. is a reversible starting point.

   Use *note voice mapping: Choosing A Voice For An Item. to choose a
different named voice for those items.  Use *note a guided feedback
change: Guided Feedback Changes. to add, remove or alter a voice, sound,
tone or spoken label.  Neither operation requires retuning the shared
named voice.

8.1.3 Retune a voice wherever it is used
----------------------------------------

Edit a named voice when its other uses in the palette should change too.
For example, adjusting ‘bolden’ affects items that resolve to that
entry, not only the item from which the editor was opened.  Inspect its
known uses before saving.  For a change limited to the original context,
use a rule instead.  See *note Edit a named voice: Editing Named Voices.
and *note voice adjustments in context: Voices In Context.

8.1.4 Prefer one physical voice throughout a palette
----------------------------------------------------

Use *note the palette-wide review: Changing A Palette Physical Voice.
when you want a physical voice to be preferred across all its effective
named entries while retaining their different adjustments.  Review the
proposed result for several entries before saving.  This is a one-time
edit, not a policy automatically inherited by future names.  It is
broader than editing ‘default’.

8.1.5 Change how an engine is chosen
------------------------------------

Use *note engine preference: Speech Engine Preferences. when you want to
change the shared engine order.  It does not override explicit physical
choices already stored in named voices.  If one item still uses another
engine, inspect that voice's physical choices before changing the engine
order again.  *note Voice availability and activation: Installed Voices
And Engines. are separate from preference: an installed or allowed voice
is not necessarily ready to play.

8.1.6 Change delivery or one unwanted sound
-------------------------------------------

For reading speed or punctuation detail, use *note Speech settings:
Reading Scope And Punctuation.  For the place where notifications play,
use *note notification routing: Notification Routing.; for their
loudness, use *note output volumes: Output Volumes.  These controls do
not decide which text an application speaks automatically.  Its *note
focused speech settings: Integration Speech Settings. control that
behaviour.

   For one unwanted cue, follow *note Silence one cue: Changing One
Sound. instead of disabling all sound.  To change the recordings used by
a family of cues, choose a *note sound pack: Sound Pack Walkthrough.

   After choosing the operation, decide whether to keep a draft, use the
result now or retain it for startup.  *note Choose what persists: Saving
And Startup. explains those choices; the scope shown in a menu does not
make every Save action equivalent.


File: emacsvox.info,  Node: Aural Home,  Next: Presentation Model,  Prev: Choosing A Presentation Change,  Up: Aural Presentation

8.2 Getting Started With Settings Menus
=======================================

* Menu:

* Listen without changing settings: Previewing An Installed Voice.  Hear an available voice and return without saving or changing selection.
* Choose an entry point and preserve your place: Returning From Settings.  Understand the captured source and return through menus and editors.
* Find a setting or an action: Finding Settings And Actions.  Search by name, inspect a control, and activate it deliberately.


File: emacsvox.info,  Node: Previewing An Installed Voice,  Next: Returning From Settings,  Up: Aural Home

8.2.1 Listen without changing settings
--------------------------------------

This exercise previews an installed voice without changing saved
settings.  Start in an ordinary working buffer so the menu has a source
to return to.

  1. Press ‘C-e H v b’ to open the engine browser through _Voices and
     engines_.
  2. Select an available engine and press ‘v’ to list its voices.
     ‘<RET>’ on an Omnivox engine opens its status, downloads, and
     settings instead.
  3. Move to an available voice and press ‘P’ to hear a sample.  ‘S’
     stops it.
  4. Use ‘q’ to return through the browser's parent views to _Voices and
     engines_.  Once back in the menu, ‘C-q’ closes the menu sequence.
     The original buffer remains your working context.

   Browsing and previewing save nothing.  A downloaded but inactive
voice may need enablement and Apply before it can be sampled; choose an
already available voice for this first exercise.  If an engine is
unavailable, its details explain why.  If speech itself is absent, begin
with *note Recovering From No Speech: (emacsvox)Recovering From No
Speech.


File: emacsvox.info,  Node: Returning From Settings,  Next: Finding Settings And Actions,  Prev: Previewing An Installed Voice,  Up: Aural Home

8.2.2 Choose an entry point and preserve your place
---------------------------------------------------

The Emacsvox menu, ‘C-e H’, groups settings by task.  ‘C-e S’ opens the
current buffer's focused integration settings, or general buffer
settings when no focused menu exists.  ‘C-e H .’ always opens general
buffer settings.  A directly opened focused menu captures the buffer on
entry; ‘q’ closes it and announces the restored buffer and mode with a
close earcon.

   The menu captures the source item for contextual actions.  In
Feedback Details, ‘q’ returns to the menu with the same item and
settings scope; another ‘q’ closes that menu.  Opening Feedback Details
directly keeps its usual return destination.  A changed or deleted
source item must be selected again by reopening the menu.  If the source
buffer has closed, the menu cannot resume.

   Editor and manager ‘h’ keys open the Transient settings menu with the
original source item.  Closing that menu returns to the editor without
saving or discarding its draft.

   The menu continues to refer to the item captured when you opened it,
even if you move point in the source buffer.  Use ‘C-e H b’ to return to
that position.  To work on a different item, close the menu and reopen
‘C-e H’ from the new item in an ordinary buffer.

   The former Aural Home table remains available through ‘M-x
emacsvox-aural’ for existing configurations.  Its ‘C-e H h’ entry has
been retired; the workflows below use the settings menus.

   Leave an editor using its own ‘q’, ‘h’ or finish action before using
menu keys.  The same letter can mean something else while the editor
still has focus.  Returning does not reverse an applied setting, and
keeping an unfinished draft does not save it.  See *note Choose what
persists: Saving And Startup. before leaving work that you need in a
later session.

  1. Navigate menus and read Help

     The settings menus use Transient: while a menu is open, its keys
     act on that menu.  Up/down selects an item; left/right changes
     sections and selects the destination's first available item.
     Section movement stops at the first and last section.  Selecting an
     item speaks it without activating it or changing its value.
     ‘<RET>’, ‘M-<RET>’ or ‘C-j’ activates that item.  A displayed
     shortcut invokes its action directly, without first moving the
     selection to it.

     An action can open another menu or editor, toggle a switch, or ask
     for a value.  For a numeric control that offers adjustment,
     Shift-left/right decreases or increases the selected value; ‘<RET>’
     opens its exact-value prompt.  The item's Help gives its step,
     range and scope.  Plain arrows continue to navigate.  Waveform,
     envelope and other choice lists use up/down to browse and ‘<RET>’
     to choose; browsing alone does not apply a choice.  When a
     minibuffer prompt asks for text or a number, use its ordinary
     editing and completion keys.  Finish with ‘<RET>’ or cancel with
     ‘C-g’ to return to the menu.

     In Emacsvox's menus, ‘q’ or ‘C-g’ goes back one menu and ‘C-q’
     closes the sequence.  A child menu normally returns to the item
     that opened it.  Changes already applied remain in effect; an
     editor's Back action states whether it retains or discards an
     unfinished draft.  Other applications using Transient, including
     Magit, retain their own activation and quit keys.  Use ‘C-j’ to
     activate a selected Magit item where ‘<RET>’ has another meaning.

     Menu feedback interrupts previous main-stream speech, including
     when you toggle scope or change a setting.  Item announcements
     include the key without repeating the word "shortcut" or the
     section name.  Under the default announcement mode, section names
     are spoken when you enter a section.  Left/right selects its first
     selectable item and normally speaks it with the section name and
     position.

     For menu Help, press ‘?’ followed by a choice's key, or ‘? ?’ for
     help on the current menu.  The overview is a read-only snapshot:
     ordinary text reading works there, but its displayed actions run
     only after you return to the menu.  ‘q’ in that Help resumes the
     menu, including its parent menus and selection.  Opening ‘C-e H’
     announces a short reminder of Help and the ‘n’ navigation and
     speech settings.  Moving between items does not repeat that
     reminder.

       1. Review an item without changing the selection

          If an announcement is unclear, review the selected item's
          label, value, description and key without leaving the menu.
          The review position is separate from menu selection: ‘<RET>’
          still activates the same item after you read one of its words
          or characters.

          ‘C-c l’
               Repeat the complete item and reset its word and character
               review.
          ‘M-f, M-b’
               Read the next or previous word.  Punctuation stays with
               its word.
          ‘C-f, C-b’
               Read the next or previous character, including spaces.
          ‘C-c w, C-c c’
               Repeat the current review word or character.
          ‘C-a’
               Move before the item's first word and character,
               announcing "Start of item".
          ‘C-e’
               Move after its last word and character, announcing "End
               of item".

          The first word or character command starts at the item's
          beginning.  To read backwards from its end, use ‘C-e’ followed
          by ‘M-b’ or ‘C-b’.  At either end, moving outwards repeats the
          boundary announcement; reversing direction reads the first or
          last element again.  ‘C-a C-f’ therefore reads the first
          character, and ‘C-e C-b’ reads the last.  Word and character
          review share a position.  Selecting another item or changing
          its displayed value starts a fresh review.

          Character review follows your normal capitalisation
          presentation.  If delayed phonetics are enabled in *note
          Typing and interaction: (emacsvox)Reading Scope And
          Punctuation., pausing after a character also speaks its
          phonetic description.  Further input or speech cancels that
          pending description.  Review interrupts obsolete main speech
          without changing a setting.

          These defaults apply to Transient menus, including Magit, only
          where the menu has no binding of its own.  The menu overview
          lists the available review keys.  While reviewing a menu,
          ‘C-e’ means the end of the item: it is not the ordinary
          Emacsvox speech prefix.  Use ‘C-c l’, ‘C-c w’ and ‘C-c c’ for
          these menu repeats.  After closing the menu, ordinary buffer
          bindings apply again.

       2. Choose navigation and announcement preferences

          Press ‘C-e H n’ for Menu navigation, also available at ‘C-e H
          s n’.  Choose ‘c’ for Continuous, which crosses sections and
          wraps around the whole menu; ‘w’ to wrap within the current
          section; or ‘s’ to stop at section edges with a boundary
          earcon.  Left/right moves between sections in every style.
          Changes apply immediately to all Transient menus,
          independently of the current-buffer settings scope.  Wrap
          within section is the default.

          In the Presentation section, choose ‘i’ for Item first or ‘n’
          for Section first.  Section first is the default: on changing
          sections it speaks the section name and position before the
          item.  Item first reverses that order.  Movement within a
          section speaks the item without repeating its section.
          Previously saved preferences remain in effect.

          Choose ‘m’ for _By movement: sections or items_.  Left/right
          speaks only the destination section and its position.  Up/down
          speaks only the item, key, and relevant value, even when
          Continuous navigation crosses a section boundary.
          Section-change and wrapping earcons remain the same.  This
          option applies to all three navigation styles and all
          Transient menus, including Magit; it does not change the
          navigation style.

          Section arrows still select the first item.  In Emacsvox
          menus, ‘<RET>’ therefore activates that item even though its
          name has not been spoken, and the next Down advances to the
          second item.  Press ‘C-c l’ to hear that first item without
          moving, then decide whether to activate it.  Magit's existing
          activation bindings remain unchanged.

          Press ‘v’ to toggle Detailed descriptions.  When enabled, menu
          items retain their labels and values and add a short command
          explanation.  This applies globally, including Magit; commands
          without documentation keep their labels, and Transient's
          native infix controls keep their usual descriptions.  Detailed
          descriptions are on by default.  Value adjustments still give
          brief feedback.  Press ‘S’ to save the navigation style,
          presentation order, and descriptions preference through
          Customize.


File: emacsvox.info,  Node: Finding Settings And Actions,  Prev: Returning From Settings,  Up: Aural Home

8.2.3 Find a setting or an action
---------------------------------

Press ‘C-e H /’ to find an Emacsvox setting by any part of its name,
ignoring capitalisation.  Type ‘cap’ to find _Capitalization indication
and camel case_, _Capital-letter beep_, _All-caps beep_, and _Spatial
capabilities_.  The aliases ‘caps beep’ and ‘allcaps’ find both beep
controls and only _All-caps beep_, respectively.  Each result appears
once under its normal name.  Use completion to choose a result;
ambiguous text does not choose one automatically.  These matching rules
apply to this search without changing completion elsewhere in Emacs.
Space inserts a literal space; ‘<TAB>’ completes.  Use your configured
completion interface to browse results, displaying completions with
‘<TAB>’ if needed.  The menu's section and item arrows do not govern
this minibuffer prompt.

   ‘<RET>’ accepts the choice, opens its menu, and selects the matching
control without changing its value.  Press ‘<RET>’ again to activate or
edit the control.  The captured source and current settings scope follow
the search.  ‘q’ returns to the menu where you searched, and cancelling
completion keeps that menu open.

   Press ‘C-e H a’ to find an action such as _Browse voices_, _Your
feedback rules_, or _Aural Doctor_.  It uses the same case-insensitive
partial-name matching and two-Enter selection as settings search: choose
the destination, then activate its control.  Child views return to their
focused menu; another ‘q’ returns to the menu where you searched.

   Press ‘C-e H b’ to close menus and return to the captured source
position, speaking its line.  A changed or deleted source item must be
captured again.

   Press ‘C-e H .’ for settings relevant to the captured buffer: speech,
indentation, typing/point feedback, capitalisation, and its major mode's
Customize group when available.  This entry starts with buffer scope
without changing the parent's scope.  ‘A’ opens the advanced Emacsvox
Customize group.  In these linked Customize buffers, use Customize's own
field navigation, Apply and Save commands.  Outside an editable field,
‘q’ finishes Customize and returns to the menu.  See *note Adjust an
integration's speech settings: Integration speech settings. for
application-specific controls.

8.2.4 Choose a task and navigate its editor
-------------------------------------------

Choose a focused menu for your task:

Menu           Destinations
‘C-e H f’      Explain, change, or tune current feedback; review recent
               feedback.
‘C-e H v’      Voice palettes, installed voices and engines, and Voice
               Workbench.
‘C-e H s’      Rate, punctuation, indentation, capitalisation, and
               interaction.
‘C-e H o’      Sounds, output volumes, notifications, and spatial
               controls.
‘C-e H r’      Presentation Options, scoped rules, training, and
               semantic vocabulary.
‘C-e H p’      Profiles, palettes, changed settings, and retained
               drafts.
‘C-e H D’      Doctor, spatial capabilities, and speech diagnostics.

   Editors keep their own controls for detailed changes.  In an editor's
table, ‘<RET>’ opens a selected destination, details, or expandable
group.

   Expandable groups announce an open or close cue with their name when
you reach or toggle them.  These cues follow the icon setting and sound
pack.  For spoken state words, enable _aural-panel-state-labels_ in
_Presentation Options_.  This adds "collapsed" or "expanded" after the
control through the normal presentation rules.  Key instructions remain
available in Help.

   In table-based managers and editors, the navigation controls are:

‘n, p, down, up’
     Move by row, retaining and speaking the selected column.  In the
     first column, settings lists announce the name and current state.
‘left, right’
     Move among the titled columns.
‘.’
     Speak the current column title and value.
‘SPC’
     Speak the complete current row.
‘RET’
     Open a destination, child list, or details.
‘C-c C-a’
     Choose an applicable action by name.
‘N’
     Create a new item where supported.
‘P’
     Preview in managers and editors.
‘S’
     Stop the preview and its remaining samples.
‘?’
     Display and speak help for the current Aural interface.
‘h’
     Open the settings menu from a manager or editor that provides this
     key.
‘q’
     Go back or leave; an editor may first offer to retain or discard
     changes.

   _Feedback for this item_ combines explanation, playback, and access
to voice and sound changes.  ‘C-e H f’ groups its editing routes.  ‘C-e
H r h’ opens _Semantic vocabulary_; change editors also link it from
matching criteria.

   The voice editor contains fields and buttons rather than table
columns: left/right changes a numeric value, and ‘<RET>’ edits a field.
See *note Edit a Named Voice: (emacsvox)Editing Named Voices.  Keys are
local to the view: ‘w’ saves a voice in its editor but exports a palette
in the palette manager.  Use ‘C-c C-a’ to choose an action by name, or
‘?’ for that view's keys.

   Panel entry, return, selection, and warning cues accompany their
spoken feedback in one utterance.  Returning from a panel announces the
destination and any unfinished draft warning together.  These cues
follow the icon setting, sound pack, and cue remapping rules.  The voice
editor uses the normal navigation voice for these announcements;
auditions still play the working voice.

   Opening settings or Help preserves the selected row and unfinished
edits.  Buffer rule editors retain separate drafts for each source
buffer; reopening a modified editor resumes its draft.  Reopening the
same voice in the voice editor likewise preserves its working settings.
If a prepared remap's source item changes or disappears, Emacsvox asks
you to prepare that change again before applying it.

   Press ‘C-e H p d’ to resume a retained draft in its original rule
editor, voice editor, or Workbench engine settings.  The draft remains
unsaved until accepted there.  Presentation Profiles capture options,
palette, sound pack, and spatial settings.  Physical choices and
adjustments belong to named palette voices; shared engine settings are
saved separately.  See *note Choose What Persists: (emacsvox)Saving And
Startup. before treating a setup as a backup.


File: emacsvox.info,  Node: Presentation Model,  Next: Explaining Presentation,  Prev: Aural Home,  Up: Aural Presentation

8.3 How Aural Presentation Works
================================

Four questions explain the path from an application event to sound:

Layer            Question               Example
Meaning          What happened or       A level-two heading was reached
                 what is this?          during navigation.
Presentation     What should convey     Speak the heading in a named
                 that meaning?          voice and play a section cue
                                        first.
Resources        Which voice and        Find the named voice in the
                 sound realise that     active palette and the cue in the
                 request?               active sound pack.
Output           What can this          Route speech to an installed
                 machine produce?       engine and apply supported
                                        spatial behaviour.

   An Emacs mode or integration supplies facts about an object or event.
A fact can identify a role such as a heading, an event such as
completion, a state such as unread, or an attribute such as heading
level.  Context says whether you are navigating, reading continuously,
or receiving a state change.  The same object can therefore receive
concise feedback during continuous reading and more orientation when
deliberately reached.

   Presentation rules match those facts and the context.  A rule can
choose a voice, add speech or a cue, insert a pause or tone, request
spatial placement, or suppress something unhelpful.  Several rules can
contribute to the same result.  A stronger rule can replace just one
part, such as pitch, while leaving the other contributions in place.

   The configuration layers, from weakest to strongest, are:

  1. A fixed internal baseline preserving established Emacsvox
     behaviour.
  2. Automatic integration behaviour supplied by the active mode or
     package.
  3. Enabled _Presentation Options_, composed in their stable order.
  4. Personal, session, and current-buffer rules for precise overrides.

   A presentation profile is not another rule layer.  It records
selected options, sound pack, voice palette, and spatial choices so you
can switch them together.

   For example, an Org integration identifies a level-two heading.  A
rule requests ‘bolden’ and a section cue.  The active palette supplies
‘bolden’'s settings and physical choices; the sound pack supplies the
cue.  The backend plays what it supports.  Changing the rule changes
which headings receive the feedback.  Editing ‘bolden’ changes uses of
that named entry.  Switching sound packs changes the recording of the
cue while preserving its meaning.

   When two rules set the same voice field, the stronger explicit value
wins; values are not added.  Personal rules are weaker than session
rules, which are weaker than current-buffer rules.  Rules can also add
actions before or after content.  Use Explain to inspect actual
contributors rather than inferring precedence from the order in which
you edited settings.

   ‘C-e H r h’ opens the semantic vocabulary without changing settings.
‘C-e H r t’ toggles training explanations across all buffers for this
Emacs session; it does not save a startup default.

* Menu:

* What you can hear: Presentation Modalities.  Recognize speech, voices, cues, tones, pauses, and spatial output.


File: emacsvox.info,  Node: Presentation Modalities,  Up: Presentation Model

8.3.1 What You Can Hear
-----------------------

Aural Presentation combines these forms of feedback:

   • _Speech_ conveys content, labels, prompts, results, and status.
     Added speech should explain meaning not already clear from the
     content.
   • _Voice_ changes characteristics of spoken content.  Named voices
     make distinctions such as headings, annotations, warnings, or
     quoted text portable across engines.
   • _Auditory cues_, also called _earcons_ or _auditory icons_, are
     short sounds associated with events or objects.  Sound packs supply
     recordings for cue names.
   • _Tones_ convey compact information using a pitch and duration,
     without requiring sampled sound or a spoken explanation.
   • _Pauses_ separate related parts without adding words.
   • _Spatial presentation_ places speech or cues across supported
     output.  A backend unable to provide requested placement keeps
     content audible through its documented fallback.

   These can be ordered around content.  Entering a section might play a
cue, speak its heading in a distinctive voice, and leave a pause before
body text.  The presentation is one result, not unrelated notifications.
Its precise sound depends on engines, installed voices, sound pack, and
hardware.  The intended distinction should remain understandable when a
simpler backend offers fewer voice characteristics or centred output.


File: emacsvox.info,  Node: Explaining Presentation,  Next: Adjusting Presentation,  Prev: Presentation Model,  Up: Aural Presentation

8.4 Understand Why Something Sounded That Way
=============================================

Use this workflow when feedback is surprising, unclear, or especially
useful:

  1. Leave point on the object and press ‘C-e E’, or open ‘C-e H x’
     through the settings menu.  Both open _Feedback Details_ with a
     brief introduction instead of reading the whole report.  The menu
     route uses the Outline layout described below.
  2. Inspect the object and occasion, matching rules, selected actions,
     concrete voices and sounds, and any backend fallback or
     degradation.
  3. Identify the strongest rule changing the part you care about.  This
     tells you whether to inspect an option, personal or temporary rule,
     named voice, sound pack, or Voice Lock contribution.

   ‘C-e H f’ groups current and recent feedback actions.  ‘x’ explains
the captured item, ‘c’ opens a guided change, ‘v’ prepares a voice
mapping, ‘t’ tunes the named voice used there, and ‘e’ prepares an exact
cue remap.  ‘h’ opens recent feedback, whose changes use the selected
frozen record.  Tuning a named voice affects its other uses; a mapping
changes which matching items use a voice.

   Opening an editor from this menu does not bypass its preview,
matching criteria, scope or Apply/Save steps.  On return, the menu
selects the same entry.  A guided draft can be resumed later in this
session, while leaving a modified rule list still asks before discarding
it.  If the captured source item has been replaced, prepare the change
again; the editor does not silently apply it to a different target.

8.4.1 Distinguish a record from a simulation
--------------------------------------------

When retained feedback matches the source buffer, position, and
unchanged text, the report describes the exact output queued, including
the source and resolved resources.  It does not reinterpret old output
with your latest settings.  Without an exact record, the report is
labelled as a simulation of what the current rules would produce at
point.

   A simulation uses captured text and context.  _Play simulation_ plays
that baseline, not a recording of earlier sound.  It supports field
review and draft previews but is never added to Recent Feedback.  A
prefix argument with ‘C-e E’ selects a simulated occasion explicitly.
‘q’ returns to the source or menu item from which the report was opened.

   Expand a field and choose _Edit named voice bolden_, or its reported
voice name, to open the voice editor.  Multiple named voices receive
separate links.  This edits the entry in the reported palette and
affects its uses there.  _Change field_ instead changes the field's
feedback rule.  Leaving the voice editor returns to the same link;
opening it does not save or apply anything.

8.4.2 Inspect recent feedback
-----------------------------

Press ‘C-e H f h’ for recent feedback.  Each row preserves one recorded
presentation within the history's size limit.  Its Content column reads
the preview with captured voice annotations, including changes within
the entry; other columns and titles use the normal interface voice.  ‘P’
requests replay with the engine and physical voice recorded for the
original delivery.

‘RET or e’
     Open Feedback Details for the selected presentation.
‘P’
     Replay retained feedback with recorded voices without adding
     history.
‘c’
     Audition only its auditory cues.
‘r’
     Prepare a voice remap from the selected record.
‘R’
     Replace, suppress, or restore one exact earcon.
‘L’
     Change the bounded history limit for this session.
‘i’
     Include or exclude feedback from Aural interfaces themselves.

   To change the same policy from a menu, open ‘C-e H f H’.  Select
_Retained records_ and use ‘<RET>’ for an exact nonnegative limit, or
Shift-left/right to adjust by one.  Lowering the limit immediately
discards the oldest excess records; zero clears and disables history.
Increasing it cannot restore those records.  The menu's ‘i’ toggles
interface recording, and ‘w’ saves both defaults through Customize.
Retained feedback itself is never saved by this action.

   Aural interface feedback is excluded by default so it does not
displace the feedback being investigated.  This includes their prompts,
completion choices, and opening/closing sounds.  Ordinary completion
elsewhere remains eligible.  Changing the setting affects new
recordings, not entries already retained.

8.4.3 Read and edit the report's fields
---------------------------------------

Feedback Details selects a separate read-only buffer and gives a brief
introduction.  It stays attached to its presentation as new feedback
arrives; ordinary Help does not replace it.  When opened from Recent
Feedback, ‘q’ returns to the same row and column.

   Where integrations supply semantic fields, the report groups
consecutive voice spans accordingly.  Notmuch results, for example,
expose _Authors_, _Subject_, _Date_, _Message count_, and _Tags_.
Headings identify recorded logical voices.  Collapsed fields show only
headings.  Visibility uses configured cues or optional spoken labels;
_expanded_ and _collapsed_ are not inserted into heading text.

   ‘<RET>’ on a heading exposes its text, playback/change controls,
voice source, sounds, and limitations.  Content uses captured voice
annotations.  Fields with multiple spans offer _Individual voice spans_
for finer inspection.  That heading announces the section without
repeating content.  A single-span field shows its text and controls once
and omits a span count.  ‘<RET>’ collapses the field again.

   Spoken labels such as _indent 2_ have separate _Before speech_ or
_After speech_ headings, distinct from content spans.  ‘<RET>’ on a
label heading, or ‘O’ within it, replays that label using recorded
settings.  _Edit named voice indent_ opens the palette voice editor with
_indent 2_ as sample text; it changes every use of that entry in the
recorded palette.  The report's recording stays frozen: use the editor's
preview for changes.  Labels without a named palette voice remain
playable and explain why an editor link is unavailable.

   Fields with unapplied drafts announce _unsaved change_.  The report
also counts such fields.  These indicators update without replacing
buttons or return positions; ‘g’ refreshes draft status, for example
after killing a draft buffer.  It does not change the recorded or
simulated baseline.

   The original report layout uses these controls.  Outline changes
heading and button navigation as described in the next subsection, not
playback semantics.

‘n or p’
     Move between headings; collapsed fields speak the heading, expanded
     fields also speak annotated content.
‘Up or Down’
     Review lines without reading the whole report.
‘TAB or Shift-TAB’
     Move between action buttons; RET activates or expands.
‘O’
     Play the original field, span, or label at point, or its labelled
     simulation.
‘P’
     Play the full original presentation or labelled simulation.
‘C’
     Open or resume a field/span change; on a label, edit its named
     voice.
‘r’
     Choose a named voice for matching items; on a label, edit its named
     voice.
‘R’
     Replace, suppress, or restore an exact sound in the field or span.
‘V’
     Preview the full presentation with linked draft changes.
‘S’
     Stop playback.
‘g’
     Refresh draft status.

   For example, select _Subject_, press ‘C’, and choose another content
voice.  In the editor, ‘O’ plays the original field and ‘P’ the changed
field.  _Play whole original_, also on ‘B’, compares with ‘V’ for the
full presentation and linked drafts.  Both keep you in the editor.  The
original remains frozen, including before a change or lifetime is
chosen.  A simulated report uses _Play whole simulation_ for its frozen
baseline.

   Return to the report and choose _Message count_ to add a sound _After
content_.  It plays once after the complete count, even if the field
contains several voices.  Subject and count keep separate drafts.  Their
criteria identify field, integration, mode, and occasion; inspect them
before selecting buffer, session, or saved-personal lifetime.  Remapping
the field does not retune a shared voice.

   _Choose another voice for matching items_ opens the compact voice
editor.  Inspect _Applies to_ and choose _How long_ before saving.
_Change field_ opens the full component editor on the same draft.  A
draft already changing another component stays in the full editor so
that work is not hidden.

   _Sound overrides: replace, suppress, or restore_ prepares a separate
exact-sound rule draft restricted to the field.  It is not included in
linked-field previews.  _Restore inherited behavior_ removes an override
made by that workflow in the selected scope.  ‘q’ returns to the report.

8.4.4 Use the Outline report from the menu
------------------------------------------

‘C-e H x’ opens the experimental Outline version of Feedback Details.
It is read-only, with a visible summary and initially folded fields.
‘<TAB>’ on a heading cycles visibility, ‘S-<TAB>’ cycles the whole
report, and ‘n~/~p’ moves between visible headings.  Standard Outline
minor-mode commands are available under ‘C-c @’, including ‘C-c @ C-u’
for the parent.  Arrows read visible lines.  ‘<RET>’ folds a heading or
activates an action.  Away from a heading, ‘<TAB>’ and ‘M-n’ move to the
next visible action.  Playback and editing use the report commands
above.

   ‘o’ compares Outline with the original layout.  Refreshing draft
status with ‘g’ preserves folds and position; returning from an editor
preserves its button.  The report still describes the captured feedback.
Reports opened directly start in the original layout; ‘o’ switches their
style.

8.4.5 Understand playback evidence and limitations
--------------------------------------------------

Original replay uses the recorded engine and physical voice, including
notification voices and fallbacks, with captured tuning even after a
palette change.  It synthesises speech again at the current reading
speed and host pronunciation settings.  History stores no audio.  Cue
and pause order remains, but timing may differ from the first delivery.

   Replay requires a completed presentation with complete Omnivox voice
receipts.  Older, interrupted, truncated, or unsupported records remain
inspectable but report replay unavailable rather than guessing a voice.
Cues positioned inside speech, or voice changes that cannot be aligned
after host text processing, also prevent original replay.  An
unavailable recorded voice stops playback instead of substituting
another.  Navigate over the source to capture a new example.

   A simulated report labels its baseline throughout the report and
change editor.  Proposed playback uses current rules, captured buffer
context, and linked drafts without applying them.  A span can be
targeted only when its semantic field, distinct named face, or explicit
voice annotation identifies it.  Truncated history cannot supply
complete playback or a comparable preview.  Records missing preview
inputs remain readable; capture a new example before previewing.

   Each field explains the sources of its voices and sounds, such as a
face or personal override, using the contributors actually captured.
Unsupported voice families and other limitations appear beside the
affected field.

   _Debug details_ opens a separate read-only raw snapshot of facts,
rule IDs, resolved voices, and backend diagnostics.  Opening speaks only
a short announcement.  ‘q’ returns to the same report location with
fields and drafts retained.

   ‘C-e H r t’ toggles _Explain feedback as you navigate_ for this
session.  It adds concise semantic explanations after normal
presentations, useful for learning an integration's vocabulary.  It is
deliberately more verbose than normal use; turn it off when no longer
needed.


File: emacsvox.info,  Node: Adjusting Presentation,  Next: Voices And Routing,  Prev: Explaining Presentation,  Up: Aural Presentation

8.5 Change Feedback And Manage Rules
====================================

Choose the narrowest tool that expresses the change:

Intention                      Use                    Scope
Try optional integration       Presentation Options   Global
feedback                                              configuration
Change a voice, sound, tone,   Feedback Details,      Personal, session,
label, or suppression          then Change field      or current buffer
Review or remove a precise     Your feedback rules    The rule's
change                                                recorded scope
Experiment only in one work    Current Buffer Rules   Life of that
buffer                                                buffer
Switch reading or working      Presentation           Selected global
settings together              Profiles               configuration

   ‘C-e H r’ opens Presentation Options and personal, session, or
captured-buffer rule editors.  Their validation, previews, saving, and
discard prompts remain in the editor.  This menu also toggles global
visual-face presentation; its ‘w’ saves only that toggle through
Customize.  Voice Lock is independent.

* Menu:

* Walkthrough Change Headings In One Buffer: Heading Voice Walkthrough.  Try a heading voice, verify the scope, and undo the rule.
* Make a guided feedback change: Guided Feedback Changes.  Change one component, preview its effect, and choose matching criteria and lifetime.
* Choose a voice for matching items: Choosing A Voice For An Item.  Choose another named voice for matching items without retuning it.
* Review And Remove Your Rules: Managing Feedback Rules.  Inspect, change, disable, or remove personal and temporary rules.
* Try A Presentation Option: Presentation Options.  Preview and enable maintained feedback additions before writing a narrow override.


File: emacsvox.info,  Node: Heading Voice Walkthrough,  Next: Guided Feedback Changes,  Up: Adjusting Presentation

8.5.1 Walkthrough: Change Headings In One Buffer
------------------------------------------------

This experiment changes headings in a temporary Org buffer without
altering palette definitions or saved personal rules.  Use an unused
buffer name; if ‘*aural-practice*’ already contains work, choose another
name throughout the exercise.  Do not erase existing text to recreate
the example.

  1. Use ‘C-x b’ to create that new buffer.  Run ‘M-x org-mode <RET>’
     and insert:

          * Reading notes
          An ordinary paragraph.
          * Next steps
          Another ordinary paragraph.

  2. Move onto the first heading and press ‘C-e E’.  Identify its named
     voice and heading facts, noting whether the report describes
     recorded output or a simulation.  Press ‘q’ to return to the source
     before opening a new menu.

  3. Press ‘C-e H f c’ for the guided change.  Press ‘c’, select
     _Content voice_, and choose ‘annotate’ with ‘<RET>’, or ‘bolden’ if
     ‘annotate’ is already used.  ‘O’ plays the original and ‘P’
     previews the proposed voice.  Only the draft has changed.

  4. Press ‘m’ for _What should match_.  Keep the Org module, heading
     role, and heading level if shown.  Leave other criteria as shown
     for this experiment.  Press ‘l’ and select _Current buffer, until
     it is killed_.  This matches every qualifying heading in the
     buffer, not only the original heading.

  5. Review the summary and press ‘a’ to apply it.  Open settings with
     ‘h’, then press ‘b’ in that menu to return to the captured item.
     Move between the two headings using the same navigation action.
     Both matching headings should use the chosen voice; ordinary
     paragraphs should keep their previous presentation.

  6. Press ‘C-e E’ on a heading again.  Check that the buffer rule
     supplies the selected voice.  If the sound is too similar to
     distinguish, the report still shows whether it matched.  Compare
     voices or consult *note Troubleshooting: (emacsvox)Presentation
     Troubleshooting.  Return to the source with ‘q’.

  7. Open ‘C-e H r o’ for _Your feedback rules_.  Find the rule in
     buffer scope and inspect its target.  ‘t’ disables it and ‘d’
     removes it.  Revisit the heading to check the result.  Killing the
     practice buffer also ends its buffer rules; none survives an Emacs
     restart.

   For a lasting change, repeat on a representative item and choose
_Saved personal rule_ at step 4.  Review the criteria carefully: that
rule can affect future matching buffers.


File: emacsvox.info,  Node: Guided Feedback Changes,  Next: Choosing A Voice For An Item,  Prev: Heading Voice Walkthrough,  Up: Adjusting Presentation

8.5.2 Guided Change This Feedback
---------------------------------

  1. Move to an item and press ‘C-e H f c’ to capture _Current item_.
     For earlier feedback, open ‘C-e H f h’, select a record, and press
     ‘C’.  A multipart record expands numbered parts in playback order.
     Move with ‘n~/~p’ to hear text in captured voices, ‘O’ to replay
     the part, and ‘<RET>’ to choose it.  Truncated history cannot
     provide a complete original/proposed comparison.

  2. Press ‘c’ to expand component changes and ‘<RET>’ on a choice.
     _Content voice_ expands named voices, identifies original and
     replacement, and lists the original first when available, then
     alternatives alphabetically.  Selecting a voice with ‘<RET>’ keeps
     the panel open for comparison.  Changes can add a cue, tone, or
     spoken label, replace a sound, change a tone, or suppress a
     component.  A cue can be added when none existed.  Choices depend
     on integration facts; they cannot create missing semantic
     distinctions.

     ‘<RET>’ on _Parts_ reopens the parts; each retains its unfinished
     choices.  ‘q’ goes up one level, for example from a voice to
     _Content voice_ and then _Change_.  The section closes and its
     parent's name and state are announced through the configured cues
     and labels.  At the top, ‘q’ leaves the panel while preserving its
     draft.

  3. Press ‘P’ to preview before choosing a lifetime.  ‘O’ replays the
     original and ‘S’ stops.  Proposed preview resolves the example with
     current rules and captured context; it does not apply the draft to
     live feedback.

  4. Press ‘m’ or ‘<RET>’ on _What should match_.  Facts, states,
     events, and occasions are marked _Included_ or _Excluded_.  All
     included conditions must match; excluded conditions are ignored,
     not forbidden.  The captured or simulated example's facts are not
     editable, but can be selected as conditions.  Opening criteria
     preserves existing selections; extra facts start excluded.

     A mail action of _select_ describes the operation producing
     feedback, not selection in this editor.  Include that action, or an
     event such as _operation failed_, to restrict the rule.  ‘<RET>’
     toggles each criterion.  Excluded criteria remain available to
     restore.  Excluding _with state unread_ while retaining _with role
     field_, _with field kind authors_, and the Notmuch integration
     matches authors in both read and unread messages.  Keep at least
     one criterion.  The parent row summarises the match; ‘m’ collapses
     the criteria.  These conditions govern matching items, not one
     document object.

     A shell example may match "Items from the shell integration, in
     shell mode, during navigation, and with text style comint highlight
     prompt."  The text style names the Emacs face used to display that
     text.

  5. Press ‘l’ for _How long_: source-buffer lifetime, this Emacs
     session, or a saved personal rule.  A buffer rule affects every
     matching item in that buffer.  Review target, change, criteria, and
     lifetime together.

  6. Press ‘a’ or ‘C-c C-c’ to apply or save.  ‘e’ opens the prepared
     rule in the Advanced editor.  At the top, ‘q’ hides the guided
     draft.  Resume it through ‘C-e H p d’, which opens the retained
     draft in its owning editor.  Aborting a choice or leaving before
     Apply does not change live rules.  Failed saves retain the draft
     and report failure.

   Replacing or suppressing a sound targets its identity in a before- or
after-content phase while retaining other components.  Existing rule
data can insert only at a phase's beginning or end, so replacing a
middle component asks which edge to use and includes that placement in
the summary.  Ambiguous components require the Advanced editor.
Current-item changes recheck their captured source; a changed or killed
target must be inspected again.  Historical changes remain tied to the
selected record.


File: emacsvox.info,  Node: Choosing A Voice For An Item,  Next: Managing Feedback Rules,  Prev: Guided Feedback Changes,  Up: Adjusting Presentation

8.5.3 Remap One Item At Point
-----------------------------

  1. Move to a representative object and press ‘C-e E’ to identify what
     controls it.  Return to the source with ‘q’ before starting a new
     action.

  2. Press ‘C-e H f v’ to choose a voice for matching items.  To tune
     the named voice already used there instead, use ‘C-e H f t’.
     Tuning changes that voice wherever the palette entry is used;
     remapping changes which voice a face or item receives.  For a shell
     prompt, you can assign a different voice to the prompt face or tune
     the current prompt voice.  It need not have been spoken before you
     begin.

     In the remapping editor, move with ‘n~/~p’ or arrows.  ‘<RET>’ on
     _Voice_ chooses a voice, _Preview original_ plays the captured
     item, and _Preview change_ plays the draft.  ‘O~/~P’ provide the
     same previews; ‘S’ stops.  Previewing does not save or retune the
     named voice.  Use ‘C-e H f e’ for an exact earcon remap.

  3. Choose _How long_: personal persists across Emacs sessions, session
     lasts until Emacs exits, and buffer affects only the captured live
     buffer.

  4. Review _Applies to_, _Voice_, and _How long_, then _Save_ or ‘w’.
     A face mapping affects matching uses within its module/mode and
     lifetime.  _Advanced_ opens the full editor for finer criteria.  At
     the top, ‘q’ hides the compact editor and retains its draft.  After
     a voice has been chosen, ‘C-e H p d’ resumes it directly in the
     original editor.

  5. Return to the source, repeat the action, and press ‘C-e E’ to
     confirm which rule wins.


File: emacsvox.info,  Node: Managing Feedback Rules,  Next: Presentation Options,  Prev: Choosing A Voice For An Item,  Up: Adjusting Presentation

8.5.4 Review And Remove Your Rules
----------------------------------

‘C-e H r o’ opens _Your feedback rules_, combining personal, session,
and captured-buffer changes.  ‘q’ returns to the selected menu item.
‘P’ previews the complete result, ‘t’ temporarily enables/disables a
rule, and ‘d’ removes it.  Removing an override restores the next weaker
contribution rather than leaving a copied default.

   In the rule list, ‘<RET>’ or ‘e’ opens the selected rule's panel.
Other rules stay in the working draft.  ‘n~/~p’ moves through settings
and ‘<SPC>’ reads the selected value.  ‘<RET>’ expands _What should
match_, _Content_, _Before content_, or _After content_.  Matching
criteria toggle included/excluded states.

   Within _Content_, _voice_ and _speak_ expand choices in place with
the current selection shown.  Other fields ask for the selected value.
Existing actions expand into settings; _Change operation_ chooses an
operation for that phase.  _Inherit_ removes a voice or speech override
and ‘D’ removes another selected setting.  Each change preserves other
settings and remains in the draft.

   ‘P’ in the panel previews the working rule and ‘S’ stops.  ‘w’ or
_Save_ writes to its original scope and announces whether it was saved
or temporarily applied.  ‘q’ closes a section and focuses its parent,
retaining edits: voice choice to _voice_ to _Content_, then panel to
rule list.  In the list, ‘N’ adds a rule and ‘q’ asks before discarding
an unfinished draft.


File: emacsvox.info,  Node: Presentation Options,  Prev: Managing Feedback Rules,  Up: Adjusting Presentation

8.5.5 Try A Presentation Option
-------------------------------

Presentation Options supply maintained additions such as heading
feedback or integration-specific cues.  Open them with ‘C-e H r f’:

  1. Expand a collection with ‘<TAB>’ or ‘<RET>’.
  2. Preview an option with ‘P’ composed with current settings, or ‘C-u
     P’ in isolation.
  3. Toggle it with ‘t’.  ‘a’ reads enabled options in
     weakest-to-strongest order.
  4. Return to the source and repeat the original task.  Personal,
     session, and buffer overrides remain stronger than enabled options.

   Do not reorder options to solve one exceptional item when a narrow
override expresses the intended change more precisely.


File: emacsvox.info,  Node: Voices And Routing,  Next: Editing Named Voices,  Prev: Adjusting Presentation,  Up: Aural Presentation

8.6 Choose And Manage Voice Palettes
====================================

A _named voice_, also called a _logical voice_ in detailed views, gives
a reusable name such as ‘bolden’ or ‘annotate’ to a way of speaking.  A
_voice palette_ contains these entries.  Each includes shared
adjustments, ordered physical choices, and adjustments for individual
choices.

   A _physical voice_ comes from an installed speech engine.  The
palette expresses intended choices; availability and shared engine
preferences determine which can be used.  A presentation rule determines
when to use a named voice.  Editing that entry changes every use
resolving to it; changing a rule changes which items select it.

   This section manages the collection.  *note Edit a Named Voice:
(emacsvox)Editing Named Voices. covers its physical choices and
adjustments; *note Installed Voices and Engines: (emacsvox)Installed
Voices And Engines. covers browsing and engine preferences.

   Open _Voice Palettes_ with ‘C-e H v p’.  ‘<RET>’ on a palette browses
its voices, ‘P’ auditions its sequence, and ‘a’ activates it.  Built-in
palettes remain intact; editing can open an unsaved personal child in
the common editor.

   *Upgrading personal palettes.*  This version accepts schema 3 only.
Before upgrading older personal palettes, back up palette/routing data
and arrange a reviewed conversion of entries and physical choices.
Opening or previewing does not convert them.  Preserve names and profile
references, with each personal parent chain ending at ‘acss-default’.
Shared per-voice routing, migration, and preset screens have been
removed; use the common editor for named-voice choices and adjustments.
Shared engine preference remains separate.

   Opening the manager announces its selected palette; entering a
palette announces its name, voice count, selected voice, and main keys.
_Kind_ distinguishes personal, built-in, and standard-default palettes.
_Parent_ names the inherited source.  Read-only ‘acss-default’ is the
root: chains such as ‘a -> b -> c -> acss-default’ are supported.  It
provides personalities from Emacsvox and loaded integrations, not a
fixed list of all custom names.  Only the root has no parent.

   A palette inherits the nearest complete definition of a named voice.
Its style, ordered physical choices and individual tuning come from that
one entry; they are not assembled from several parents.  The _Source_
column names the supplying palette, and capital ‘O’ opens it.

   Press ‘u’ for _Where Used_ before editing a shared entry.  This lists
known loaded references, aliases and affected descendant palettes.  It
cannot inspect configuration that has not been loaded.  Editing a shared
entry asks you to review those known uses and descendants.

   An _alias_, such as ‘voice-bolden’ for ‘bolden’, is a declared
alternative spelling of the same entry.  Integrations maintain aliases;
the manager does not edit them as separate voices.  Use Copy Voice for
an independently adjustable entry.

   ‘N’ in a voice list creates New Voice and ‘c’ copies one into an
unsaved editor draft.  Copy preserves the full effective entry, fallback
order, and individual tuning.  Editing or copying a built-in voice
requests a personal child palette; saving adds only the requested direct
entry.  Opening or previewing writes nothing.

   ‘R’ resets a personal definition to its inherited entry.
Confirmation names the source that will take over and rejects a source
changed during confirmation.  Other saved settings remain.  ‘d’ deletes
a direct custom voice with no inherited replacement; built-in and
inherited entries cannot be deleted.  Remap known references first if
deletion would leave them unresolved.  Finish or discard voice drafts
before deletion.  ‘o’ returns to the palette manager.

   New Palette, ‘N’ in the manager, creates a child of the chosen
parent.  Copy Palette, ‘c’, saves all effective entries independently
under ‘acss-default’; local physical choices belong to the new palette.
The summary and parent action ‘E’ inspect or change a personal parent;
inheritance cycles are rejected.

   To delete a personal palette, select it and press ‘d’.  Change
children and known references, including saved profiles, and
finish/discard its voice drafts first.  Deleting an active palette
returns to the standard baseline.

   In Voice Palettes, ‘a’ activates the selected palette for this
session.  To return to following the standard baseline, press ‘f’.  That
clears an explicit session selection without deleting palette data or
changing startup.

   Capital ‘U’, _Use at Startup_, changes the palette named by the
selected saved Presentation Profile and preserves that profile's other
fields.  It does not apply the profile now.  With no startup profile,
the operation proposes one captured from the current setup; review it
before saving.  Its checks cover saved rules and profile options, not
session or buffer rules.  An open Profiles view refreshes to show the
saved choice.

* Menu:

* Walkthrough Make A Personal Reading Palette: Personal Palette Walkthrough.  Create, edit, save, activate, and optionally select a palette for startup.
* Walkthrough Inheritance, Aliases, And Reset: Palette Inheritance Walkthrough.  Follow an entry to its parent and distinguish an alias from a copy.
* Rename Voices And Palettes: Renaming Voices And Palettes.  Rename personal entries while checking their known uses.


File: emacsvox.info,  Node: Personal Palette Walkthrough,  Next: Palette Inheritance Walkthrough,  Up: Voices And Routing

8.6.1 Walkthrough: Make A Personal Reading Palette
--------------------------------------------------

Use an unused name; ‘reading-demo’ is an example.  Note the active
palette and whether selection is explicit or follows the standard
baseline.  The experiment saves a personal palette and briefly activates
it, but leaves startup selection unchanged.  Restore the selection
policy as well as the audible result at the end.

  1. Open ‘C-e H v p’.  Press ‘N’, enter ‘reading-demo’, describe its
     purpose as "My reading experiments", and choose ‘acss-default’ as
     parent.  Creation saves but does not activate the palette.  Its
     standard voices are inherited.
  2. Press ‘<RET>’ to enter it.  Find ‘bolden’ and inspect _Source_,
     which should name ‘acss-default’.  ‘.’ repeats the column
     title/value, ‘<SPC>’ reads the row, and ‘P’ previews the voice.
  3. Press ‘e’ to edit ‘bolden’.  Check destination and inherited
     source, then set _Average Pitch_ with ‘<RET>’ to ‘6’, or ‘5’ if
     already six.  ‘B’ compares opening and edited voices using one
     sample.  Unsupported adjustments remain reported; consult *note
     Voice Adjustments: (emacsvox)Voice Adjustments. if the contrast is
     unclear.
  4. Choose _Save to collection without selecting_.  After the save
     result, ‘q’ returns to the voice list.  _Source_ should now name
     ‘reading-demo’; other inherited entries retain their providers.
     This is a complete local entry, not a pitch-only patch on its
     parent.
  5. Use ‘o’ to return to the manager and ‘a’ to activate
     ‘reading-demo’.  Check status and revisit content using ‘bolden’.
     This changes every use resolving to that entry.  Explain at the
     source distinguishes palette settings from contextual rules.
  6. Leave startup selection unchanged for this experiment.  Capital ‘U’
     is the separate _Use at Startup_ action, not the next step required
     to hear your saved palette.
  7. In the palette manager, use ‘f’ if you originally followed the
     standard baseline.  Otherwise select the previous explicit palette
     and press ‘a’.  The saved demo remains for later experiments.  To
     remove only its ‘bolden’ override, follow *note Inheritance,
     aliases and reset: (emacsvox)Palette Inheritance Walkthrough.

   For a deliberate lasting change, ‘U’ changes the palette in the saved
profile.  If it proposes a new profile, review its other settings too.
Re-selecting an old palette does not by itself undo creating or editing
that startup profile.  See *note Choose what persists: (emacsvox)Saving
And Startup. before taking this separate step.


File: emacsvox.info,  Node: Palette Inheritance Walkthrough,  Next: Renaming Voices And Palettes,  Prev: Personal Palette Walkthrough,  Up: Voices And Routing

8.6.2 Walkthrough: Inheritance, Aliases, And Reset
--------------------------------------------------

Use ‘reading-demo’ from the previous exercise or another personal
palette with ‘acss-default’ as parent.

  1. In the manager, create ‘reading-child-demo’ with ‘N’ and choose
     ‘reading-demo’ as parent.  The chain becomes ‘reading-child-demo ->
     reading-demo -> acss-default’.
  2. Inspect ‘bolden’'s _Source_ in the child.  A direct entry in
     ‘reading-demo’ supplies the whole voice; otherwise ‘acss-default’
     does.  ‘O’ opens its provider and ‘u’ shows known uses, aliases,
     and descendants.
  3. ‘voice-bolden’ is an alias of ‘bolden’, not a separate adjustable
     entry.  Aliases are not created or edited in this view.
  4. Copy ‘bolden’ in the child with ‘c’ to an unused name, such as
     ‘reading-heading-demo’.  Change pitch, compare, and save to the
     collection without selecting.  A rule must choose this name before
     ordinary content uses it; copying does not remap headings.
  5. Edit the child's inherited ‘bolden’, change a field, and save to
     the collection.  Its Source becomes the child.  Back in the list,
     press ‘R’.  Confirmation names the parent entry that will take
     over.  After confirming, ‘bolden’ remains but its Source returns to
     the provider.
  6. Delete ‘reading-heading-demo’ with ‘d’ when it has no inherited
     replacement or known use.  Remove/remap a referencing rule first.
     Reset restores an inherited entry; Delete removes a removable
     custom name.

   The root keeps standard voices available after Reset and cannot be
deleted or edited here.  Palettes can contain custom names, and loaded
integrations can register more standard names.  Copy Palette differs
from New Palette: it copies effective entries and stops following edits
to the source while still inheriting new standard names from
‘acss-default’.  Definitions referring to an existing personality still
follow that personality's settings.  A copy is not a sound recording.


File: emacsvox.info,  Node: Renaming Voices And Palettes,  Prev: Palette Inheritance Walkthrough,  Up: Voices And Routing

8.6.3 Rename Voices And Palettes
--------------------------------

‘r’ in the voice list renames a direct custom voice in a personal
palette.  Entering the new name saves it with its settings, physical
choices, and tuning intact.  Finish/discard voice drafts first.
Standard names cannot be renamed; copy one for a custom entry.  An
inherited voice must be copied or renamed in its owning palette.

   Renaming updates personal, session, and buffer rules, face mappings,
and loaded global/buffer face maps.  Personal rules and the voice are
saved together; a failure leaves the old name and mappings usable.
Update face mappings written in your Lisp configuration there too for
future sessions.  Maintained integration rules, Presentation Options,
and other voice definitions can block a rename until their references
are updated.  Another palette directly defining either name also blocks
it because mappings are shared.

   In _Voice Palettes_, ‘r’ renames a personal palette and saves
immediately.  It retains settings and fallback tuning, updates children
and personal Presentation Profiles, and keeps an active palette selected
under its new name.  Finish/discard unfinished voice edits first.
Built-ins cannot be renamed, and maintained integration references can
block the change until their owner updates them.


File: emacsvox.info,  Node: Editing Named Voices,  Next: Emoji Speech Names,  Prev: Voices And Routing,  Up: Aural Presentation

8.7 Edit A Named Voice
======================

Open a palette through ‘C-e H v p’, select a voice, and press ‘e’.  To
tune the voice used by the captured source item, use ‘C-e H f t’.  This
changes the shared named entry, not a rule for that one item.  ‘q’
offers to retain unfinished work and returns to the menu.

   Saving an inherited voice creates a complete entry in the destination
palette.  Editing a built-in palette requests a personal child.  Opening
and previewing do not change live feedback or save settings.

   ‘default’ controls ordinary reading and labels without an explicit
voice.  Choose it to change normal text's physical voice, fallbacks, or
tuning.  Each palette has an effective ‘default’, inherited until
locally edited.  Switching palettes changes it too.  Other entries such
as ‘indent’ and ‘bolden’ retain their own settings; editing ‘default’ is
not a whole-palette voice change.

* Menu:

* Use one physical voice across a palette: Changing A Palette Physical Voice.  Review a preferred physical voice across named entries before saving.
* Understand Voice Adjustments: Voice Adjustments.  Understand the voice controls and their defaults.
* Choose And Tune Fallback Voices: Voice Fallbacks.  Order physical choices and customize their individual adjustments.
* Inspect Or Adjust A Voice In Context: Voices In Context.  Compare a voice with contextual rules and edit a rule rather than its base entry.

8.7.1 Adjust and compare one voice
----------------------------------

The editor reads field labels in the normal navigation voice.  Move
between fields with ‘n’, ‘p’, the up/down arrows or ‘<TAB>’.  Left/right
adjusts a numeric field and moves between other fields.  Press ‘<RET>’
to activate a control or enter a value.

   Lowercase ‘d’, or blank numeric input, requests the adapter's default
for that adjustment.  Entering zero instead requests an explicit value,
such as the lowest pitch or leftmost pan.  A default and zero may sound
alike for a particular effect, but they remain different settings.

   _More adjustments and effects_ exposes filters, gain, pan, and
optional controls.  Point stays on its button and speech says "More
adjustments shown below" or "More adjustments hidden".  Move down or use
‘<TAB>’ to enter exposed controls, and activate the button again to hide
them.  Changed fields state earlier values; unsupported settings stay
visible and retained.  Field navigation stops at its ends without
wrapping, and numeric limits announce minimum or maximum.

   ‘P’ previews the edited voice; ‘B’ compares captured original and
edited versions with the same sample and spoken labels.  ‘T’ changes
sample text and ‘S’ stops.  An automatic sample follows adjustments
unless _Automatic sample after adjustment_ is off.  The value precedes
it, for example "1.  The quick brown fox…".

   That toggle announces on/off with distinct cues.  _More adjustments
and effects_ and _Fallbacks_ use open/close cues and optional state
labels on both toggling and navigation.  Navigation stops a sample.  ‘u’
undoes an edit, including reset.  ‘q’ returns to the originating view,
offering to retain or discard unsaved work.

   _Physical voice_ searches engine or voice names.  Audition with
working adjustments before accepting; cancellation keeps the earlier
choice and returns to the field.  _Fallbacks_ allows replacement,
reordering, removal, individual auditions, appending, or returning to
Automatic.  Unavailable, engine-default, and property choices are
retained.  Shared adjustments supply inherited fields; _Customize this
voice_ provides optional per-choice settings.

8.7.2 Tune through the focused menu
-----------------------------------

From ‘C-e H v p’, enter a palette with ‘<RET>’, select a voice and press
‘e’ to open its editor.  Press ‘m’ there for focused tuning.  This menu
edits the same retained draft.  Up/down selects controls, left/right
changes sections, and Shift-left/right adjusts relative rate, pitch,
pitch range, or gain by one unit.  ‘<RET>’ enters an exact value.  ‘d’
restores the selected adjustment to adapter default, or asks which one
to reset.  Zero remains explicit.

   ‘f’ selects an existing fallback, ‘h’ returns to shared settings, and
the title identifies scope.  For a fallback, ‘i’ inherits the shared
value and ‘d’ requests adapter default.  Play and Compare use the
selected scope.

   ‘P’ previews, ‘B’ compares original/edited with one sample, ‘S’
stops, and ‘T’ changes text.  These previews are private and exclude
contextual rules.  Adjustments speak values without automatic samples
here; the full editor's automatic-sample preference is retained.  ‘u’
undoes the last draft change.

   ‘e’ opens engine-specific settings, waiting for a qualified runtime
catalogue before permitting edits.  Its ‘q’ returns to the common
editor; ‘m’ reopens focused tuning on the same draft and scope.
Physical experiments, individual fallbacks, and engine controls remain
available in the full editor.

   ‘w’ saves and applies; ‘c’ saves to the collection without selecting.
Existing destination prompts, inheritance, and recovery remain.  ‘q’
stops playback and returns to the editor with the draft.  Leaving that
editor still offers keep, discard, or continue choices.

8.7.3 Save the draft and check application
------------------------------------------

‘w’ or ‘C-c C-c’ saves and applies a complete named-voice draft.  Save
buttons follow settings, previews, and change details.  _Save to
collection without selecting_ saves an inactive palette without changing
selection.  The header separates unsaved changes, partial saves,
applying, failures, and saved/applied.

   Retry keeps the proposal and records which saving steps already
succeeded.  If you make more edits while an earlier Apply is still
waiting for a reply, that reply does not save the later edits.  They
remain unsaved.  If a file or destination has changed, review the
proposal again rather than expecting the editor to merge those changes
automatically.

   Complete-choice previews require Omnivox's complete-voice-preview
capability.  They do not select the draft or change live settings.
Older hosts report this unsupported; individual physical auditions
remain available.  Playback evidence identifies accepted audio when
reported, including unsupported adjustments, without inferring a
successful fallback from inventory or claiming exact native parameter
measurements.

   ‘q’ returns to the originating row and column.  Keep, discard, or
continue with unsaved work.  Retained drafts can be resumed through ‘C-e
H p d’ during this Emacs session only.  They do not survive restart, and
leaving does not undo a completed save.

   _Save and apply_ selects the palette for this session.  ‘U’, _Use at
Startup_ in Voice Palettes, retains its startup selection separately.
Saving can involve multiple files; partial failures retain the proposal
for retry.  See *note Recovering Unfinished Changes:
(emacsvox)Recovering Aural Changes.  Physical experiments first request
a destination; see *note Keep an Installed Voice Experiment:
(emacsvox)Keeping A Voice Experiment.


File: emacsvox.info,  Node: Changing A Palette Physical Voice,  Next: Voice Adjustments,  Up: Editing Named Voices

8.7.4 Use one physical voice across a palette
---------------------------------------------

Select a physical voice in _Browse voices_ or eSpeak _Voice variants_
and press ‘V’.  Choose an existing personal palette or create one.
Creation copies entries, adjustments, and fallbacks from your chosen
source.  All effective names, including ‘default’ and inherited entries,
are reviewed.

   The review starts at ‘default’.  Up/down selects rows, ‘O’ plays
original, ‘P’ proposed, ‘B’ compares both, and ‘A’ compares
representative voices.  ‘S’ stops; ‘x’ details descendants, retained
fallbacks, and playback results.  An unavailable selected physical voice
may cause fallback.

   If an entry already contains the exact physical choice, the review
moves that choice to the front and preserves its tuning.  When several
choices match, only the first moves; the others keep their order.  A
newly added choice follows that entry's shared adjustments, while
existing choices keep their individual tuning.  Saving makes inherited
entries complete local entries.

   This review changes the entries once.  Later edits are independent,
and names inherited in the future do not automatically acquire the
chosen physical voice.

   ‘w’ saves the review and applies the palette to both streams; ‘c’
saves without selection or application.  Startup is separate.  ‘C-c C-k’
cancels an unsaved review without writing; ‘q’ returns to the browser
but retains it.  Its actions menu offers _Resume palette voice review_.

   Use ‘r’ to resolve other unsaved voice editors before continuing,
then refresh this review with ‘g’.  Refresh it after saved inputs change
as well.  If only some saving steps succeed, the original proposal
remains available for retry with the same action.

   A failure on one speech stream does not undo a successful palette
save.  Retry and inspect the result for each stream.  Selecting another
palette ends that pending retry; to use the saved palette later, select
it deliberately through Voice Palettes.  _Close saved result_ closes a
completed review without undoing its data or changing the selection.


File: emacsvox.info,  Node: Voice Adjustments,  Next: Voice Fallbacks,  Prev: Changing A Palette Physical Voice,  Up: Editing Named Voices

8.7.5 Understand Voice Adjustments
----------------------------------

Change one field at a time and compare with ‘B’.  Portable controls are
not measurements in Hertz or decibels, and equal values may sound
different on different engines.  Most numeric fields use integer levels
zero through nine; Relative Rate is the exception.

   • _Relative Rate_ ranges from -20 to 20.  Negative requests slower
     speech, positive faster, and zero leaves base rate unchanged.
     Engine-relative steps are not words per minute.  A stronger
     contextual value replaces rather than adds to this one.
   • _Average Pitch_ requests lower or higher overall pitch.  Start with
     a small contrast between voices for related content.
   • _Pitch Range_ requests less or more pitch variation; supported low
     settings can sound monotone and higher settings more varied.
   • _Stress_ requests emphasis according to the engine's
     implementation.
   • _Richness_ changes tonal character or fullness where supported.
     Compare samples rather than treating it as volume.
   • _Gain_ changes output level: five is unchanged, lower reduces,
     higher raises.
   • _Pan_ requests left at zero, centre at five, and right at nine
     where supported; output arrangement also matters.
   • _Low-pass Amount_ and _High-pass Amount_ remove high and low
     frequencies, respectively.  Zero is neutral and nine strongest.
   • _Reverb_ adds reverberation, _Echo_ delayed repeats, and _Chorus_ a
     doubled voice.  Zero disables each; higher values increase it.
   • _Portable Fallback Family_, where offered, is a compatibility hint.
     Use _Physical voice_ for an installed choice.  Family is outside
     complete Omnivox voice preview.

   In shared settings, ‘d’ or blank input requests _adapter default_ and
‘u’ undoes it.  For a physical choice, ‘i’ means _use the shared value_.
Zero is explicit in either context.  The standard-default palette
chooses the collection, Automatic delegates physical selection, and
adapter default delegates one adjustment: they answer different
questions.

   Filter amount scales apply to arrows and numeric input in the common
editor.  Zero is the neutral cutoff; blank requests adapter default.
Existing saved styles retain their sound and stored low-pass cutoff
values keep their original meaning.

   Neutral values are named: gain five _unchanged_, pan five _centre_,
and zero reverb, echo, or chorus _disabled_.  Chorus adds bounded
doubled-voice modulation without changing speech duration or marker
positions.  Omnivox applies it to buffered audio and reports unsupported
routes.  Portable levels are translated into adapter-specific values.

   The standard palette includes ‘telephone’, combining high-pass and
low-pass filtering for a narrow-band sound on buffered Omnivox engines
without replacing the physical voice or changing duration/markers.
Preview it in Voice Palettes or Workbench before assignment.  Adapters
without post-synthesis filtering keep the style but cannot apply those
filters.

   For overall Omnivox pitch contrast
(‘omnivox-average-pitch-contrast’), open ‘C-e H v o’ and select _Omnivox
pitch contrast_.  Shift-left/right adjusts by ‘0.1’; ‘<RET>’ enters an
exact value from zero to two.  The default ‘0.5’ gives gentler
distinctions, ‘1.0’ restores historical contrast, and zero neutralises
average pitch.  This scales all average-pitch levels without changing
individual named-voice definitions; other backends are unaffected.

   Changes refresh logical-voice registration for subsequent previews,
structured speech and legacy inline voices.  Compare familiar content,
then restore the previous value if needed.  The menu's ‘w’ saves both
contrast and comparison labels through Customize; it does not save a
voice or palette.


File: emacsvox.info,  Node: Voice Fallbacks,  Next: Voices In Context,  Prev: Voice Adjustments,  Up: Editing Named Voices

8.7.6 Choose And Tune Fallback Voices
-------------------------------------

Shared settings provide a base for the whole voice.  Physical choices
are tried in displayed order, subject to availability, engine policy,
and synthesis failure.  An empty explicit list means _Automatic_, using
shared engine policy.  Availability predicts a possibility; the last
sample's result identifies what played.

   Palette inheritance supplies the whole entry.  Within that entry,
each physical choice can either inherit a shared adjustment or supply
its own.  For example, suppose shared pitch is six, this physical choice
specifies four, and a matching contextual rule requests seven.  The
resulting request is seven because the rule overrides the choice.
Without that rule it is four.  Selecting _Use shared value_ for the
choice then makes it six.

   Expand _Fallbacks_ to distinguish rows using shared settings from
customised ones.  A row's menu auditions or compares it; comparison
follows the same row after reordering, even where two rows use the same
physical voice.  A newly added row has no original yet: audition it or
compare the whole voice.  Replacing a customised physical choice asks
whether to keep tuning or return to shared.

   _Customize this voice_ opens individual tuning.  Each field
distinguishes shared, custom, and explicit adapter default.  ‘<RET>’
offers all three, left/right sets numbers, ‘d’ requests adapter default,
and ‘i’ inherits.  Zero remains explicit.  Later shared changes affect
inherited fields only.  _More adjustments and effects_ reveals
additional controls.  Automatic samples name the value/state and
audition that row; _Play whole fallback chain_ tests normal selection.
_Return to shared settings_ leaves individual tuning within the same
draft and Save operation.  Removing a row disables its controls until
undo or return to shared settings.

   With per-choice support, complete previews retain the chain and each
row's tuning.  _Last sample playback started on …_ identifies the
engine/voice whose audio started.  Accepted audio or spoken _Original_
and _Edited_ labels do not prove a start.  Stop, text changes, draft
changes, or context changes retire pending previews; retained evidence
is labelled _Earlier preview_.  Saving and application status are
separate from playback evidence.

   _Details and last playback evidence_ explains shared, row, and
contextual contributions to the confirmed sample.  The context view
offers _Where the last sample's settings came from_.  These explanations
use captured settings despite later edits, identify original versus
edited, and distinguish inherited values, defaults, zero, and
unsupported requests.  They describe requested settings, not measured
native parameters or sound.

   Older servers provide shared-only previews.  Customised complete
previews need per-choice support; individual auditions are available
only if tuning can be represented faithfully, including defaults.
Saving remains possible when a preview is unsupported.

  1. Work with engine-specific parameters

     _Engine-specific settings_ opens parameters described by the
     selected worker within the same voice draft.  A worker that can
     describe but not apply them shows them read-only.  Engine-wide and
     startup controls also remain read-only here, with scope identified
     by Help.

     Actions precede expandable parameter groups with value, source, and
     range.  ‘<RET>’ offers _Set value_, _Follow common settings_,
     _Engine voice default_, and _Parameter help_.  Units and limits
     belong to the engine.  Help explains default source, relevant
     common controls, and reported side effects.  ‘?’ opens and reads
     parameter Help; ‘q’ returns to the row.  Left/right steps a set
     numeric value within limits.  For inherited or unknown defaults,
     first choose a value with ‘<RET>’; the editor does not guess
     current native state.

     ‘i’ removes an override, ‘d’ requests engine voice default, and
     _Reset engine-specific settings_ removes native overrides without
     altering common adjustments or other choices.  ‘u’ undoes these
     edits.

     Automatic samples name the parameter/value and share the common
     editor's _Automatic sample after adjustment_ preference.  Opening,
     searching, refreshing, and Help do not audition.  ‘P’ previews, ‘B’
     compares, and the normal Save keeps the draft.  ‘q’ returns to
     common settings.  _Search controls_ searches names, identifiers,
     and Help across folded groups; empty input clears the filter.
     _Adjusted controls only_ shows overrides.  ‘g’ refreshes without
     restarting; a busy engine is retried quietly for up to five
     seconds.  Leaving cancels retry.  If still busy, finish speech and
     retry ‘g’.  Unrecognised saved fields stay visible for explicit
     removal.

     _Explain planned settings_ describes the captured draft without
     speech playback.  Where supported, last-playback details also
     explain applied sample settings.  Planned, applied, and
     engine-read-back values are labelled separately.  An expired sample
     plan cannot be reconstructed by these queries.


File: emacsvox.info,  Node: Voices In Context,  Prev: Voice Fallbacks,  Up: Editing Named Voices

8.7.7 Inspect Or Adjust A Voice In Context
------------------------------------------

In the voice editor, choose _Effective sound / Where settings came
from_.  It uses the attached source item captured through the entry
route or voice list.  If none is attached, choose an ordinary buffer at
its current point.  The item must provide inspectable facts or valid
retained feedback.  _Choose another source context_ deliberately selects
a replacement.

   This simulates captured facts and rules, showing the selected voice,
matching rules, requested fields, and their sources.  One rule can set
pitch and another richness.  A stronger pitch rule can mask a base edit;
a rule choosing a different named voice can make the open entry
irrelevant.  Competing relative rates also replace, not add.

   ‘P’ previews the edited voice in context and ‘B’ compares
original/edited with the same facts, rules, and text.  These voice-only
previews omit cues and spatial placement.  Family remains a portable
fallback outside full preview.  Per-choice support applies the actual
fallback's tuning before context, and an explicit context value
overrides even when numerically equal to shared tuning.  Requested
values remain distinct from confirmed starts, accepted audio, and
unsupported adjustments.  Changed/deleted sources are rejected.  After
saving a rule, refresh facts and rules from the source to capture its
contribution.  ‘q’ returns to the base editor.

   _Adjust for this context_ presents matching criteria and personal,
session, or buffer lifetime.  Choose one adjustment and value to prepare
the ordinary rule editor.  A richness-only change does not select a
different voice.  Review criteria with ‘<RET>’, use ‘V’ for another
voice field, and save the chosen scope with ‘w’.  The palette is
unchanged.  Finish existing rule-editor work before preparing another
contextual rule.

   _Inherit_ removes a field so weaker contributions can supply it;
removing the last voice field removes the patch.  Existing explicit
defaults are shown and preserved when another field is edited.  Zero is
not inheritance.  Some older default forms cannot reset underlying
tuning on every backend; inspect preview and explanation.  Context
changes are saved as rules, not as a palette entry containing the
combined result.  The *note Developer Guide: (emacsvox)Integration
Maintenance. describes representation and compatibility.


File: emacsvox.info,  Node: Emoji Speech Names,  Next: Installed Voices And Engines,  Prev: Editing Named Voices,  Up: Aural Presentation

8.8 Name Occasional Emoji In Speech
===================================

An emoji can require fallback from a voice such as Eloquence.  Naming
transforms speech text before engine selection: ‘Forecast 🔮’ becomes
‘Forecast crystal ball’, while source text stays intact.  This can keep
the preferred voice when the remaining text is compatible; normal
repertoire checks still decide.

   Naming is enabled by default.  Use ‘M-x customize-group <RET>
emacsvox-emoji <RET>’.  Disable ‘emacsvox-emoji-naming-enabled’ globally
or only in a source buffer:

     (setq-local emacsvox-emoji-naming-enabled nil)

   For Org buffers in your configuration:

     (add-hook 'org-mode-hook
               (lambda () (setq-local emacsvox-emoji-naming-enabled nil)))

   All four options support buffer-local values, which override global
defaults.  Aural previews use the remembered source policy, and
contextual previews its captured policy.  Changes during playback do not
change an already submitted sample.

   ‘emacsvox-emoji-approved-sequences’ defaults to ‘t’ for every exact
name in Emacs's emoji table, including seedling ‘🌱’, flags, keycaps,
and joined emoji.  You need not approve each separately; an explicit
list restricts eligible complete sequences.  Unknown joined sequences
remain intact even if a component is named.

   Arrows and harpoons also receive names in all directions, including
diagonal, double, and curved forms.  ‘← ↑ → ↓’ becomes _left arrow up
arrow right arrow down arrow_.  Symbols such as ‘⇒’ and ‘⟶’ outside the
emoji table use Unicode names.  Text/emoji selectors are supported;
unknown joined or accented sequences remain unchanged.  Box-drawing
characters use Unicode names too: ‘│’ becomes _box drawings light
vertical_.  Light, heavy, double, rounded, dashed, and junction forms
follow the same naming settings and limits; custom names can shorten
frequent ones.  Foreign-language letters and unrelated symbols retain
normal fallback, and exact character inspection is unchanged.

   ‘emacsvox-emoji-maximum-count’ defaults to 64 occurrences, counting
repetition.  ‘🤔Dinner? 🍕🍕🍕’ becomes _thinking face Dinner?  pizza
pizza pizza_ without fallback caused by those four emoji.  Above the
limit, naming is skipped for the whole item.  One captured presentation
can contain several voice runs; a standalone preview is one item.
Transport chunk sizes do not change this limit.  Sequences crossing
style or positioned-cue boundaries remain unchanged.

   Names come from Emacs's bundled emoji/Unicode data and may vary with
its version.  Arrows prefer emoji names where available; the four plain
arrows share their emoji equivalents' names.  Missing data leaves the
sequence to normal fallback; nothing is downloaded.  Names are English
and apply even to engines capable of speaking the original.

   Explicit pronunciation entries take precedence.  For eligible
sequences, ‘emacsvox-emoji-custom-names’ overrides a bundled name:

     (setq-local emacsvox-emoji-custom-names '(("🔮" . "prediction")))

   Custom names must contain 1–80 characters with no control characters.
Empty names are invalid; use explicit pronunciation to omit content.
Restriction and custom-name lists allow at most 64 entries, with
sequences up to 32 characters; full-table mode has no entry limit.  The
occurrence limit accepts 0–64.  Invalid settings skip automatic naming
with an explanation rather than stop speech.

   Feedback Details (‘C-e E’) shows original sequences and names or
reasons for skipping.  Voice-editor playback details report sample
substitutions.  These are prepared-text evidence, not confirmation of
what you heard.  _Play original_ retains captured speech text after
settings change.  Rule proposals change presentation while retaining
that text; capture new source feedback to try another naming policy.


File: emacsvox.info,  Node: Installed Voices And Engines,  Next: Sounds And Space,  Prev: Emoji Speech Names,  Up: Aural Presentation

8.9 Browse Installed Voices And Choose Engines
==============================================

Open ‘C-e H v’ for _Voices and engines_.  ‘b’ browses engines and
voices, ‘p’ palettes, and ‘w’ resumes Voice Workbench.  Browser ‘q’
commands unwind child views and return to the originating menu item.
Filters, selection, and staged work survive; unsaved engine-policy
changes are still announced.  A directly opened browser retains its
ordinary return destination.

   The main Workbench views are:

‘e’
     Engines, for trying voices or inspecting availability.
‘v’
     Physical voices, including engine voices and Omnivox downloads; on
     an engine row, restrict to that engine.
‘l’
     Named voices, labelled Logical voices, such as bolden and annotate.
‘s’
     Styles and effects for those entries, including supported
     parameters.

   From the engine browser at ‘C-e H v b’, ‘v’ opens voices.  ‘<RET>’ on
an Omnivox engine opens status, downloads, and settings; other adapters
use it to browse voices directly.  ‘<RET>’ or ‘P’ on a voice auditions,
‘x’ shows details, and ‘t’ opens an experiment.  ‘q’ returns through the
engine's details and list as appropriate before leaving Workbench.

   Filters set with ‘F’ and each view's row/column survive navigation
through settings menus.  The browsed engine is a temporary limit,
removed when switching to logical, style, or engine views, or pressing
‘v’ from a voice list.  Explicit filters remain; ‘C’ clears them to show
voices across engines.  Direct view shortcuts remain available.

* Menu:

* Experiment With An Installed Voice: Physical Voice Experiments.  Try physical voices without saving, then keep a useful experiment.
* Walkthrough Keep An Installed Voice Experiment: Keeping A Voice Experiment.  Place an experiment in a named voice and verify the saved combination.
* Set Shared Engine Preferences: Speech Engine Preferences.  Choose engine order and distinguish it from explicit palette choices.

8.9.1 Find and filter installed voices
--------------------------------------

A language with one voice shows it directly; several voices form an
expandable heading with count and state.  ‘<RET>’ toggles it.  Initial
sorting ignores capitalisation.  Regions form a tree, for example
English then United Kingdom then English (West Midlands).  Redundant
single-child levels are omitted; private language-tag codes do not
create extra headings.  Unknown codes stay visible and missing language
is grouped as _Unknown language_.

   A one-language list starts expanded.  Each engine scope remembers
folds; refresh preserves voice and column as library data arrives.
Sorting keeps headings with their voices.  ‘A’ samples usable visible
voices, including single-voice languages, in display order.  Expand a
group to include hidden voices.

   ‘/’ searches names across collapsed languages and disabled downloads
within the current engine and explicit filters.  Choosing expands its
language and selects it.  A hiding quick filter switches to _All
voices_.  Search neither plays a sample nor changes enablement.

   Omnivox quick filters are available through ‘Q’ with counts, or
directly:

1
     All reported engine and installed library voices.
2
     Catalogue downloads, including disabled ones; imports and built-ins
     remain in All voices.
3
     Library voices enabled for the next Apply.
4
     Needs Apply: desired enablement differs from either worker's
     eligibility.  Unknown or stale worker evidence cannot establish a
     pending change and is counted separately.

   Counts include collapsed groups and respect engine/explicit filters.
Each quick filter remembers row/column.  After Apply empties _Needs
Apply_, ‘2’ returns to downloads for previewing or editing.  Engine
overrides may prevent confirmation; _Active_ and engine details still
report health and usability.  ‘C’ clears the quick filter as well.

8.9.2 Distinguish installation, enablement, and active speech
-------------------------------------------------------------

A voice can be installed without being ready to speak.  _Installed_
means it belongs to the library or is supplied by an engine.  _Enabled_
records whether it should be available after the next Apply.  _Active_
identifies eligibility on the main stream, notification stream or both;
it does not say that a model is currently resident in memory.

   Library information arrives asynchronously.  When worker evidence is
missing or inconsistent, the view reports _Unknown_ rather than guessing
whether the voice is active.

   ‘+’ enables/disables a managed voice, ‘a’ reviews Apply, and ‘d’
opens downloads.  Downloads start disabled; enable and Apply before
auditioning.  Engine-managed voices retain their own selection
mechanism.  Other adapters retain their physical-inventory view.

   To remove a downloaded Piper, Flite, or MBROLA package, disable its
voices, Apply, then press ‘C-c u’ on one voice.  Review all speakers
sharing it, size, known palette references, and retained-file reasons.
Confirmation removes only reviewed managed files.  Built-in voices,
imports, and runtimes remain, as do saved palette choices for a later
redownload.  The backend guide describes supported managed-engine
download and removal boundaries in more detail.

   Another session may still need the package; files remain until
retirement is confirmed, and older session records may need inspection.
Results distinguish removed bytes, remaining files, and unconfirmed
amounts.  ‘C-c U’ or ‘omnivox-library-resume-uninstall’ reviews
interrupted cleanup on hosts supporting uninstallation.

   Check an engine's _Availability_ before changing its routing policy.
A missing runtime remains listed as unavailable, with _Last failure_
explaining the problem.  _Routing policy: allowed_ permits selection; it
does not install the runtime.  An engine being disabled, unhealthy or
temporarily failed are also distinct conditions.

8.9.3 Compare samples and interpret the result
----------------------------------------------

‘A’ auditions available matching voices in displayed order, skipping
unavailable rows.  ‘B’ uses the selected voice as A and asks for B;
_Search all engines_ can compare outside current filters.  Both use one
sentence.  ‘T’ edits it, ‘F’ filters, ‘C’ clears filters, and ‘R’
refreshes inventory.

   Use ‘C-e H v o l’ to choose comparison labels: engine and voice
names, letters or sequence numbers, or no labels.  The option is
‘emacsvox-aural-preview-label-verbosity’.  The list starts on the
current choice.  Arrows browse without applying; ‘<RET>’ applies and
returns, while ‘q’ cancels.  This global option changes labels, leaving
sample text and order intact.  With no labels, the sequence no longer
identifies its voices aloud.  Press ‘w’ in Voice presentation to save
labels and pitch contrast through Customize.

   Labels and samples form one sequence: ‘S’ or foreground navigation
cancels remaining playback.  Exact-preview failure is reported rather
than accepting a substitute as the requested voice.  Capabilities
describe adapter claims, not a listening test of every parameter.


File: emacsvox.info,  Node: Physical Voice Experiments,  Next: Keeping A Voice Experiment,  Up: Installed Voices And Engines

8.9.4 Experiment With An Installed Voice
----------------------------------------

‘t’ on a physical voice opens an experiment with that exact
engine/voice, adapter-default characteristics, current base rate, and
neutral audio effects.  Opening, adjusting, and comparing save nothing.
Controls match *note Edit a Named Voice: (emacsvox)Editing Named
Voices., but ‘w’ must first choose a palette destination.

   To compare pitch:

  1. Open ‘C-e H v b’, select an available engine, and press ‘v’.
     Select a physical voice and press ‘P’; ‘B’ can compare with another
     available voice using the same sentence.
  2. Press ‘t’, set _Average Pitch_ to ‘3’, listen, then set ‘7’ and
     listen again.  An unsupported pitch control remains identified.
  3. ‘B’ compares the captured starting voice with the edited one, not
     the immediately previous change.  ‘u’ undoes; ‘d’ restores adapter
     default.
  4. Finish with ‘q’ and discard, or keep the experiment using the next
     walkthrough.

   ‘w’ chooses a destination palette and entry.  The default keeps
_Physical voice and adjustments — this choice_.  Alternatives keep only
the physical voice, only one row's adjustments, or shared settings for
inheriting choices.  Physical voices can replace a row, become
preferred, or append as fallback.  A row-level keep replaces that row's
tuning with the experiment's values and defaults, preventing unrelated
destination shared tuning from changing the base sound.  Use ‘i’
afterward for fields intended to inherit.

   Other rows/shared settings survive a row-level keep.  Replacing only
the physical voice asks whether to keep/reset its tuning.  Shared
adjustment keeps affect inherited fields; custom rows override them.  An
existing destination draft can accept the changes or resume untouched,
retaining other work and pending save recovery.  The destination preview
uses the proposed combination, which can differ because of
scope/fallback order.  Choosing it has not saved.


File: emacsvox.info,  Node: Keeping A Voice Experiment,  Next: Speech Engine Preferences,  Prev: Physical Voice Experiments,  Up: Installed Voices And Engines

8.9.5 Walkthrough: Keep An Installed Voice Experiment
-----------------------------------------------------

Create ‘reading-demo’ as in *note Make a personal reading palette:
(emacsvox)Personal Palette Walkthrough., or deliberately choose an
existing personal destination.  Note the active palette and whether
selection follows the standard baseline or is explicit.  Saving into an
existing destination changes that resource; it is not preview-only
practice.

  1. In the voice browser, open ‘t’, adjust a field, and preview with
     ‘P~/~B’.  Press ‘w’ to keep it.
  2. Select the personal destination and named voice ‘bolden’.  Choose
     _Physical voice and adjustments — this choice_, then _Add as
     preferred_.  This places the experiment first while retaining
     alternatives.
  3. The common editor opens an unsaved proposal.  Expand _Fallbacks_:
     the new first choice is customised, preceding previous explicit
     choices.  ‘P’ plays the whole proposed voice; inspect its actual
     reported engine/voice because an unavailable preference may fall
     back.
  4. ‘w’ saves and applies.  Wait for its result and follow *note
     Recovering Unfinished Changes: (emacsvox)Recovering Aural Changes.
     after partial saving or failed application.  Success selects the
     destination for this session.  Use ‘U’ in Voice Palettes only to
     choose it for startup too.
  5. Before saving, undo or discard to reverse.  After saving, reopen
     the voice, expand fallbacks, choose _Remove_ in the added row's
     ‘<RET>’ action menu, preview, and save.  Other choices and shared
     tuning remain.  To restore session selection, use ‘f’ in Voice
     Palettes if you originally followed the standard baseline;
     otherwise activate the recorded explicit palette.

   For independent fallback tuning, open its menu and _Customize this
voice_.  Change one field, audition that row, then test _Play whole
fallback chain_.  The custom field overrides shared tuning only for that
choice.  ‘i’ restores inheritance; reordering carries adjustments with
the row.


File: emacsvox.info,  Node: Speech Engine Preferences,  Prev: Keeping A Voice Experiment,  Up: Installed Voices And Engines

8.9.6 Set Shared Engine Preferences
-----------------------------------

Engine preference is global, not buffer-local.  In ‘C-e H v’, ‘e’
chooses a session preference, ‘S’ chooses and saves one, and ‘r’
restores saved or initial order.  Availability and unsaved-policy checks
remain.  The speech-settings scope does not change these saving rules.

  1. Edit Saved Engine Configuration

     From engine details choose _Engine configuration_, or run ‘M-x
     omnivox-engine-configuration’.  This needs an updated local Omnivox
     host.  Files belong to the speech host, including Windows under
     WSL; entered paths must make sense there.

     ‘<RET>’ edits a row: enabled engines, preferred/backup order, or
     automatic-selection permission for a helper.  _Advanced startup
     settings_ exposes literal arguments and wait limits.  ‘l’ changes
     engines while retaining drafts.  _Add installed helper_ registers a
     compatible program disabled; it neither downloads nor executes it.
     Enable, Save, then Apply to check runtime support.

     ‘s’ saves without changing speech.  A file conflict retains the
     draft for fresh review.  ‘g’ rereads saved settings, asking before
     discarding.  ‘q’ returns while retaining the draft.  Saved/draft
     states are distinct from worker availability, and explicit
     environment overrides remain visible.

     ‘A’ reviews and applies saved engine/library settings to both
     workers through preflight and rollback.  Save all edits first.
     _Check saved settings_ validates files only.  ‘P’ opens live voices
     in Workbench, so saved changes are audible there only after
     successful Apply.

  2. Choose The Shared Preferred Engine

     ‘C-e d e’ chooses an available engine for this session; ‘C-u C-e d
     e’ saves the preference.  With no saved policy, an ordinary first
     invocation uses live Omnivox policy as an in-memory baseline
     without writing.  The prefix saves that baseline and preference.
     Explicit named-voice physical choices outrank it.

     Workbench policy is shared across palettes.  ‘O’ toggles preferred
     engines and ‘[~/~]’ reorders them; ‘f’ toggles fallbacks and
     ‘{~/’}~ reorders them.  ‘D’ enables/disables and ‘K’ requests a
     recovery probe.  These are staged changes; ‘w’ saves and applies.
     Physical choices for an individual entry are saved in its voice
     editor.

     Names appear first in tables; ‘x’ details capabilities, native IDs,
     and registration.  Availability, failure, predicted selection, and
     observed playback are distinct.  Saving engine policy is separate
     from voice or profile saving.  For unconfirmed application, see
     *note Recovering Unfinished Changes: (emacsvox)Recovering Aural
     Changes.  Installation belongs to *note Optional Omnivox Engines:
     (emacsvox)Optional Omnivox Engine Modules.


File: emacsvox.info,  Node: Sounds And Space,  Next: Aural Speech Output,  Prev: Installed Voices And Engines,  Up: Aural Presentation

8.10 Sounds, Tones, And Spatial Cues
====================================

Rules request cues by semantic name.  Sound packs map names to audio
assets and can inherit missing cues.  This lets you change the sound of
a family of cues without rewriting rules.  For one event, use its
feedback rule instead.

   To enable or disable earcons in the captured buffer, use ‘C-e H r l
e’.  In that submenu, ‘E’ changes the global default for buffers without
a local override; ‘i’ removes the source buffer's override so it follows
that default.  Speech remains available when earcons are off, but menu
cues also disappear where the off setting applies.  These switches last
for the session or buffer and have no Customize Save action.  Toggle
again or restore inheritance to reverse an experiment.

   ‘C-e H o p’ opens _Sound Packs_.  Its ‘q’ returns to _Sounds and
output_ with the same settings scope, and another ‘q’ returns to the
main menu.  Directly opened browsers keep their usual returns.

‘P’
     Audition a representative cue without activating the pack.
‘RET’
     Browse individual cues and their source or fallback.
‘P in the cue browser’
     Audition the selected concrete cue; RET shows details.
‘v’
     Validate resources and report missing/invalid entries.
‘a’
     Activate the selected pack.

   Spatial requests describe placement portably, not a particular
device.  ‘C-e H D s’ reports capabilities and fallbacks; ‘C-e H o n’
controls output, separation, direction, speech, and cues.  Unsupported
or mono output retains audible content at the centre.  Named tones are
separate compact resources, independent of sampled files and the active
pack.

   For waveform, pitch sweep and envelope adjustments, use the *note
complete spelling tone editor: spelling-tone-editor.  It currently edits
the global spelling cue.  Line cues, capitalisation beeps and
indentation keep the controls and saving rules described in their own
sections; they do not yet use that draft editor.

* Menu:

* Walkthrough Try A Sound Pack: Sound Pack Walkthrough.  Preview, activate, retain, and restore a sound pack.
* Walkthrough Silence One Cue: Changing One Sound.  Suppress a cue while retaining speech and restore it afterward.
* Change A Blank-Line Tone: Changing Tones.  Change a recorded tone and distinguish it from a sampled cue.
* Adjust Spatial Presentation: Spatial Presentation Controls.  Check stereo support, adjust placement, and restore centered output.
* Inspect And Maintain A Personal Sound Pack: Personal Sound Packs.  Understand pack metadata, discovery, and the limits of the manager.

8.10.1 Tune line and boundary cues from the menu
------------------------------------------------

‘C-e H o c’ opens _Line and boundary cues_.  Blank, whitespace-only,
separator, decorated, and unspeakable-line tones have pitch and
integer-duration controls.  Shift-left/right adjusts by 25 Hz or 5
milliseconds; ‘<RET>’ enters exactly.  Boundary/warning and large
movement instead choose replacement earcons.  Changing those named
earcons affects every use, including warnings elsewhere.

   Each cue retains a global preview draft.  ‘<SPC>’ auditions it, ‘s’
stops, ‘n’ silences in the draft, ‘r’ restores bundled values, and ‘D’
discards edits.  ‘S’ saves through personal rules while preserving
unrelated rules; stronger presentation rules may override it.  ‘q’
retains the draft.  This changes cue output, not classification of
separators or decorated lines.


File: emacsvox.info,  Node: Sound Pack Walkthrough,  Next: Changing One Sound,  Up: Sounds And Space

8.10.2 Walkthrough: Try A Sound Pack
------------------------------------

  1. Open ‘C-e H o p’ and note the active pack.  Select another of kind
     _sound_ and press ‘P’ to preview without activating.
  2. Enter with ‘<RET>’ and inspect _Availability_ and _Provider_.  Cues
     may come from the pack, parent, or registered fallback.  ‘P’
     previews and ‘S’ stops.
  3. Press ‘s’ to return to the manager, then ‘v’ to validate.  Resolve
     missing files or cues before relying on the pack.  Activate with
     ‘a’, return through the browser/menu, and repeat a familiar action
     such as entering a list.
  4. To retain the selection, open ‘C-e H p p’ for profiles.  ‘N’
     captures a new setup and ‘w’ deliberately updates the selected
     profile.  Review all fields: this saves more than the sound-pack
     selection.  See *note Save, Switch, and Share Your Setup:
     (emacsvox)Presentation Profiles.
  5. Restore the original pack by activating it.  If the experiment was
     saved in a profile, restore/update that profile too.  Preview alone
     needs no undo.

   For one unwanted cue, change its rule rather than switching every cue
in a pack.


File: emacsvox.info,  Node: Changing One Sound,  Next: Changing Tones,  Prev: Sound Pack Walkthrough,  Up: Sounds And Space

8.10.3 Walkthrough: Silence One Cue
-----------------------------------

  1. Repeat the action producing the cue, then open ‘C-e H f h’.  Select
     the record and ‘<RET>’ to explain it.  Identify cue name and
     before/after phase.
  2. Return to the list and press ‘C’ for _Change this feedback_.
     Select the relevant part if needed, then ‘c’, _Suppress one
     component_, and the cue.
  3. Compare ‘O~/~P’: speech and other components should remain.
     Inspect criteria with ‘m’, then choose _This Emacs session_ through
     ‘l’.
  4. Apply with ‘a’ and repeat the source action.  Matching feedback
     should omit that cue; other identities or unmatched contexts remain
     unchanged.
  5. Open ‘C-e H r o’, find the session rule, and disable with ‘t’ or
     delete with ‘d’.  It also expires when Emacs exits.

   To add a missing cue, choose _Add a sound_, _Before content_ or
_After content_, then a registered cue.  Preview and review
criteria/lifetime.  Adding and later removing use the same rule
workflow.


File: emacsvox.info,  Node: Changing Tones,  Next: Spatial Presentation Controls,  Prev: Changing One Sound,  Up: Sounds And Space

8.10.4 Change A Blank-Line Tone
-------------------------------

This changes a tone actually present in recorded feedback, not every
visually blank line regardless of its presentation.

  1. Navigate onto a blank line and open ‘C-e H f h’.  Select its
     record, press ‘C’, and choose a part if necessary.
  2. Press ‘c’, _Change a tone_, select the tone, and choose _Pitch and
     duration_.  Try ‘500’ Hertz and ‘80’ milliseconds.  If it
     originally lay between other components, choose _Before the other
     feedback_ when asked about placement.  Review that movement in the
     summary as well as pitch/duration.
  3. Compare ‘O~/~P’.  Other content should remain.  A named tone can be
     selected instead of numeric values.
  4. Review criteria with ‘m’, choose session lifetime with ‘l’, then
     ‘a’.  Visit another matching blank line to hear the live result.
  5. Disable/delete the session rule through ‘C-e H r o’ to restore the
     earlier behaviour.  It also expires when Emacs exits.

   If the record contains a sampled cue, _Change a tone_ is unavailable.
Explain it, then suppress that cue and add a tone with separate rules if
appropriate; remove both to restore.  If no record exists, check the
history limit and repeat the action.  The interface cannot infer a
missing component from appearance.


File: emacsvox.info,  Node: Spatial Presentation Controls,  Next: Personal Sound Packs,  Prev: Changing Tones,  Up: Sounds And Space

8.10.5 Adjust Spatial Presentation
----------------------------------

Open ‘C-e H D s’ to inspect reported speech/cue capabilities, then
return to the menu.  ‘C-e H o n’ opens _Notifications and spatial
output_.  Enable presentation, speech, and cues independently.  Output
follows capabilities or can be declared mono.  Separation ranges from
zero (centred) to one (full); direction can be normal, reversed, or
centred for some/all requests.

   These spatial controls apply immediately and are global.
Shift-left/right adjusts selected separation by 0.1; ‘<RET>’ enters an
exact value.  ‘<SPC>’ requests left then right cue previews; mono or
limited capabilities may centre them.  ‘x’ stops.  ‘S’ saves spatial and
routing defaults without restarting speech, separately from
output-volume saving.

   For a gentler layout, note the current settings, select maximum
separation, and enter ‘0.3’.  Repeat feedback that requests spatial
placement.  Stereo headphones help compare it.  If everything stays
centred, Explain the source to check its placement request and inspect
backend capability.  A sample without a placement request cannot
demonstrate separation.

   For comparison, select mono and repeat the action; content remains
audible.  Restore the original values afterward.  Save deliberately with
‘S’ or capture spatial choices in a Presentation Profile.  A voice's Pan
effect and notification-device routing are separate from presentation
placement.


File: emacsvox.info,  Node: Personal Sound Packs,  Prev: Spatial Presentation Controls,  Up: Sounds And Space

8.10.6 Inspect And Maintain A Personal Sound Pack
-------------------------------------------------

In the manager, ‘o’ opens the directory and ‘g’ rescans.  For a
discovered personal pack, ‘e’ edits summary, parent, coverage profiles,
and default spatialisation.  Built-ins are read-only.  Editing metadata
saves immediately; this is not a retained voice-editor draft.

   Coverage profiles declare cue sets for validation, not working
Presentation Profiles.  Native assets belong to the pack; effective ones
include inherited and fallback cues.  Validation and details explain
those sources.

   This manager does not record/edit audio or create a sound pack
through a New Palette-style wizard.  New packs need audio files and
resource metadata; see *note Authoring Sound Packs: (emacsvox)Authoring
Sound Packs. in the Developer Guide.  For a change to when a cue plays,
use the feedback-rule workflow.


File: emacsvox.info,  Node: Aural Speech Output,  Next: Presentation Profiles,  Prev: Sounds And Space,  Up: Aural Presentation

8.11 Speech Output And Notifications
====================================

These settings control reading detail and delivery.  They do not replace
the rules choosing content or the palette shaping its voice.  Start with
rate and punctuation for reading, routing for where notifications play,
or volumes for how loud the components are.  Each control has its own
scope and saving action; a capital ‘S’ in one menu does not save
everything described in this section.

* Menu:

* Set the reading scope, rate, and punctuation: Reading Scope And Punctuation.  Set rate or punctuation for the current buffer and understand saving.
* Understand the main and notification streams: Notification Routing.  Choose where notifications play and distinguish selection from restart.
* Adjust output volumes: Output Volumes.  Adjust speech and cue levels without confusing volume with routing.
* Adjust an integration's speech settings: Integration Speech Settings.  Find focused controls and their integration-specific saving boundaries.


File: emacsvox.info,  Node: Reading Scope And Punctuation,  Next: Notification Routing,  Up: Aural Speech Output

8.11.1 Set the reading scope, rate, and punctuation
---------------------------------------------------

‘C-e H s’ opens Speech settings for the captured buffer.  ‘g’ switches
to global defaults; ‘C-u C-e H’ starts there.  Indentation shares this
scope with its parents, including changes made inside its own menu.
Opening/closing alone saves nothing.

   Select _Rate_ and adjust with Shift-left/right by the configured
step, or use ‘<RET>~/~r’ for an exact value.  Speech must be running;
Omnivox uses 0–100.  Global rate changes also affect the current buffer.
A named voice's relative rate modifies this base as described in *note
Voice Adjustments: (emacsvox)Voice Adjustments.

   In Speech settings, press ‘p’ for the punctuation menu.  Choose ‘n’
for none, ‘s’ for some or ‘a’ for all punctuation.  That menu's ‘p’
offers the built-in levels and available named profiles.  In buffer
scope, ‘r’ restores automatic mode policy.  Press ‘q’ to return to
Speech settings.

   The punctuation menu's ‘e’ opens the Omnivox pronunciation editor.
In that editor, lowercase ‘s’ saves overrides on the speech host and
lowercase ‘r’ restarts speech after confirmation.  Its ‘q’ returns to
the same punctuation menu item, retaining the draft and selected
character for reopening.

   Capital ‘S’ in the punctuation menu has a different purpose: it saves
the selected level or profile through Customize.  It does not save the
pronunciation editor's host-side draft.

   ‘S’ in Speech settings saves displayed rate and punctuation as
startup defaults.  Rate uses the backend's startup option.  Saving from
buffer scope promotes those displayed choices to defaults.  Mode
punctuation policy and explicit buffer settings still override the saved
punctuation default.

  1. Use Emacs word and phrase dictionaries

     For word or phrase substitutions managed by Emacs, open ‘C-e H s p
     d’.  This menu is separate from the Omnivox pronunciation editor on
     ‘e’.  Its dictionaries are stored in an Emacs dictionary file, not
     Customize or the speech host, and changes need no speech restart.
     Opening the menu writes nothing and leaves the source's
     pronunciation table unchanged.

     Press ‘d’ to define an entry at the captured source item.  Choose
     buffer, file, directory or major-mode scope, then enter the term
     and spoken replacement.  The existing prompt can load saved
     dictionaries first.  Buffer entries last only in that buffer's
     current pronunciation table.  Other scopes update the in-memory
     dictionary collection and need a separate ‘w’ Save to persist.

     Use ‘e’ to choose an existing dictionary and open its widget
     editor.  ‘<TAB>’ moves between fields and buttons; ‘<RET>’
     activates them.  Field edits update the in-memory collection.  Its
     Save button writes dictionaries to a chosen file.  Return with ‘C-c
     C-c’ from anywhere, or ‘q’ outside editable fields; inside a field,
     ‘q’ inserts text.  Returning retains edits without saving or
     rolling them back, and reopening resumes the same editor buffer.

     Back in the menu, ‘r’ rebuilds and enables the captured source's
     table from its dictionaries.  This replaces temporary buffer-only
     entries.  ‘t’ turns substitution off or composes the table again
     when turning it on; turning off also loses buffer-only entries.
     Changes to file, directory and mode entries remain in the
     collection.  To reverse an entry change, edit it back and refresh
     the source; save again if you changed the saved dictionary.

     Finish and close existing widget editors before loading
     replacements: their retained fields are not reloaded.  The menu's
     ‘l’ loads a dictionary file into the collection; refresh the source
     afterward to use those entries.  ‘w’ saves file, directory and mode
     dictionaries, excluding buffer-only entries.  Both actions prompt
     for a file; Load offers ‘emacsvox-pronounce-dictionaries-file’,
     while Save offers its basename in the Emacsvox user directory.
     Saving elsewhere does not change the configured startup file.  Use
     ‘?’ followed by an item's key for its scope and effects before
     applying it.

  2. Adjust indentation feedback

     Open ‘C-e H s i’ or directly ‘C-e d I’.  In global-default scope,
     ‘S’ saves indentation defaults and tone volume through Customize.
     Select _Volume_ and adjust by five percentage points with
     Shift-left/right, or enter with ‘<RET>’.  Plain left/right changes
     sections and ‘<SPC>’ previews indentation.  Other selected items
     give guidance rather than treating every shifted arrow as volume.

     ‘t’ opens pitch-tone settings: base pitch, rise per column, maximum
     pitch, and requested duration.  Shift-left/right adjusts; ‘<RET>’
     enters exactly.  ‘<SPC>’ previews four columns without changing
     presentation or enabling feedback.  ‘s’ stops.  Short durations
     show the effective blank-line-tone minimum.  ‘R’ restores the four
     bundled values in scope.  Global changes preserve local overrides;
     ‘S’ saves global defaults and tone volume.  ‘q’ retains live
     changes.

  3. Set capitalisation feedback and its beeps

     ‘C-e H s c’ opens _Capitalization_.  ‘i’ toggles indication, ‘p’
     chooses spoken, tone, combined, personal-rule, or no presentation,
     and ‘c’ independently toggles camel-case splitting.  They share
     buffer/global scope on ‘g’.  ‘<SPC>’ previews reading; ‘S’ saves
     these three startup defaults.  Buffer overrides still apply.

     Within Capitalization, ‘l’ edits the capital-letter beep and ‘a’
     the all-caps beep.  Each has a separate global draft.  Select
     pitch/duration and adjust by 25 Hz/5 milliseconds with
     Shift-left/right, or use ‘<RET>’.  ‘<SPC>’ previews only the beep
     and ‘s’ stops.  Previewing neither changes reading settings nor
     enables capitalisation indication.

     In a beep editor, ‘S’ saves that cue globally for tone/combined
     presentation.  ‘r’ restores bundled values in the draft; save to
     remove this menu's override.  ‘D’ discards/rereads, ‘q’ returns,
     and ‘C-q’ closes menus while retaining drafts for this session.
     Unrelated rules and labels survive, and stronger rules may override
     the cue.  Finish unsaved personal-rule editors before cue saving.
     If saved rules changed elsewhere, discard the cue draft and reopen
     it.

  4. Choose spelling feedback

     ‘C-e H s f’ opens _Spelling feedback_.  These settings are global.
     Toggle ‘v’ for voice changes, ‘t’ for a tone, and ‘e’ for the help
     earcon.  You can combine them or turn all three off without
     disabling Flyspell checking and highlighting.  The default is voice
     plus earcon.

     When you read text already marked by Flyspell, tones and earcons
     occur at the flagged word.  Voice feedback uses the existing
     spelling face mappings.  Tone and earcon feedback also report
     errors detected while editing.  Character movement cues entry into
     a flagged word: its first character moving forwards, or its last
     character moving backwards.  Moving within the same word or onto an
     adjacent space does not repeat the entry cue.  Opening a mail reply
     checks and highlights inserted text without a burst of cues.

     Use ‘m’ to choose tone timing.  _Alongside speech_ is the default:
     it plays the tone while the word is spoken without adding a tone
     pause.  _Before speech_ finishes each tone before its flagged word,
     adding a pause.  Previously saved choices remain in effect.  Arrows
     browse without changing the setting; ‘<RET>’ applies and returns to
     Timing, while ‘q’ cancels.  Timing leaves voice and earcon feedback
     unchanged.  Compare it with ‘<SPC>’ or by reading flagged text: a
     tone-only preview or editing cue has no accompanying speech to
     overlap.

     Select ‘p’ for pitch, ‘d’ for duration, or ‘l’ for spelling tone
     volume.  ‘<RET>’ enters an exact value; Shift-left/right adjusts by
     25 Hz, 5 milliseconds, or 5 percentage points.  Spelling tone
     volume ranges from 0 to 100 percent: zero silences the tone, and
     the default of 100 preserves its original level.  ‘V’ changes the
     shared volume of all main-stream tones, including indentation and
     spelling.  The shared volume still scales the spelling tone after
     its individual adjustment; it is also available in Sounds and
     output.

     These controls change the current session immediately.  ‘b’
     previews the tone alone, even when tone feedback is off.  ‘<SPC>’
     reads a sample containing one flagged word with the selected
     combination.  ‘S’ saves the spelling settings, including tone
     timing and shape, and the shared tone volume through Customize.
     ‘q’ returns and ‘C-q’ closes the menus without saving.

       1. Edit the complete spelling tone

          Press capital ‘E’ in Spelling feedback, or use ‘C-e H s f E’,
          for a private draft of the complete tone.  The title
          identifies its global spelling scope.  This editor adds
          waveform, pitch sweep and envelope controls to pitch, duration
          and individual volume.  Unlike the parent menu's immediate
          adjustments, changes here affect only the draft until you
          apply it.  Timing stays in Spelling feedback at ‘C-e H s f m’.
          Voice, earcon and tone enablement also remain in that menu;
          shared main-tone volume controls all main tones from Sounds
          and output.

          The sections follow the editing task: the spelling tone's
          basic controls, Pitch movement, Envelope, Preview, Presets,
          Apply and save, then Finish.  Left/right changes sections;
          up/down selects an item.  Shift-left/right adjusts only a
          selected number.  ‘<RET>’ enters an exact number, toggles
          Pitch sweep, or opens the selected choice list.  In a list,
          up/down browses without changing the draft; ‘<RET>’ chooses
          and returns, while ‘q’ cancels.  Waveform and Envelope are
          choices, so shifted arrows do not adjust them.  ‘?’ followed
          by an item's key explains its effect, units and dependencies.
          Use ‘C-c l’ or the other *note menu review keys:
          settings-menu-review. to inspect an announcement without
          activating it.

            1. Try a tone without changing live feedback

                 1. Open ‘C-e H s f E’ and press capital ‘C’ to hear the
                    currently applied tone.  Press ‘<SPC>’ to hear the
                    draft, initially the same sound.  Both play the tone
                    alone, interrupting previous main speech.
                 2. Move to _Volume_ and lower this draft's individual
                    level with Shift-left if wanted.  This leaves other
                    tones and the live spelling cue unchanged.
                 3. Press ‘w’ to open Waveform.  Browse a choice, read
                    its Help, then press ‘<RET>’ to return with that
                    waveform in the draft.  Press ‘<SPC>’ to hear it.
                    For Pulse, adjust _Pulse width_ back in the editor,
                    then preview again.
                 4. Alternate ‘C’ for the applied tone and ‘<SPC>’ for
                    the draft.  Capital ‘P’ reads a spelling sample with
                    the draft tone, using the parent's current timing,
                    voice and earcon settings.  Use this to judge
                    whether the tone obscures the word.  Capital ‘S’
                    stops; navigation also interrupts playback.
                 5. Press ‘q’ to return and discard unapplied edits.
                    Reopening starts from the currently applied tone.
                    To keep the experiment instead, use the Apply, Save
                    or preset actions described below before leaving.

               These previews privately enable the tone even if live
               spelling tones are off.  They do not turn the live cue
               on, apply adjustments or save defaults.  A tone alone
               cannot demonstrate _Before speech_ versus _Alongside
               speech_; use capital ‘P’ for that comparison.  Shared
               tone volume still scales playback, so a shared volume of
               zero silences these previews too.  After Apply, ‘C’
               compares against the newly applied tone, not the editor's
               opening value.

            2. Shape the pitch and waveform

               ‘p’ sets pitch; Shift-left/right changes it by 25 Hz.
               Choose ‘w’ for waveform: sine is a smooth pure tone,
               triangle adds a softer harmonic character, and square
               sounds brighter.  Pulse offers adjustable width; Sawtooth
               has a bright, buzzy character.  Their perceived loudness
               can differ; compare at a comfortable individual volume.

               For Pulse, select ‘u’ for _Pulse width_.  This is the
               percentage of each cycle spent high: 50 percent sounds
               like Square.  Shift-left/right changes it by five
               percentage points within 5–95 percent, and ‘<RET>’ enters
               an exact value.  Only Pulse uses width.  Trying another
               waveform keeps the width in this draft, so you can return
               to Pulse and continue comparing.

               Toggle ‘t’ for _Pitch sweep_ to make pitch rise or fall
               during the tone.  The ordinary Pitch control sets the
               start; ‘f’ sets End pitch.  Shift-left/right changes the
               end by 25 Hz and ‘<RET>’ enters it exactly.  Equal start
               and end values keep constant pitch.  Turning the sweep
               off retains End pitch in the draft.  The sweep occupies
               the existing duration and does not change timing relative
               to speech.

               Pitch and End pitch range from 50 to 10000 Hz.  Duration
               ranges from 5 to 1000 milliseconds and steps by 5;
               individual Volume ranges from 0 to 100 percent and steps
               by 5 percentage points.  Zero volume silences this tone.
               Waveform changes can alter perceived loudness even at the
               same volume.

            3. Shape loudness with an envelope

               Choose ‘e’ for the envelope, which describes how loudness
               changes over the life of the tone.  _Classic_ uses the
               original short fades.  _ADSR_ uses four controls, with
               times in milliseconds and sustain in percent of peak
               level:

                  • ‘a’, Attack: time to rise from silence to peak
                    level.
                  • ‘c’, Decay: time to fall from peak to the sustain
                    level.
                  • ‘s’, Sustain: the level held for the remaining time,
                    rather than a duration.
                  • ‘r’, Release: time to fade from sustain to silence.

               For example, with Duration 100 milliseconds, Attack 10,
               Decay 20 and Release 30, the tone holds its Sustain level
               for the remaining 40 milliseconds.  A Sustain value of 40
               means 40 percent of peak loudness during that hold, not a
               40-millisecond duration.  A longer Attack softens the
               onset; a short Attack followed by a longer Decay can make
               a plucked sound.

               Shift-left/right changes each timed stage by 5
               milliseconds and Sustain by 5 percentage points.  ‘<RET>’
               accepts stage times from 0 to 60000 milliseconds and a
               Sustain level from 0 to 100 percent.  The duration of the
               whole tone remains the limit, even when a stage requests
               a much longer time.

               Total duration includes the whole envelope.  If attack,
               decay and release would exceed it, they shorten
               proportionally and leave no sustain hold.  Zero skips a
               timed stage; endpoint smoothing still limits clicks.
               Classic ignores these four controls, retaining their
               values while this draft remains open.  The initial
               waveform is sine with Classic fades.

               With ADSR, ‘k’ chooses the envelope curve.  _Linear_ uses
               straight ramps and preserves the original ADSR behaviour.
               _Exponential_ starts the attack more slowly and starts
               decay and release more quickly, while retaining the same
               stage durations and endpoint levels.  Arrows browse the
               choices; ‘<RET>’ selects and returns, and ‘q’ cancels.
               Classic ignores the curve but retains its choice in the
               draft.

               Preview after each small change with ‘<SPC>’ or its
               equivalent, ‘b’.  Capital ‘S’ stops immediately without
               waiting for the Release stage.  Compare a short tone with
               the spelling sample too: a shape that is clear alone can
               be masked by speech or make the word harder to hear.

            4. Reuse a tone through presets

               Capital ‘L’ opens presets.  Arrows browse without
               changing the draft; ‘<RET>’ loads a copy and returns,
               while ‘q’ cancels.  Soft tick, Rounded beep and Short
               pluck are starting points with different pitches and
               envelopes, each at 35 percent individual volume.  Use ‘?’
               followed by a preset's displayed key for its character.
               After loading it and returning to the editor, press
               ‘<SPC>’ to hear the draft.  The preset list does not
               preview while you browse.  Further adjustments leave the
               preset itself unchanged.

               Capital ‘N’ saves the draft under a name in your personal
               preset collection through Customize.  This does not apply
               the tone or save the spelling cue defaults.  Reusing a
               personal name asks before replacing it; bundled names
               cannot be replaced.  Saved presets appear alongside the
               bundled choices at ‘L’.

            5. Apply, save or discard the draft

               Use ‘A’ to apply the draft for this session.  Capital ‘W’
               saves and applies its tone defaults through Customize.
               These actions leave timing, voice, earcon, tone
               enablement and shared volume unchanged.  ‘R’ resets only
               the draft to factory defaults for preview and deliberate
               application.  ‘q’ returns to Spelling feedback and
               discards unapplied edits; applied and saved changes
               remain.  Reopening starts from the current tone.  To
               reverse an applied adjustment, edit it back and apply
               again, saving too if you changed the saved defaults.

               Apply and Save store the active tone shape.  Unused Pulse
               width, End pitch and ADSR values remain available while
               editing this draft, but reopening reads the applied
               shape; it need not recover those inactive values.  A
               named preset stores the complete draft for later reuse.

               The keys have different jobs in the two menus: capital
               ‘S’ stops playback in the tone editor, but saves settings
               in the parent Spelling feedback menu.  Capital ‘W’ in the
               editor saves only tone defaults.  To keep a choice such
               as voice off and tone on, return with ‘q’ and save with
               ‘S’ in Spelling feedback.  That parent Save also includes
               timing, earcon selection and shared tone volume.  Saving
               a preset with ‘N’ stores neither those choices nor the
               live cue defaults.

            6. When a tone cannot be played

               Individual volume below 100, harmonic waveforms and ADSR
               require an Omnivox worker with richer-tone support.
               Pulse, Sawtooth, sweeps and exponential curves require
               extended-tone support.  Older workers omit an unsupported
               tone while retaining speech and earcons, and the feedback
               explanation records the omission.  A preview reports
               missing support instead of playing an inaccurate
               substitute.  Your settings remain intact.  Use an updated
               worker, or restore volume 100, sine and Classic and turn
               off Pitch sweep to hear the tone on an older worker.

  5. Choose typing and point feedback

     ‘C-e H s t’ opens _Typing and interaction_.  Character, word,
     completed-line echo, point indication/presentation, and spoken
     messages share buffer/global scope; global changes also affect the
     source buffer.  Delayed phonetics and delay are global.
     Shift-left/right changes delay by 0.1 seconds; exact entry through
     ‘<RET>’ accepts at least 0.1 seconds.

     ‘<SPC>’ previews the captured line with point settings, ‘P’ speaks
     its character's phonetic name immediately, and ‘s’ stops.  ‘S’
     saves displayed settings through Customize, promoting buffer
     choices to startup defaults.  Closing retains live changes without
     saving.
